export const FINISHED_LESSON_MD: Record<string, string> = {"s1-u1-l01":"# \"I Was, You Were\" — the Past of *to be*\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 1 · **Lesson 1**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: Stage 0 (present *to be*)\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit1-was-and-were.md` · **S1-U1-L01***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 6 · Your Turn 3 · Feedback 1.\n> **Objectives:** use *was/were* to say where someone was · use past time expressions · produce 3–4 true past sentences.\n> **First lesson of a new stage — name the level-up out loud.** They arrive from Stage 0 able to talk about *now*; they leave this unit able to talk about *before*. That is the single biggest jump in A2.\n> **Watch for:** the classic agreement error *\"we was\"*. Fix it gently today; it recurs all unit.\n> **Support:** hand them the conjugation card + one time word. **Stretch:** add a feeling and a reason — *\"I was tired because I worked a lot.\"*\n> **Arabic ~40%** — noticeably less than Stage 0. Explain in English first, then gloss the tricky part in Arabic.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Use **was** and **were** to say where you and other people were.\n- Use past time words: *yesterday · last night · last week · two days ago*.\n- Say **3–4 true sentences** about your own past. 🎙️\n\n> من النهاردة بتتكلم عن **اللي فات**، مش بس عن دلوقتي.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nAlmost every real conversation includes the past. *\"How was your day?\"* · *\"Where were you?\"* ·\n*\"I was at work.\"* Without the past tense you can only describe this exact moment — and that is not how\npeople talk.\n\n> ده أكبر قفزة في المستوى الجديد. الفرق بين إنك تقول \"أنا في الشغل\" وإنك تحكي يومك كله.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom Stage 0, say these out loud in the **present**:\n- *I am at home.* · *She is a teacher.* · *They are busy.*\n\nNow hold that thought — today we move all three into the past.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Fast retrieval of present *to be* from Stage 0. Do not teach yet; you are activating the\n> pattern they already own so the past forms land as a change, not as new material.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nListen to three people answer the same question (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **Q:** Where were you yesterday?\n> **A1:** I **was** at work all day.\n> **A2:** I **was** at my sister's house in Alexandria.\n> **A3:** We **were** at home. It was a quiet day.\n\n**Notice:** every answer uses **was** or **were** — never *am* or *is*.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| Where were you yesterday? | كنت فين امبارح؟ |\n| I was at work. | كنت في الشغل |\n| We were at home. | كنا في البيت |\n| It was a quiet day. | كان يوم هادي |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. First for meaning, second with learners underlining every *was/were*. Ask what\n> changed from the warm-up — let them discover *am/is → was* and *are → were* before you show the table.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\nThe past of *to be* has only **two** forms. That is all.\n\n| Subject | Present | **Past** |\n|---|---|---|\n| I | am | **was** |\n| he / she / it | is | **was** |\n| you / we / they | are | **were** |\n\n> القاعدة كلها في سطر واحد: **am / is** بيبقوا **was**، و**are** بيبقى **were**. مفيش استثناءات.\n\n**Past time expressions** — put them at the start or the end of the sentence:\n\n- **yesterday** · *Yesterday I was at work.*\n- **last** night / week / month / year · *I was in Cairo last week.*\n- **… ago** · *We were there two days ago.*\n- **this morning** · *This morning I was tired.*\n\n> كلمات الزمن دي هي اللي بتوضح إن الكلام عن الماضي. حطها في أول الجملة أو في آخرها — الاتنين صح. ومن\n> غيرها السامع مش هيعرف إنت بتتكلم عن امتى.\n\n> ⚠️ **خلي بالك:** بنقول *we **were***، مش *we was*. الغلطة دي أشهر غلطة في المستوى ده.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Teach the two-form table in English — it is genuinely simple and they can handle it at A2.\n> Reserve Arabic for the *we was* warning, which is the error that actually costs them marks.\n> **Watch for:** learners over-applying *was* to plurals. Drill *we/they were* three extra times.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| was / were | كان / كانوا | *I was busy. They were late.* |\n| yesterday | امبارح | *Yesterday I was at home.* |\n| last night / week | امبارح بالليل / الأسبوع اللي فات | *She was here last night.* |\n| … ago | فات / من | *two days ago* |\n| tired / busy / happy | تعبان / مشغول / مبسوط | *I was tired.* |\n| at work / at home | في الشغل / في البيت | *He was at work.* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Target 1 — the /w/ in *was* and *were*.**\nRound your lips before the sound. Arabic speakers often soften it toward a /v/.\n\n- **Feel it:** *was · was · was* — lips forward, then relax.\n- **Compare:** *was ✅ → vas ❌*\n\n**Target 2 — the weak *was* /wəz/.**\nIn a real sentence, *was* is almost never stressed. The **adjective** carries the stress:\n\n> I was **tired**. · She was **late**. · They were **happy**.\n\nSay it fast and let *was* shrink. That single habit makes you sound far more natural.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"Yesterday I was tired, but today I'm fine.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Drill /w/ with rounded lips, then contrast a stressed *was* (unnatural) against the weak\n> /wəz/ (natural). Praise any shrinking of the vowel — that is the A2 rhythm win.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — was or were?**\n1. I ______ at the office yesterday.\n2. They ______ very tired last night.\n3. She ______ my English teacher last year.\n4. We ______ at my mother's house.\n5. It ______ a long day.\n\n**Exercise 2 — add a time expression.** Rewrite each sentence with a past time word.\n- *I am at home.* → __________________________\n- *They are busy.* → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 3 — true for you.** Complete honestly.\n- Yesterday I was ______________.\n- Last weekend I was ______________.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** **Ex1:** was · were · was · were · was. **Ex2:** accept any correct past form plus a time\n> expression. **Ex3:** circulate and check *was/were* choice, not content.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nSay **three true sentences** about where you were: yesterday, last night, and last weekend.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record them.** Keep the recording — you will compare it at the end of the unit.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~3 min.** **Success check:** correct *was/were* with a past time expression, intelligible. Note each\n> learner's weaker target (/w/ or the weak form) for the Unit-1 clinic in L05.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post a voice note: *\"Yesterday I was ___. Last weekend I was ___.\"*\n- **Lock your daily 20-minute slot again** — a new stage is where people quietly stop.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- **am / is → was** · **are → were**. Two forms, no exceptions.\n- Never *we was* — always **we were**.\n- *was* is weak in a sentence; the adjective takes the stress.\n- *(was/were returns in every warm-up this unit.)*\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 1) was · 2) were · 3) was · 4) were · 5) was\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — Citizen rank. بقيت تتكلم عن اللي فات*\n","s1-u1-l02":"# Negatives & Questions: *wasn't · weren't · Were you…?*\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 1 · **Lesson 2**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L01\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit1-was-and-were.md` · **S1-U1-L02***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 6 · Your Turn 3 · Feedback 1.\n> **Objectives:** make past *to be* negative · ask past questions with inversion · give short answers · hold a short past Q&A.\n> **Watch for:** learners keeping statement word order — *\"You were at home?\"* with rising tone only. Accept it as a stepping stone, then model the inversion.\n> **Support:** give the short-answer cards to read from. **Stretch:** add a follow-up — *\"Why? Where were you?\"*\n> **Arabic ~40%.** The form is simple; spend the time on the Q&A drill, not on explanation.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Say what **wasn't** true: *I wasn't at home.*\n- **Ask** past questions: *Were you at work?*\n- Answer briefly and naturally: *Yes, I was. / No, I wasn't.* 🎙️\n\n> هتعرف تنفي، وتسأل، وترد بسرعة — تلات حاجات بتخليك تعمل محادثة حقيقية عن اللي فات.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nA conversation is not one person making statements. It is questions and answers. Once you can **ask**\nabout the past, you can start a conversation instead of only responding to one.\n\n> اللي بيسأل هو اللي بيقود الكلام.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nAnswer out loud, from L01: *Where were you yesterday?*\n\nThen say one sentence with **were**, and one with **was**.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Two or three learners answer aloud. Listen specifically for *we was* and correct it here,\n> quickly, before the new forms build on top of it.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nA short exchange between two friends (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **A:** **Were** you at the party last night?\n> **B:** No, I **wasn't**. I **was** at home with my family.\n> **A:** Oh! **Was** it a long day at work?\n> **B:** Yes, it **was**. I was really tired.\n\n**Notice:** the question puts **were** *before* **you** — that swap is the whole grammar of this lesson.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| Were you at the party? | كنت في الحفلة؟ |\n| No, I wasn't. | لأ، ماكنتش |\n| Was it a long day? | كان يوم طويل؟ |\n| Yes, it was. | أيوه، كان |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second time, learners mark where the verb sits in the question versus the\n> statement. Make the inversion visible before naming it.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**1) Negative — just add *not*, then contract it.**\n\n| Full | Contracted |\n|---|---|\n| I was **not** at home | I **wasn't** at home |\n| They were **not** ready | They **weren't** ready |\n\n> في الكلام العادي بنستخدم **wasn't** و**weren't** دايمًا. الصيغة الكاملة تستخدمها بس لو عايز تأكيد قوي.\n\n**2) Question — swap the subject and the verb.**\n\n| Statement | Question |\n|---|---|\n| You **were** at work | **Were** you at work? |\n| It **was** cold | **Was** it cold? |\n\n> ⚠️ **القاعدة:** الفعل الأول، بعدين الفاعل. مش بنستخدم *do* ولا *did* مع الفعل ده خالص.\n\n**3) Short answers — never a bare *yes* or *no*.**\n\n- *Were you tired?* → **Yes, I was.** / **No, I wasn't.**\n- *Was she there?* → **Yes, she was.** / **No, she wasn't.**\n\n> بنكرر الفاعل والفعل في الرد. *\"Yes\"* لوحدها بتبان جافة شوية، والرد الكامل بيبان مؤدب أكتر.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Three short blocks, each with an immediate oral drill. **Watch for:** learners answering\n> *\"Yes, I were\"* — the short answer must agree with the subject, same as the statement.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| wasn't / weren't | ماكانش / ماكانوش | *I wasn't ready.* |\n| Were you…? | كنت…؟ | *Were you at home?* |\n| Was he / she / it…? | كان…؟ | *Was it good?* |\n| Yes, I was. / No, I wasn't. | أيوه كنت / لأ ماكنتش | short answers |\n| really | فعلاً / قوي | *I was really tired.* |\n| ready / late / cold | جاهز / متأخر / برد | *We weren't late.* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Target 1 — the final /t/ in *wasn't* and *weren't*.**\nDo not swallow it. The /t/ is what tells the listener the sentence is **negative** — drop it and you have\nsaid the opposite of what you meant.\n\n- *was ↔ **wasn't*** · *were ↔ **weren't***\n- Say it slowly, then at speed, keeping the /t/ audible.\n\n**Target 2 — rising intonation on yes/no questions.**\n\n> Were you at work? ↗ · Was it cold? ↗\n\nThe voice goes **up** at the end. A flat tone sounds like a statement, and your listener will not know\nyou asked anything.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"Were you at home last night? No, I wasn't.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** The final /t/ is the priority — a dropped /t/ inverts the meaning. Then drill the rising\n> tone in pairs, exaggerated first, then natural.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — make it negative.**\n1. I was at the meeting. → __________________________\n2. They were happy. → __________________________\n3. It was expensive. → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 2 — make it a question.**\n1. You were at university. → __________________________\n2. She was your teacher. → __________________________\n3. They were at home. → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 3 — short answers.**\n1. *Were you busy yesterday?* → Yes, ____________ / No, ____________\n2. *Was the food good?* → Yes, ____________ / No, ____________\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** **Ex1:** wasn't at the meeting · weren't happy · wasn't expensive.\n> **Ex2:** Were you at university? · Was she your teacher? · Were they at home?\n> **Ex3:** Yes, I was / No, I wasn't · Yes, it was / No, it wasn't.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nWith a partner, run a **mini-interview**. Ask three past questions and answer theirs.\n\nUseful openers: *Were you…? · Was it…? · Where were you…?*\n\n> 🎙️ **Record one exchange** — your question and their answer.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~3 min.** **Success check:** one correct question, one correct short answer, one negative, all\n> intelligible. Pair a confident learner with a hesitant one.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Ask a classmate a **Was/Were** question in the community channel, and answer someone else's.\n- Say your three sentences from L01 again, but make one of them **negative**.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- **wasn't · weren't** — and the final **/t/** is what makes it negative.\n- Questions **swap** the verb and the subject. No *do*, no *did*.\n- Short answers repeat the subject and verb: **Yes, I was.**\n- *(These forms come back in Unit 3 with did/didn't.)*\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 1) I wasn't at the meeting · 2) They weren't happy · 3) It wasn't expensive\n- **Ex2:** 1) Were you at university? · 2) Was she your teacher? · 3) Were they at home?\n- **Ex3:** 1) Yes, I was / No, I wasn't · 2) Yes, it was / No, it wasn't\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — اللي بيسأل بيقود المحادثة*\n","s1-u1-l03":"# *There Was / There Were* — Describing a Past Place\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 1 · **Lesson 3**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L02\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit1-was-and-were.md` · **S1-U1-L03***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 6 · Your Turn 4 · Feedback 1.\n> **Objectives:** describe what existed in a past place with *there was/were* · use its negative and question forms · describe a real place from their own past.\n> **Watch for:** plural agreement — *\"there was two windows\"* is the error to hunt. Drill with obviously plural nouns.\n> **Support:** give a sentence frame plus a word bank. **Stretch:** add a feeling — *\"It was small but I was happy there.\"*\n> **Arabic ~40%.** This lesson is emotionally warm — people enjoy describing a childhood place. Use that.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Say what existed somewhere in the past: *There was a small bed.*\n- Use the plural: *There were two windows.*\n- Describe **a real place from your own past** in 3–4 sentences. 🎙️\n\n> هتوصف مكان من ماضيك — بيتك القديم، مدرستك، أو مكان زرته.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nThis is how people tell stories. *\"There was a small café near our house…\"* Describing a place is the\nframe that memories hang on, and it is one of the most common things you will ever be asked to do in\nEnglish.\n\n> لما تحكي عن ذكرى، أول حاجة بتوصفها هي المكان.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom L01 and L02, say one sentence with **was**, one with **weren't**, and one **question**.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Rapid chain round the group. Keep it to one sentence each so the pace stays high.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nSomeone describes the room they grew up in (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **There was** a small bed near the window. **There were** two old chairs and a wooden desk.\n> **There wasn't** a television — we watched TV in the living room with everyone.\n> It **was** a simple room, but I **was** happy there.\n\n**Notice:** *there was* for **one** thing, *there were* for **more than one**.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| There was a small bed. | كان فيه سرير صغير |\n| There were two chairs. | كان فيه كرسيين |\n| There wasn't a television. | ماكانش فيه تليفزيون |\n| It was simple. | كان بسيط |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice; second time learners count how many things were singular and how many plural.\n> That counting is what makes the agreement rule stick.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**The rule is only about the noun that follows.**\n\n| | Use | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **one** thing | **there was** | *There was a chair.* |\n| **more than one** | **there were** | *There were three chairs.* |\n\n> القاعدة بتبص على الاسم اللي بعدها: مفرد يبقى **there was**، جمع يبقى **there were**. مالهاش دعوة بالفاعل.\n\n**Negative and question — same pattern as L02:**\n\n- *There **wasn't** a lift in the building.*\n- *There **weren't** any shops nearby.*\n- *__Was__ there a garden?* → **Yes, there was.**\n- *__Were__ there many people?* → **No, there weren't.**\n\n**Quantity words that pair with it:** *a lot of · some · any · many · two, three…*\n\n> في العربي بنقول \"كان فيه\" مع المفرد والجمع، فمفيش حاجة في لغتك بتنبهك للفرق ده. عشان كده الغلطة دي\n> بتتكرر كتير مع المتعلمين العرب. الحل إنك تبص على الاسم اللي بعد الفعل قبل ما تتكلم: لو أكتر من\n> واحد، لازم **there were**.\n\n> ⚠️ **أشهر غلطة:** *\"there was two windows\"*. لو الاسم جمع، لازم **there were**. اسمع نفسك وانت بتتكلم.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Teach the table in English, then use Arabic only for the agreement warning. Drill five quick\n> prompts with obviously plural nouns (*windows, chairs, people, books, cars*) — the error is a habit, not\n> a misunderstanding, so it needs repetitions rather than explanation.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| There was / There were | كان فيه | *There was a garden.* |\n| There wasn't / weren't | ماكانش فيه | *There weren't any shops.* |\n| a lot of / some / any | كتير / شوية / أي | *There were a lot of people.* |\n| near / next to | جنب / قريب من | *There was a café near us.* |\n| old / small / simple | قديم / صغير / بسيط | *It was a simple room.* |\n| garden / building / shop | جنينة / عمارة / دكان | *There was a garden.* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Target 1 — linking *there was* into one smooth sound.**\nNative speakers do not say three separate words. They say something close to **\"there-wuz-uh\"**:\n\n> *There was a…* → **there-wuz-a** · *There were a lot of…* → **there-wer-a-lot-of**\n\n**Target 2 — keep the singular and plural clearly different.**\nBecause *was* and *were* both shrink in fast speech, learners often blur them into the same sound. Keep\nthe vowel distinct: *was* /wəz/ against *were* /wər/.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"There was a bed, and there were two chairs.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Drill the linking first, then immediately contrast the two forms in one sentence so the\n> distinction survives the linking.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — was or were?**\n1. There ______ a big tree in front of the house.\n2. There ______ four people in the car.\n3. There ______ a lot of noise.\n4. There ______ two schools in our street.\n\n**Exercise 2 — make it negative.**\n1. There was a lift. → __________________________\n2. There were any shops. → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 3 — ask the question.**\n1. (a garden) → __________________________\n2. (many people) → __________________________\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** **Ex1:** was · were · was · were. **Ex2:** There wasn't a lift · There weren't any shops.\n> **Ex3:** Was there a garden? · Were there many people? Check agreement on every single answer.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nDescribe **a real place from your past** — your old home, your school, a place you visited. Use at least\n**three** sentences with *there was* or *there were*, and finish with how you felt about it.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** **Success check:** three sentences with correct *there was/were* agreement, intelligible.\n> This one often produces genuinely warm recordings — worth keeping with consent.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post three sentences about a place you remember: *\"There was… / There were…\"*\n- Put **one English cue** somewhere in your room — a sticky note, a label, anything.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- Look at the **noun**: singular → **there was**, plural → **there were**.\n- *There wasn't · There weren't · Was there…? · Were there…?*\n- Link it: **there-wuz-a**.\n- *(there was/were comes back in Unit 8 for travel, and in Unit 10.)*\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 1) was · 2) were · 3) was · 4) were\n- **Ex2:** 1) There wasn't a lift · 2) There weren't any shops\n- **Ex3:** 1) Was there a garden? · 2) Were there many people?\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — الذكريات بتبدأ بوصف المكان*\n","s1-u1-l04":"# *How Was It?* — Talking About a Past Experience\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 1 · **Lesson 4**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L03\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit1-was-and-were.md` · **S1-U1-L04***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 5 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 7 · Your Turn 4 · Feedback 1.\n> **Objectives:** ask and answer *How was…?* · describe an experience with opinion adjectives · react naturally.\n> **Push for *because* + a reason here** — it is the bridge from single sentences to connected A2 speech, and it is the one thing that most raises their fluency score.\n> **Support:** one adjective plus one reaction is enough. **Stretch:** add *because* and a follow-up question.\n> **Arabic ~35%** — down a notch. Reactions must be learned as sound, not translation.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Ask **\"How was your weekend?\"** and answer it properly.\n- Describe an experience with opinion words: *great · boring · tiring · delicious*.\n- **React** like a real person: *Really? · That's great! · That's a pity.* 🎙️\n\n> هتتعلم تسأل عن تجربة، وترد بإحساسك، وتتفاعل — مش بس تقول \"fine\".\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\n*\"How was your weekend?\"* is probably the most common question in everyday English. Answering it with one\nword ends the conversation. Answering it with an opinion and a reason **continues** it.\n\n> الرد بكلمة واحدة بيقفل الكلام. الرد بإحساس وسبب بيفتحه.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom L03, describe one place in two sentences using *there was* and *there were*.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Quick pair round. Listen for lingering plural-agreement slips and correct in passing.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nTwo colleagues on a Sunday morning (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **A:** Hi! **How was** your weekend?\n> **B:** It **was** great, thanks! I **was** with my family in Alexandria.\n> **A:** **Really?** That's nice. **How was** the weather?\n> **B:** It **was** hot, but the sea **was** beautiful. **How was** yours?\n> **A:** Not bad. It **was** a bit boring, actually — I **was** at home all weekend.\n> **B:** Oh, **that's a pity**.\n\n**Notice:** every answer has an **opinion word**, and the other person always **reacts** before asking again.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| How was your weekend? | إيه أخبار الويكند؟ |\n| It was great, thanks. | كان جميل، شكرًا |\n| Really? That's nice. | جدًا؟ حلو |\n| It was a bit boring. | كان ممل شوية |\n| That's a pity. | يا خسارة |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second time, learners mark the three reactions. Point out that B answers **and\n> asks back** — that reciprocity is what makes it a conversation.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**1) The question is just *was/were* plus *how*.**\n\n- **How was** your weekend / your day / the food / the trip?\n- **How were** the people / your exams?\n\n> نفس الفعل اللي اتعلمته، بس بنحط **how** قبله.\n\n**2) The answer is *was/were* plus an opinion word.**\n\n| Positive | Negative | Neutral |\n|---|---|---|\n| great · fun · delicious · beautiful | boring · tiring · difficult · terrible | okay · not bad · quiet |\n\n> *It was **tiring**.* · *The food was **delicious**.* · *It was **not bad**.*\n\n**3) Soften or strengthen it.**\n\n- **a bit** + negative word — *It was **a bit** boring.* (politer)\n- **really** + any word — *It was **really** good.*\n\n**4) Add *because* + a reason.** This is the step that makes you sound A2 rather than A1:\n\n> *It was tiring **because** I worked on Saturday.*\n> *It was great **because** I was with my family.*\n\n> الفرق بين المستوى الأول والتاني هو السبب. لما تقول إحساسك وبعديها تقول ليه، بتبقى بتتكلم كلام\n> متصل، مش جمل مقطعة. وده بالظبط اللي بيرفع تقييمك في الطلاقة. حتى لو السبب بسيط جدًا، قوله —\n> المهم إنك تربط الجملتين بكلمة واحدة.\n\n**5) React before you reply.**\n\n| They say | You react |\n|---|---|\n| something good | **Really? · That's great! · That's nice.** |\n| something bad | **Oh, that's a pity. · I'm sorry to hear that.** |\n\n> التفاعل مهم زي الرد نفسه. لو سكتّ، الكلام بيقف عندك.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** Move fast — the grammar is already known. The new content is *because*, the softener *a bit*,\n> and the reactions. Drill the reactions chorally so they become automatic sounds.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| How was…? | كان عامل إيه…؟ | *How was your day?* |\n| great / fun | جميل / ممتع | *It was great!* |\n| boring / tiring | ممل / متعب | *It was tiring.* |\n| delicious | لذيذ | *The food was delicious.* |\n| a bit / really | شوية / قوي | *a bit boring* |\n| because | لأن | *because I was busy* |\n| That's a pity. | يا خسارة | reaction |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Target 1 — stress the opinion word, hard.**\nThe feeling lives in the adjective, so that is where your voice rises and lengthens:\n\n> It was **GREAT**! · It was a bit **BOR**ing. · The food was **deLIcious**.\n\nSay the sentence flat and it sounds insincere — as if you did not mean it.\n\n**Target 2 — the reaction *\"Really?\"***\nStress the **first** syllable and let the tone rise: **REAL**ly? ↗\n\nFlat and it sounds sarcastic. Rising and warm, it sounds interested.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"It was great, because I was with my family.\"* — then *\"Really? That's nice!\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Have them say *\"It was great\"* twice — once flat, once stressed — and ask which one they\n> would believe. Then drill *\"Really?\"* until it sounds warm rather than flat.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — choose the opinion word.**\n1. I worked twelve hours. It was ______________. (tiring / delicious)\n2. The restaurant was excellent. The food was ______________. (boring / delicious)\n3. I was at home alone all day. It was a bit ______________. (fun / boring)\n\n**Exercise 2 — add *because* and a reason.**\n1. My weekend was great ______________________________.\n2. Monday was difficult ______________________________.\n\n**Exercise 3 — react.**\n1. *\"My weekend was wonderful!\"* → ______________________\n2. *\"I was sick last week.\"* → ______________________\n\n**Exercise 4 — full exchange.** Write your own three-line dialogue: question, answer with a reason,\nreaction.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~7 min.** **Ex1:** tiring · delicious · boring. **Ex2:** accept any sensible reason with correct\n> *was/were*. **Ex3:** *Really? That's great!* · *Oh, that's a pity. / I'm sorry to hear that.*\n> **Ex4:** check the reaction is present — learners skip it first time.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nTalk for about **one minute** about a recent experience — a weekend, a trip, a day at work. Use *was/were*,\nan opinion word, and at least one **because**.\n\nThen ask a partner *\"How was your…?\"* and **react** to their answer.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record your minute.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** **Success check:** a coherent mini-answer with an opinion and a reason, plus one natural\n> reaction. The reason is the thing to insist on — accept imperfect grammar around it.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post: *\"My weekend was ___ because ___.\"*\n- **React to one classmate's post** — a real reaction, not just an emoji.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- **How was…?** plus an **opinion word**.\n- **because** + a reason is what turns a sentence into a conversation.\n- Soften with **a bit**, strengthen with **really**.\n- Always **react** before you reply.\n- *(Opinion words and reactions come back in Unit 5 and Unit 9.)*\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 1) tiring · 2) delicious · 3) boring\n- **Ex3:** 1) *Really? That's great!* · 2) *Oh, that's a pity.*\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — السبب هو اللي بيفتح الكلام*\n","s1-u1-l05":"# Unit Speaking Task: \"Where Were You Last Weekend?\" (+ Review)\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 1 · **Lesson 5** — *UNIT MILESTONE*\n⏱️ ~35 min · 📹 Recorded + live (assessment-style) · Prerequisite: L01–L04\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit1-was-and-were.md` · **S1-U1-L05***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~35 min):** Warm-up 5 · Model 4 · Checklist 4 · Accent clinic 4 · Rehearse 7 · Record 4 · Feedback 2 · Assign 1.\n> **Objectives:** give a connected 5–7 sentence past account · ask a partner about their weekend and react · record the Unit-1 milestone.\n> **This is the first \"I can talk about my past in English\" moment.** Make it feel big — it is a visible level-up from Stage 0 and it pairs well as a mid-journey proof clip.\n> **Support:** five sentences with the card in hand. **Stretch:** add a *because* reason and a question back.\n> **Arabic ~30%** — fading. Instructions and reassurance only.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك (milestone)\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Give a connected account of your weekend in **5–7 sentences**.\n- Combine everything from this unit: *was/were* · *there was/were* · time words · feelings.\n- Ask someone about their weekend and **react**.\n- **Record your Unit-1 past account.** 🎙️\n\n> النهاردة تجمع كل حاجة اتعلمتها في الوحدة في حكاية واحدة متصلة.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن (سلسلة)\nRapid chain — one each, no pausing: *was/were* → a question → *there was/were* → *How was it?*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** Fast retrieval round the whole group. This is the testing effect doing the consolidation for\n> you, so keep the pace uncomfortable rather than comfortable.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — النموذج\nA clean model account (📹 in the portal).\n\n> Last weekend **was** really good. On Friday I **was** at my parents' house in Cairo. **There were** a\n> lot of people — all my cousins **were** there. My mother cooked, and the food **was** delicious.\n> On Saturday I **was** tired, so I **was** at home all day. It **was** a quiet weekend, but I **was**\n> happy.\n\n**Seven sentences.** Every one uses *was*, *were*, or *there were*.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Play once for enjoyment, once for structure. Ask them to count the sentences — seven feels\n> achievable, which matters more than the grammar right now.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 The Checklist — تشيك ليست الحكي الكويس\n1. ✅ **When** — *Last weekend… / On Friday…*\n2. ✅ **Where you were** — *I was at…*\n3. ✅ **Who with** — *I was with… / …were there*\n4. ✅ **What was there** — *There was… / There were…*\n5. ✅ **How it was** — *It was… because…*\n6. ⭐ **A question back** — *How was your weekend?*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Break the model into these six boxes on the board, then hand out the card. Learners who have\n> the card in front of them produce noticeably longer accounts.\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — عيادة النطق\nDrill your **one weakest** sound from this unit:\n\n- **/w/** in *was · were* — lips rounded, no /v/.\n- **Weak *was*** /wəz/ — let it shrink so the adjective can carry the stress.\n- **Final /t/** in *wasn't · weren't* — it is what makes the sentence negative.\n- **Opinion stress** — *It was **GREAT**.*\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare** just your weakest one, three times.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Personalised, using the flag you noted in L01–L04. One sound each, not four.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — التسجيل (milestone)\nRehearse with the checklist and a partner, then record.\n\nGive your **weekend account in 5–7 sentences** — when, where, who with, what was there, how it was — and\nfinish by asking your partner about theirs.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it and keep it.** 👑\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min recording + 2 feedback.** Rehearse in pairs first; the second attempt is always markedly better,\n> so record that one.\n> **Success check (Unit-1):** connected 5–7 sentence past account, intelligible, correct *was/were*\n> (rubric: Fluency + Accuracy + Pronunciation at A2). Compare privately against their Stage-0 graduation\n> clip — the jump is usually obvious and worth telling them about.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- **Post your weekend account.**\n- **Complete the Unit-1 quiz.**\n- Listen to your Stage-0 recording, then to today's. Notice the difference.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر (Unit 1 wrap)\n- **am/is → was** · **are → were**. Two forms.\n- **wasn't · weren't** — keep the final /t/.\n- Questions **swap** the verb and subject. Short answers repeat them.\n- **there was** for one thing, **there were** for more.\n- **because** + a reason is what makes it sound A2.\n- *(All of it returns in Unit 2 with regular past verbs.)*\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — Unit 1 mini-quiz\n1. *We ___ at home yesterday.* → **were**\n2. *She ___ at work. (negative)* → **wasn't**\n3. *___ you tired last night?* → **Were**\n4. *There ___ three cars outside.* → **were**\n5. *How ___ your weekend?* → **was**\n6. *It was great ___ I was with my family.* → **because**\n\n---\n\n## 🎉 Unit 1 complete — أول وحدة في المستوى الجديد\nYou can now talk about the past. A month ago you could only describe this moment; now you can tell someone\nabout your weekend, your childhood room, and how you felt about both.\n\n**Next → Unit 2: The Past (2) — Regular Verbs.** The *-ed* ending, and the Accent Lab focus that comes\nwith it. 👑\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — بقيت تحكي ماضيك بالإنجليزي*\n","s1-u10-l01":"# Big Review 1: Past Narration\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 10 · **Lesson 1** — *RETRIEVAL REVIEW*\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: Units 1–3 and 8\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit10-putting-it-together.md` · **S1-U10-L01***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Retrieval 5 · Model 5 · Error check 5 · Accent 5 · Build 6 · Record 3 · Feedback 1.\n> **Objectives:** retrieve *was/were*, regular and irregular pasts, and *did/didn't* · narrate a connected past account · self-correct the three patterned past errors.\n> **Retrieval, not re-teaching.** Let learners produce before you show anything. A five-second struggle to retrieve *took* strengthens memory; supplying it immediately does not.\n> **Correct patterns, not every slip.** Today's three are double-marked past after *did*, missing or wrong *-ed* endings, and irregular substitutions. Note anything else for later rather than breaking the story.\n> **The output must be connected.** Eight accurate but unrelated past sentences do not meet the goal. Sequence and consequence — *first, then, because, so, in the end* — make narration.\n> **Support:** retrieval deck + six-slot skeleton. **Stretch:** include direct speech, a feeling, and answer a follow-up without preparation.\n> **Arabic ~20%** — reassurance and the error contrast only.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Retrieve the complete Stage-1 past system without notes.\n- Tell a connected event in **6–8 sentences**.\n- Hear and fix your own top three past-tense errors. 🎙️\n\n> ده مش شرح جديد. النهاردة بتثبت إن الماضي بقى أداة عندك، مش قاعدة بتفتكرها.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nAt Stage 0, you could name your routine. Now you can take a listener backwards through time: set the scene,\nmove events in order, explain what did not happen, and say how it ended. That is a different level of control.\n\n> الاختبار إن الماضي يشتغل جوه حكاية من غير ما تقف.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن (retrieval sprint)\nWithout opening a lesson, write the past of these verbs in 60 seconds:\n\n> **go · take · see · come · buy · eat · have · make · get · leave**\n\nThen sort five regular verbs by ending: **worked · played · wanted · watched · called**.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** Answers: *went, took, saw, came, bought, ate, had, made, got, left*. Endings: /t/ *worked,\n> watched* · /d/ *played, called* · /ɪd/ *wanted*. Learners answer before any model appears.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — النموذج\nA connected past account (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **Last Thursday**, I **left** work late because we **had** a problem with a customer. I **didn't have**\n> time to eat, so I **bought** a sandwich at the station. **While I was waiting**, I **saw** an old school\n> friend. We **took** the same train and **talked** all the way home. She **told** me she **was** starting a\n> new job. **In the end**, the late train **wasn't** a disaster — it **gave** me someone back.\n\nThis is not a list. **Because** explains, **so** shows the result, **while** connects two past scenes, and\n**in the end** closes the account.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** First listen for the event. Second listen: learners mark irregulars, regulars, a negative, and\n> four connectors. Ask what makes the final sentence feel like an ending.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 The Past Error Card — كارت أخطاء الماضي\n\n**Error 1 — marking the past twice.**\n\n> ✅ *I **didn't go**.* · *Where **did** you **go**?*\n> ❌ *I didn't went.* · *Where did you went?*\n\nAfter **did / didn't**, the main verb returns to its base. الماضي بيتحط مرة واحدة بس.\n\n**Error 2 — using a regular ending on an irregular verb.**\n\n> ✅ *went · took · saw · bought* · ❌ *goed · taked · seed · buyed*\n\n**Error 3 — losing *-ed* when the story speeds up.**\n\n> *worked* /t/ · *played* /d/ · *wanted* /ɪd/\n\nThe ending may be small, but it tells the listener **when** the event happened.\n\n**Six slots build the narration:**\n\n| Slot | Prompt |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | When and where? |\n| 2 | What was the situation? — *was/were* |\n| 3 | What happened first? |\n| 4 | What happened next, and why? |\n| 5 | What didn't happen? |\n| 6 | How did it end or feel? |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** Learners self-correct the three wrong examples before you explain. Keep the six-slot skeleton\n> visible for Train, then remove the error card during the final recording.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك (retrieval)\n\n| Job | Language |\n|---|---|\n| set the scene | *Last… · Two years ago… · When I was…* |\n| describe it | *There was/were… · It was…* |\n| move forward | *first · then · after that · while* |\n| explain | *because · so* |\n| negative / question | *didn't + base · Did… + base?* |\n| close | *in the end · finally · the best part was…* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق (past endings)\n\nSort by the **sound**, not the spelling:\n\n| /t/ | /d/ | /ɪd/ |\n|---|---|---|\n| worked · watched · stopped | played · called · lived | wanted · needed · visited |\n\n**Rule you can feel:**\n- /ɪd/ only after a final /t/ or /d/ — it creates an extra syllable.\n- /t/ after a voiceless sound.\n- /d/ after a voiced sound.\n\nSpeed round: teacher says the base; learner says the past and its sound. Then put three into one connected\nsentence without losing the endings.\n\n> النطق مش إضافة تجميلية هنا. لو النهاية اختفت، المستمع ممكن يسمع الحكاية كأنها بتحصل دلوقتي.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"I worked late, called my friend, and waited at the station.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** Do not ask learners to recite the rule first. Make them sort and say. Include *wanted* so they\n> preserve the extra syllable and *worked* so they do not invent one.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — البروفة الكاملة\nUse a picture sequence or a real prompt. Build one story through the six slots with a partner.\n\n**Requirements:**\n- one *was/were* scene\n- two irregular past verbs\n- two audible regular endings\n- one *didn't + base*\n- three connectors\n\nPartner listens with the error card and identifies **one pattern**, not every slip. Tell the story again with\nthe correction.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** First round with the skeleton, second without it. If feedback becomes a list, stop it and ask\n> for the one error that repeated. Patterned correction transfers; scattered correction does not.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nNarrate a real event from the last month in **6–8 connected sentences**. Include what happened, one thing that\ndidn't happen, and how it ended.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it, listen once, then correct one sentence if needed.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~3 min + 1 feedback.** **Success check:** connected rather than listed; accurate *did/didn't + base*;\n> intelligible regular endings; at least two correct irregulars; one self-correction after listening.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post the narration and mark the sentence you corrected yourself.\n- Compare it with any Stage-0 recording. Do not ask whether your accent is perfect; ask what you can express\n  now that you could not express then.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- **did / didn't + base verb** — the past appears once.\n- Irregulars must be retrieved: *went · took · saw · bought*.\n- *-ed* has three sounds: /t/, /d/, /ɪd/.\n- A story needs sequence and consequence, not just past verbs.\n- Self-correction is evidence of control, not evidence of failure.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n1. *Where did you went?* → **Where did you go?**\n2. *I didn't saw him.* → **I didn't see him.**\n3. *We buyed two tickets.* → **We bought two tickets.**\n4. *wanted* ends in → **/ɪd/**\n5. A connector that shows result → **so**\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — مش بتراجع قاعدة؛ بتسترجع قدرة*\n","s1-u10-l02":"# Big Review 2: Future, Comparison, Quantity & Advice\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 10 · **Lesson 2** — *RETRIEVAL REVIEW*\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: Units 4–7\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit10-putting-it-together.md` · **S1-U10-L02***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Retrieval 5 · Model 5 · Error check 5 · Accent 4 · Combine 7 · Record 3 · Feedback 1.\n> **Objectives:** retrieve future, comparison, quantity, and advice/obligation · combine at least three areas in one talk · self-correct the top error in each.\n> **Combination is the assessment.** A learner who can answer four isolated grammar questions has recognition. A learner who can compare two plans, quantify the difference, and advise a friend has usable range.\n> **Use error inoculation, not a lecture.** Show the five plausible wrong forms — *I going to, more big, the most biggest, much people, should to* — and make learners repair them from memory.\n> **Preserve meaning distinctions:** *going to* plan / *will* prediction or instant decision; *should* advice / *have to* necessity; *many* count / *much* uncount. These are choices, not merely forms.\n> **Support:** mixed retrieval deck + four-part prompt. **Stretch:** sustain two minutes and include a reasoned recommendation.\n> **Arabic ~20%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Move from now into the future, compare options, talk about quantity, and give advice.\n- Combine **at least three** of those areas naturally.\n- Catch the five most persistent Stage-1 errors. 🎙️\n\n> القوة مش إنك تستخدم قاعدة لوحدها؛ القوة إنك تختار كذا أداة عشان توصل رأي واحد.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nRange is what lets a conversation go somewhere. You can now discuss **when**, **which is better**, **how\nmuch**, and **what someone should do** — four jobs that ordinary decisions require every day.\n\n> المطلوب دمج الأدوات في كلام واحد.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن (six-shot retrieval)\nGive one original example of each, without notes:\n\n1. a plan with *going to*\n2. a prediction with *will*\n3. a comparative\n4. a superlative\n5. a count and an uncount quantifier\n6. advice with *should*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** Accept meaning first, then form. Ask one learner to explain why they chose *going to* rather\n> than *will* — retrieving the distinction is stronger than repeating it.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — النموذج\nChoosing between two weekend plans (📹 in the portal).\n\n> I'm **going to** take a short trip next weekend, but I haven't chosen between Alexandria and Dahab.\n> Alexandria is **closer than** Dahab, and there are **more trains**, so it **will probably** be easier. Dahab\n> is **more expensive**, but it has **less traffic** and the beaches are **much quieter**. I don't have **much\n> time**, so I think Alexandria is **the better** choice this weekend. My friend wants Dahab, but she **has\n> to** work on Sunday. I think she **should** save it for a longer holiday.\n\nOne decision naturally uses all four areas: a plan, predictions, comparisons, quantities, and advice with a\nreason.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** Learners tally examples by area on first listen. On second, ask which sentence actually makes\n> the recommendation convincing — it is the one that connects limited time to the advice.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 The Five-Error Card — كارت الخمس أخطاء\n\nRepair each before reading the correction.\n\n| Wrong | Correct | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| *I going to travel.* | *I**'m** going to travel.* | *going to* needs **be** |\n| *This one is more big.* | *This one is **bigger**.* | short adjective + *-er* |\n| *It's the most biggest.* | *It's **the biggest**.* | one superlative marker |\n| *There are much people.* | *There are **many** people.* | count plural → *many* |\n| *You should to rest.* | *You should **rest**.* | modal + base verb |\n\n> خلي عينك على معنى الاختيار كمان: **should** رأي ونصيحة، لكن **have to** ضرورة. الشكل الصحيح بمعنى غلط لسه\n> غلط.\n\n**The decision map:**\n\n| Job | Choose |\n|---|---|\n| planned before speaking | **be going to** |\n| prediction / instant decision | **will** |\n| compare two / compare with all | **comparative / superlative** |\n| count plural / uncount | **many/few / much/little** |\n| advice / necessity | **should / have to** |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** Learners repair all five aloud, then explain one meaning choice from the map. Do not expand\n> into a full re-teach; any unresolved pattern becomes an individual review target before L05.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك (combine)\n\n| Move | Frame |\n|---|---|\n| state the plan | *I'm going to…* |\n| predict | *I think it will…* |\n| compare | *X is cheaper / more useful than Y.* |\n| add degree | *much better · a little cheaper* |\n| quantify | *many people · much time · a few options · a little money* |\n| recommend | *I think you should… because…* |\n| state reality | *You have to… · You don't have to…* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق (three carry-over targets)\n\n**1) Long-adjective stress stays inside the adjective:**\n\n> ex-**PEN**-sive → more ex-**PEN**-sive · com-**FOR**-ta-ble → more com-**FOR**-ta-ble\n\nDo not stress *more* and flatten the important word.\n\n**2) *than* begins with /ð/** — tongue lightly between the teeth, voice on:\n\n> *cheaper **than** · better **than** · more useful **than***\n\n**3) *have to* changes in connected speech:**\n\n> *have to* → /ˈhæftə/ — “hafta” · *has to* → /ˈhæstə/\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"This plan is more expensive than that one, but we have to choose today.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** One minute each, then the combined sentence. The goal is intelligible connected speech, not\n> a performance of isolated sounds.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — البروفة الكاملة\nYour friend has one free weekend and a limited budget. Compare **two plans** and recommend one.\n\nYour talk must contain:\n- one future plan or prediction\n- one comparative and one superlative\n- one count or uncount quantity\n- one piece of advice with a reason\n- one necessity or lack of necessity\n\nPartner listens for **combination** and checks the five-error card. Swap, then repeat after one correction.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~7 min.** Do not let learners deliver five disconnected requirement sentences. Reward language that\n> connects: *because, so, but*. Ask the partner which structure helped the recommendation rather than merely\n> whether all boxes appeared.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nTalk for about **90 seconds** on one decision you are making. Compare the options, mention a limit, say what\nyou are going to do, and give yourself or someone else advice.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it and self-correct one patterned error.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~3 min + 1 feedback.** **Success check:** at least three areas integrated accurately, one reasoned\n> connection, and one self-correction against the five-error card.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post the mixed talk: plan + comparison + quantity + advice.\n- Circle the structures after recording, not before. If they are present naturally, you combined them; if\n  you inserted them from a checklist, repeat once and make the meaning lead.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- ***be* going to** needs *am/is/are*; **will** does not.\n- Short adjective + **-er**; long adjective uses **more**; never both.\n- **many/few** for count · **much/little** for uncount.\n- **should + base** = advice · **have to + base** = necessity.\n- Range means combining choices to express one idea.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n1. *I going to call him.* → **I'm going to call him.**\n2. *This route is more easy.* → **This route is easier.**\n3. *It's the most cheapest.* → **It's the cheapest.**\n4. *We don't have many time.* → **We don't have much time.**\n5. *She should to wait.* → **She should wait.**\n6. Advice, not obligation → **should**\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — النطاق الحقيقي يظهر لما الأدوات تشتغل مع بعض*\n","s1-u10-l03":"# Accent Clinic: Fix Your Top Three Sounds\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 10 · **Lesson 3**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: all Stage-1 units\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit10-putting-it-together.md` · **S1-U10-L03***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Identify 4 · Perceive 5 · Re-cue 5 · Produce 9 · Passage 4 · Free speech 2 · Compare 1.\n> **Objectives:** identify each learner's top three weak sounds from tracker evidence · improve them from word to connected speech · self-monitor and self-correct.\n> **Personalisation is non-negotiable.** Do not give every learner the same three sounds. Use actual flags from recordings; if no tracker data exists, diagnose with the passage before selecting targets.\n> **One sound improved beats three sounds named.** Support learners may work on one high-impact contrast. Stretch learners carry all three into spontaneous speech and self-correct without a prompt.\n> **Honesty guardrail:** the target is clearer and more intelligible than the learner's own earlier recording — never “native.” Accent identity is not a defect.\n> **The clinic cycle:** perceive → locate → produce alone → word → phrase → sentence → spontaneous speech → compare. Skipping perception or comparison turns practice into repetition without evidence.\n> **Arabic ~20%** — articulation cues may use Arabic; production stays English.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Name your **three** current pronunciation targets from evidence.\n- Use a physical cue to improve each one.\n- Notice a slip and repair it in connected speech.\n- Compare today's recording with your own earlier one. 🎙️\n\n> هدفك مش تختفي لكنتك. هدفك إن المستمع يفهمك بسهولة، وإنك تعرف تصلّح نفسك بعد الكورس.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nA teacher cannot follow you forever. Self-monitoring can. Once you can hear a target, feel its mouth position,\nand repair it inside a sentence, improvement no longer depends on a course timetable.\n\n> المطلوب وضوح أحسن، مش تقليد.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن (evidence first)\nOpen your tracker or the notes from Units 1–9. Write three **specific** targets:\n\n> not “pronunciation” → **final /t/ in can't**\n> not “th” → **voiced /ð/ in than and this**\n> not “endings” → **/ɪd/ in wanted and needed**\n\nRank them by impact: which one most often makes a word unclear or changes meaning?\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** If no reliable flags exist, use the seeded passage in Train as a diagnosis. Do not invent data\n> or copy another learner's list.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع (perception before production)\nFor each selected target, listen to a correct version and a common substitution. Do **not** speak yet. Mark\nwhich one you hear.\n\n| Common target | Contrast |\n|---|---|\n| past *-ed* | *worked* /t/ · *played* /d/ · *wanted* /ɪd/ |\n| /θ/ and /ð/ | *think / sink* · *then / den* |\n| *can / can't* | /kən/ · /kænt/ |\n| /p/ and /b/ | *pack / back* |\n| /v/ and /f/ | *van / fan* |\n| /ŋ/ ending | *sing / sing-guh* |\n| clusters | *street / se-treet* |\n\n**If you cannot hear the contrast reliably, slow down there.** The mouth cannot consistently repair a\ndifference the ear does not yet track.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** Use A/B listening with eyes closed. A target only advances when the learner identifies at least\n> four of five contrasts. Perception failure is information, not failure.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — الطريقة (physical cue cards)\nChoose only the cards that match your targets.\n\n| Target | Physical cue | Quick test |\n|---|---|---|\n| /θ/–/ð/ | tongue tip lightly between teeth; /ð/ adds voice | hand on throat |\n| /p/–/b/ | close both lips; /p/ releases air, /b/ adds voice | tissue moves for /p/ |\n| /v/–/f/ | top teeth touch lower lip; /v/ adds voice | hand on throat |\n| *can/can't* | *can't* has full /æ/ and a closed final /t/ | partner's eyes closed |\n| /ŋ/ | back of tongue closes and stays; no released /g/ | hold the final sound |\n| cluster | build backwards: /t/ → /tr/ → /str/ → *street* | no inserted vowel |\n| *-ed* | /ɪd/ after /t,d/; otherwise voice decides /t/ or /d/ | touch throat before ending |\n\n> وقت الكلام، استخدم إشارة جسدية.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** Re-cue, do not re-lecture. Learner states the cue and demonstrates the test. The cue must be\n> usable without a mirror or teacher after today.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك (the repair language)\n\n| When you notice… | Do this |\n|---|---|\n| the word was unclear | pause, repeat the **whole phrase**, continue |\n| a missing ending | slow the final word, not the whole sentence |\n| a substituted sound | reset the mouth cue, then say the phrase once |\n| uncertainty | record two versions and choose by listening |\n| repeated failure | step back: sentence → phrase → word → sound |\n\nA repair should be small: *“I can go — sorry, I **can't** go — on Tuesday.”* Correct, then continue. Do not\nrestart the entire answer.\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — عيادة النطق (the nine-minute core)\nFor **each** target, run the same ladder:\n\n1. **Hear** the contrast.\n2. **Set** the physical cue.\n3. Say the **sound** alone five times.\n4. Say three **words**.\n5. Say one **phrase**.\n6. Say one **sentence**.\n7. **Record and compare** with the model.\n8. Repeat the sentence once, changing only the target.\n\n**Required *-ed* lane for everyone:**\n\n> /t/: *worked · watched · stopped*\n> /d/: *played · called · lived*\n> /ɪd/: *wanted · needed · visited*\n\nThen personalise lanes two and three from your tracker.\n\n> 🎧 **Checkpoint:** save the first and final attempt. Improvement you cannot compare is only a feeling.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~9 min.** Roughly three minutes per target. Support: six minutes on the highest-impact target, three on\n> *-ed*. Stretch: final sentence carries all three targets and is produced without a written cue.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب (seeded passage)\nRead once for meaning, once for targets:\n\n> **Last Thursday**, three friends **visited** the new theatre. They **walked** there because the train was\n> **faster than** the bus. They **wanted** to leave at ten, but the final act **finished** late. “I **can't**\n> miss the last train,” said Victor, **thinking** quickly. They **thanked** the staff and **ran** to the\n> station. In the end, they **caught** it.\n\nUnderline words containing your targets. Read again, then speak for one minute about a recent evening without\nthe passage. When you notice a slip, repair the phrase and continue.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min passage + 2 min free speech.** The passage seeds *-ed, th, can/can't, /v/, /ŋ/* and clusters.\n> Score only each learner's selected targets. Count spontaneous self-repairs as a success.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nRecord a one-minute account containing all three targets. Listen once and mark:\n\n- one clear gain\n- one remaining slip\n- one successful self-repair\n\n> 🎙️ **Keep both the first ladder recording and this final recording.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **Success check:** clearer production than the learner's own earlier sample, all selected targets attempted\n> in connected speech, and at least one independent notice-or-repair. Update residual flags for Stage 2.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post a before/after pair for your #1 target sentence.\n- Use the **five-minute drill** for seven days: perceive 1 · cue 1 · ladder 2 · spontaneous sentence 1.\n- Carry unresolved targets forward. Completion does not mean pretending a sound is finished.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- Clear and intelligible is the goal; “native” is not.\n- Compare with your own past recording.\n- The ladder: **hear → cue → sound → word → phrase → sentence → speech → compare**.\n- One physical cue survives pressure better than a paragraph of theory.\n- Self-correction keeps improvement alive after the teacher is gone.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- [ ] My targets came from evidence, not a generic list.\n- [ ] I can identify each contrast by ear.\n- [ ] I know one physical cue for each.\n- [ ] I saved a first and final recording.\n- [ ] I repaired at least one slip without restarting.\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — الوضوح مكسب، والمراقبة الذاتية هي اللي تكمّله*\n","s1-u10-l04":"# Practice Conversation: The A2 Exchange\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 10 · **Lesson 4** — *FULL MOCK*\n⏱️ ~35 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L01–L03\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit10-putting-it-together.md` · **S1-U10-L04***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~35 min):** Retrieval 5 · Model 5 · Rubric 4 · Accent 3 · Pair mock 10 · Coach mock 6 · Feedback 2.\n> **Objectives:** sustain a mixed A2 conversation with narration, role-play, and Q&A · move across tenses and functions · self-assess readiness for L05.\n> **Preview the summative exactly.** A mock only reduces anxiety if its order, prompt types, and rubric match the real event. Do not make this one harder to “prepare” them or easier to “encourage” them.\n> **Low stakes, high honesty.** Today produces one short polish list, not a pass/fail label. Name evidence: *\"Your narration connected six events; repair didn't + base before next lesson.\"*\n> **Score the exchange, not a monologue.** Follow-up handling, clarification, and recovery count. A2 does not mean error-free; it means connected and intelligible enough to keep the task moving.\n> **Support:** full prompt, frames, and two minutes' preparation. **Stretch:** prompt only, no frames, spontaneous role-play complication.\n> **Arabic ~15%** — reassurance only; the mock itself is English-first.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nToday you will complete the exact shape of the A2 speaking assessment:\n- **Part 1:** narrate a past event.\n- **Part 2:** handle a real-world role-play.\n- **Part 3:** answer and ask follow-up questions across time.\n- Use the rubric to identify what to polish before L05. 🎙️\n\n> دي بروفة بنفس الشكل الحقيقي. الهدف إنك تدخل التقييم عارف الخطوات، مش حافظ الإجابات.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nUncertainty creates more anxiety than difficulty. Once you have moved through the full format, the summative\nstops being a surprise and becomes one more conversation you know how to handle.\n\n> دي بروفة، مش نجاح أو رسوب.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن (mixed retrieval)\nComplete four 20-second turns:\n\n1. something you did last weekend\n2. a plan for next month\n3. advice for someone who is tired\n4. a comparison between two places you know\n\nYour partner asks one follow-up after each.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** Keep it moving. This warms tense switching and follow-up handling, not topic depth.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — النموذج\nObserve one complete miniature exchange (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **Coach:** Tell me about a journey that did not go as planned.\n> **Learner:** Last month I took a bus to Alexandria. It left late because there was a problem…\n> **Coach:** What did you do while you were waiting?\n> **Learner:** I called my friend and bought some coffee. I didn't get angry because I had plenty of time.\n> **Coach:** Now imagine we are at a ticket office. Buy a return ticket and ask about one rule.\n> **Learner:** Good morning. Could I have a return ticket to Cairo, please? What time does the last train leave?\n> **Coach:** It leaves at nine, but you have to book a seat.\n> **Learner:** That's fine. Do I have to pay now?\n> **Coach:** Last question: are you going to travel more next year?\n> **Learner:** Yes, I'm going to visit Aswan. I think it will be quieter than Cairo, and…\n\nThe learner does not deliver one perfect speech. They understand a prompt, respond, handle new information,\nand keep going.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** Learners identify the three parts and one example of interaction in each. Ask where the coach\n> introduced information the learner could not have memorised.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 The A2 Rubric Card — كارت التقييم\nScore each dimension **0–5** using evidence from the whole exchange.\n\n| Dimension | At-threshold evidence |\n|---|---|\n| **Fluency** | connected short turns; pauses do not repeatedly stop the task |\n| **Accuracy** | Stage-1 forms usually carry the intended time and meaning |\n| **Pronunciation** | generally intelligible; key contrasts do not invert meaning |\n| **Range** | uses past, future, comparison, quantity, and modal/functions as needed |\n| **Interaction** | understands prompts, asks or answers follow-ups, repairs when needed |\n\n**A strong A2 answer is not error-free.** It is connected, understandable, responsive, and broad enough to\ncomplete ordinary tasks.\n\n> ركّز على الدليل، مش الانطباع. بدل \"كويس\" اكتب: استخدم الماضي وربط الأحداث، أو طلب توضيح وكمل.\n\n**Useful recovery lines:**\n\n> *Could you say that again? · Do you mean…? · Let me think for a second. · Sorry, I mean…*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Read the actual rubric bands from the assessment suite when scoring; this card explains the\n> dimensions to learners. Do not invent a new threshold in the room.\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — عيادة النطق (top-one tune-up)\nChoose the **one** target from L03 most likely to affect meaning or intelligibility. Run one quick ladder:\n\n> word → phrase → assessment sentence → one unrehearsed sentence\n\nThen stop. A three-minute tune-up should settle the mouth, not fill the learner's head with monitoring.\n\n> 🎧 Record the assessment sentence once. No repeated perfection loop.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~3 min.** Prioritise meaning-changing targets such as *can/can't* before cosmetic ones. Do not introduce\n> a new sound on mock day.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — البروفة الكاملة (pair mock)\nTake turns as learner and assessor.\n\n### Part 1 — Narration\nTell a connected 6–8 sentence story from one prompt:\n- a journey that went wrong\n- a day you were proud of\n- a time you helped someone\n\nAssessor asks **two** follow-ups.\n\n### Part 2 — Role-play\nChoose one:\n- buy a ticket; one option is sold out\n- describe symptoms at a pharmacy and ask what to do\n- order for two people; one item is unavailable\n- ask directions, then clarify one step\n\nAssessor introduces one complication.\n\n### Part 3 — Q&A\nAnswer across time: one question about your routine, one about a future plan, and one comparison or opinion.\nAsk one question back.\n\nUse the rubric card for **one evidence note + one next action** per partner.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~10 min.** Five minutes each. Keep assessor behaviour consistent: two narration follow-ups, one role-play\n> complication, three mixed questions. Frames are permitted for the support route, not read as a script.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك (coach mock)\nComplete one full mock with the coach, or observe one learner while scoring independently with the rubric.\n\n> 🎙️ **Optional recording for private comparison — this is not the milestone clip.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min + 2 feedback.** Compare observer scores to the coach's evidence. Give a maximum of **two** polish\n> targets: one language/interaction target and one clarity target.\n> **Success check:** sustains all three parts at or near A2 threshold, uses a recovery strategy if needed,\n> and leaves with a specific, manageable L05 plan.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Complete the rubric self-assessment using evidence, not feelings.\n- Prepare topic ideas, **not memorised answers**.\n- Practise only your two polish targets. Last-minute review of the whole stage increases noise and reduces\n  control.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- Format: **narration → role-play → mixed Q&A**.\n- The rubric measures **Fluency · Accuracy · Pronunciation · Range · Interaction**.\n- A2 is connected and intelligible, not error-free.\n- Repair lines are part of interaction, not an escape from it.\n- The summative is the same shape as today; there is no trap waiting.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- [ ] I completed all three parts.\n- [ ] I answered an unprepared follow-up.\n- [ ] I handled a complication in the role-play.\n- [ ] I used or could use a recovery phrase.\n- [ ] My two polish targets are specific and manageable.\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — بروفة صادقة، وتقييم من غير مفاجآت*\n","s1-u10-l05":"# Stage-1 Summative Assessment + A2 Milestone Sample 🎓\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 10 · **Lesson 5** — *STAGE FINALE*\n⏱️ ~40 min · 📹 Live recorded assessment · Prerequisite: L01–L04\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit10-putting-it-together.md` · **S1-U10-L05***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~40 min):** Settle 4 · Quiz 10 · Setup 3 · Accent 2 · A2 exchange 8 · Standard sample 5 · Feedback 3 · Queue 2.\n> **Objectives:** demonstrate the A2 exit criteria · complete the Stage-1 summative quiz and extended speaking task · record the standard-prompt A2 milestone sample for the three-point proof timeline.\n> **Use the governing documents:** `curriculum/05-assessment-suite.md` for rubric bands and determination; `curriculum/06-before-after-protocol.md` for the standard prompt, recording conditions, storage, and consent. This lesson does not invent replacements.\n> **The assessment and proof sample are different recordings.** The extended A2 exchange determines performance; the standard prompt enables fair Day-1 → Stage-0 → A2 comparison. Keep both.\n> **Consent is granular and revocable.** Course participation never implies permission to publish. Record the consent tier; without publication consent, retain or delete according to the protocol and use privately only where authorised.\n> **Clear, not native.** Pronunciation is scored for intelligibility. Accent identity is never a pass criterion.\n> **Arabic ~10%** — reassurance and safety-critical consent only.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك (Stage finale)\nToday you will:\n- complete the **Stage-1 summative quiz**\n- complete the A2 speaking exchange: **narration + role-play + Q&A**\n- record the standard **A2 milestone sample**\n- receive an evidence-based A2 determination and your next targets\n\n> النهاردة مش فخ. دي لحظة إثبات وتوثيق للمسافة اللي قطعتها.\n\n---\n\n## 1️⃣ Settle — اهدا\nTake one breath. You have already completed this format in L04.\n\n> **This is a milestone, not a filter.** A result describes today's evidence and the next useful step. It\ndoes not describe your intelligence, effort, or worth.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Confirm the sequence and answer procedural questions only. Do not coach content. Verify recording\n> works and confirm consent/storage before capture begins.\n\n---\n\n## 2️⃣ The Summative Quiz — الاختبار\nComplete the Stage-1 quiz independently. It samples recognition and short production across:\n\n- *was/were*, regular and irregular past, *did/didn't*\n- *going to / will*\n- comparatives and superlatives\n- count/uncount quantity and restaurant language\n- symptoms, *should*, *have to / don't have to / can't*\n- travel requests and booking\n- feelings, adverbs, and simple-versus-continuous\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~10 min.** Use the committed portal/Google Form instrument and its set band. Preserve raw result and\n> production responses. A quiz command exiting successfully is not evidence of speaking ability; it is one\n> component only.\n\n---\n\n## 3️⃣ Speaking Task Setup — شكل المحادثة\nYou will complete three connected parts:\n\n1. **Narration:** a past event, 6–8 connected sentences, then follow-ups.\n2. **Role-play:** one ordinary A2 situation with an unexpected complication.\n3. **Q&A:** plans, comparison, advice, routine/current change; ask one question back.\n\nThe five dimensions are **Fluency · Accuracy · Pronunciation · Range · Interaction**.\n\n> You may ask for repetition or clarification. That is assessed interaction, not lost marks.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~3 min.** Explain only the format and rubric. Support route may use brief preparation notes while still\n> covering every criterion; record that accommodation with the result.\n\n---\n\n## 4️⃣ Accent Tune-up — تجهيز الصوت\nUse your L03 cue for your single highest-impact target:\n\n> sound → word → one phrase → stop\n\nDo not attempt to monitor every sound during the task. Meaning and interaction come first.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~2 min.** No new correction. Settle one established cue, then remove the practice card.\n\n---\n\n## 5️⃣ The A2 Speaking Exchange — المحادثة\n\n### Part A — Narration\nTell me about **a trip, problem, or important day** from the past. Set the scene, connect events, include one\nnegative, and say how it ended.\n\n### Part B — Role-play\nThe assessor selects one: ticket office · pharmacy · restaurant · directions. Complete the task and respond\nto one complication.\n\n### Part C — Q&A\nDiscuss a future plan, compare two options, give advice with a reason, describe a routine and what is different\nnow, then ask one question back.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~8 min, recorded.** Follow the assessment suite's prompt and scoring procedure. Do not silently simplify\n> after one error or escalate after strong performance. Score the whole sample against the five rubric\n> dimensions and preserve evidence notes.\n\n---\n\n## 6️⃣ The A2 Milestone Sample — تسجيل التطور 🎬\nRecord the standard prompt under the before/after protocol's comparable conditions:\n\n> **“Tell me about yourself, your family, and your typical day.”**\n\nThen add the Stage-1 extension:\n\n> **“Tell me about something you did recently and one plan you have for the future.”**\n\nSpeak naturally. Do not memorise or read a script. This recording is for comparison with your Day-1 and\nStage-0 samples, not for changing the assessment result.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** Capture as a separate file. Match prompt and conditions as closely as the protocol requires.\n> Log file link, date, level point, and consent tier. Never publish, edit into marketing, or transfer beyond\n> that tier without explicit authorisation.\n\n---\n\n## 7️⃣ Result & Feedback — النتيجة\nThe coach gives:\n\n1. the rubric evidence in all five dimensions\n2. the A2 determination using the assessment suite's threshold\n3. two specific wins, quoted from the performance\n4. one language/interaction target for Stage 2\n5. one residual pronunciation target from the tracker\n\n> النتيجة مبنية على دليل النهاردة. ولو في جزء محتاج إعادة، ده مسار عمل واضح، مش حكم عليك.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~3 min.** State evidence before labels. Do not round up for motivation or down for strictness. If evidence\n> is insufficient or a technical failure affected capture, record that and reschedule the affected component.\n\n---\n\n## 8️⃣ Save, Consent & Queue — الحفظ والخطوة الجاية\n\n### Completion checklist\n- [ ] Summative quiz score and responses saved.\n- [ ] Extended A2 exchange recorded and scored on all five dimensions.\n- [ ] Standard milestone prompt recorded separately.\n- [ ] A2 determination and evidence notes entered in the Cohort Tracker.\n- [ ] Consent tier, storage link, and any publication restriction recorded.\n- [ ] Residual structure and weak-sound flags carried to Stage 2.\n- [ ] Learner received the result and next action.\n\n### Your next campaign\nStage 2 moves toward B1: longer stories, opinions with reasons, present perfect against past, conditionals,\nrelative clauses, and greater independence when the conversation becomes unpredictable.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~2 min.** Pair the Day-1, Stage-0, and A2 samples privately where consent permits. The three-point timeline\n> is proof of development, not permission to publish. Set one concrete B1 goal with the learner.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- You moved from describing a day to narrating, comparing, planning, advising, and handling complications.\n- A2 means connected, intelligible everyday communication — not perfection.\n- Self-repair and clarification are signs that you can keep communication alive.\n- Your recording is evidence of progress; your consent controls how it may be used.\n- This is a milestone on the route, not the end of the route.\n\n---\n\n## 🎓 Empire sign-off\n> **Citizen → A2 milestone reached when the evidence meets the standard.**\n> You did not collect rules. You built enough control to use English across time, choices, needs, and real\n> interaction. Keep the recording. Let the evidence speak. Then begin the next climb. 👑\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 · Stage 1 — Citizen · A2 MILESTONE · من دراسة الإنجليزي لاستخدامه*\n","s1-u2-l01":"# \"Yesterday I Worked\" — Past Simple, Regular Verbs\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 2 · **Lesson 1**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: Unit 1 (*was/were*)\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit2-past-regular-verbs.md` · **S1-U2-L01***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 6 · Your Turn 3 · Feedback 1.\n> **Objectives:** form the regular past with *-ed* for all persons · say 3–4 things they did yesterday · use *ago*.\n> **The one error to hunt today:** dropping the ending — *\"Yesterday I work.\"* Insist on it in writing and in speech. Do not chase pronunciation yet; that is L02's whole job.\n> **Support:** hand them the verb bank plus the frame. **Stretch:** add a time expression and a *because* reason.\n> **Arabic ~40%.** Unit 1 gave them a past tense with two forms; this one gives them a past tense for *every* verb, which is a much bigger door.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Put **-ed** on a regular verb to talk about the past: *work → worked*.\n- Use the **same form for everyone**: *I worked · she worked · we worked*.\n- Say **3–4 true things** you did yesterday, and use *ago*. 🎙️\n\n> في الوحدة اللي فاتت اتعلمت تقول إنت **كنت** فين. دلوقتي هتقول إنت **عملت** إيه.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\n*was* and *were* only describe states. This lesson gives you **actions** — and actions are what stories are\nmade of.\n\nOne ending on the end of a verb, and any regular verb moves into the past. You do not memorise a new form\nfor each verb, and nothing changes for *he* or *she*.\n\n> قاعدة واحدة بتفتحلك آلاف الأفعال، ودي أسهل قاعدة في الإنجليزي كله فاستغلها.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom Unit 1: *Where were you yesterday?* Answer in one sentence.\n\nNow notice what you **could not** say yet — what you actually *did*.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Two or three answers aloud, then name the gap explicitly. Learners engage far better with a\n> rule when they have just felt its absence.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nSomeone describes an ordinary evening (📹 in the portal).\n\n> Yesterday I **worked** until six. Then I **cooked** dinner and **watched** TV with my family.\n> My brother **called** me at ten. I **finished** the day early — I **wanted** to sleep.\n\n**Notice:** every action verb ends in **-ed**. Six verbs, one ending.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| I worked until six. | اشتغلت لحد ستة |\n| Then I cooked dinner. | بعد كده عملت العشا |\n| He called me. | كلمني |\n| I finished early. | خلصت بدري |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second time learners count the *-ed* verbs. Do not correct pronunciation of the\n> endings yet — noticing the pattern is today's win.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**The rule is one line: regular verb + *-ed*.**\n\n| Present | Past |\n|---|---|\n| work | work**ed** |\n| watch | watch**ed** |\n| cook | cook**ed** |\n| call | call**ed** |\n| finish | finish**ed** |\n\n**And the form is the same for every person.** Say all five aloud and hear that nothing changes:\n\n> *I worked · you worked · he worked · we worked · they worked*\n\nUnlike the present tense, there is no extra **-s** for *he* or *she*. This is the simplest tense in English.\n\n> مفيش تغيير حسب الفاعل زي المضارع، وده بيوفر عليك مجهود كبير.\n\n**Compare with the present:**\n\n- *I **work** every day.* → habit, now\n- *I **worked** yesterday.* → finished, in the past\n\n> الفكرة اللي لازم تستقر في دماغك إن الـ **-ed** دي علامة وقت، مش مجرد حرفين في آخر الكلمة. لما\n> بتقولها، إنت بتقول للسامع إن الحدث خلص وانتهى. وعشان كده لو نسيتها، الجملة بتتحول من حاجة حصلت\n> امبارح لحاجة بتعملها كل يوم، والمعنى بيتغير تمامًا. ودي أهم نقطة في الدرس، لأنها بتتكرر مع كل فعل\n> هتتعلمه بعد كده.\n\n> ⚠️ **الغلطة المتوقعة النهاردة:** إنك تنسى الـ **-ed**. ساعتها السامع بيفهمها عادة، مش امبارح.\n\n**Using *ago* — count backwards from now.** It goes **after** the period of time, never before it:\n\n- ✅ *two days **ago*** · *a week **ago*** · *three years **ago***\n- ❌ *ago two days*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Teach the single form first and make them say all five persons aloud — the sameness is\n> genuinely reassuring at this level. Then contrast present against past, then *ago* word order.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| worked / finished | اشتغلت / خلصت | *I worked late.* |\n| cooked / cleaned | طبخت / نضفت | *She cooked dinner.* |\n| watched / listened | اتفرجت / سمعت | *We watched a film.* |\n| called / talked | كلمت / اتكلمت | *He called me.* |\n| studied / visited | درست / زرت | *I visited my aunt.* |\n| … ago | فات | *two days ago* |\n| until | لحد | *I worked until six.* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**A first taste of the three *-ed* sounds.** Say these and listen to how the endings differ:\n\n> work**ed** · play**ed** · visit**ed**\n\nThey are **not** the same sound. One is a /t/, one is a /d/, and one adds a whole syllable.\n\n**Today, one rule only: do not add an extra vowel.** *worked* is **one** syllable — not \"work-ed\".\n\n> النقطة الوحيدة النهاردة: ماتزودش حركة في آخر الفعل. الكلمة تفضل مقطع واحد.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"Yesterday I worked and cooked.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Deliberately shallow — the full three-way sort is L02. Today just kill the inserted vowel,\n> which is the habit that makes *-ed* verbs hard to understand.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — make it past.**\n1. I ______ (work) on Saturday.\n2. She ______ (cook) a big meal.\n3. They ______ (watch) the match.\n4. We ______ (finish) at nine.\n\n**Exercise 2 — present or past?**\n1. *I ______ English every morning.* (study — habit)\n2. *I ______ English for two hours yesterday.* (study — past)\n\n**Exercise 3 — put *ago* in the right place.**\n1. I visited Cairo (three years) → __________________________\n2. She called me (ten minutes) → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 4 — true for you.** Three things you did yesterday.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** **Ex1:** worked · cooked · watched · finished. **Ex2:** study · studied.\n> **Ex3:** three years ago · ten minutes ago. **Ex4:** check the ending is present in writing — that is\n> the target, not the sound.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nSay **three true things** you did yesterday, then add one thing you did **a long time ago**.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~3 min.** **Success check:** three correct regular past forms, intelligible, *-ed* audible as a\n> consonant. Note who inserts a vowel — they are your L02 priority group.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post: *\"Yesterday I ___, ___, and ___.\"* using three regular verbs.\n- **Stack the habit:** attach your twenty minutes to something you already do daily — after coffee, after\n  the commute, after prayer. A new habit needs an old one to lean on.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- Regular verb + **-ed**, and the form is **the same for every person**.\n- Do not drop the ending: *worked*, not *work*.\n- **ago** comes **after** the time: *two days ago*.\n- Keep it one syllable — no extra vowel.\n- *(The three -ed sounds are next lesson.)*\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 1) worked · 2) cooked · 3) watched · 4) finished\n- **Ex2:** 1) study · 2) studied\n- **Ex3:** 1) three years ago · 2) ten minutes ago\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — قاعدة واحدة فتحتلك آلاف الأفعال*\n","s1-u2-l02":"# The *-ed* Sound: /t/ · /d/ · /ɪd/ ⭐\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 2 · **Lesson 2** — *ACCENT LAB FOCUS*\n⏱️ ~35 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L01\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit2-past-regular-verbs.md` · **S1-U2-L02***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~35 min):** Warm-up 4 · Perceive 6 · Decode 7 · Accent Lab 8 · Guided 5 · Your Turn 3 · Feedback 1 · Assign 1.\n> **Objectives:** hear and produce the three endings · sort verbs into the three groups · use them correctly in sentences.\n> **This is the highest-value pronunciation lesson in the stage.** Arabic speakers add a full vowel to every *-ed*, so *worked* becomes \"work-ed\" — three syllables where there should be one. It affects every regular verb they will ever say, so it is worth the extra five minutes.\n> **Perception before production.** Do not let them produce until they can reliably hear the difference; producing an ending you cannot hear just installs a guess.\n> **Support:** master killing the extra vowel first, then /t/ against /d/. Ignore /ɪd/ until those land. **Stretch:** self-correct inside connected speech.\n> **Arabic ~35%** — the voiced/voiceless idea needs Arabic, the drilling does not.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- **Hear** the difference between *worked* /t/, *played* /d/, and *visited* /ɪd/.\n- **Sort** any regular verb into the right sound group.\n- Say past verbs so a listener understands them the first time. 🎙️\n\n> نفس الحروف **-ed** بتتنطق **بتلات طرق مختلفة**. ودي مش تفاصيل — دي الفرق بين إن حد يفهمك من أول مرة أو لأ.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nEnglish writes the past **one** way and says it **three** ways. Most Arabic speakers add a full vowel to\nevery ending, so *worked* comes out as three syllables instead of one.\n\n> والمشكلة إن الأفعال دي بتيجي في كل جملة ماضي، فالغلطة بتتكرر مية مرة في الكلام. وأصعب حاجة إنك\n> ماتسمعهاش في نفسك، فبتفضل تتكرر من غير ما تحس.\n\nRegular verbs appear in almost every past sentence you will ever speak. Fix this once and all of them get\nclearer at the same time — which is why this lesson gets its own star, and five extra minutes.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom L01, say five regular verbs in the past — any five.\n\nKeep them in your head; you are about to sort them.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Collect five on the board from the group. They become the sorting material, which makes the\n> rule feel discovered rather than delivered.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف (listen only)\nThree groups. Just **listen** and feel the difference (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **Group 1:** work**ed** · watch**ed** · stopp**ed** · help**ed** · talk**ed**\n> **Group 2:** play**ed** · clean**ed** · listen**ed** · call**ed** · stay**ed**\n> **Group 3:** visit**ed** · want**ed** · start**ed** · need**ed** · wait**ed**\n\nGroup 1 ends in a **/t/**. Group 2 ends in a **/d/**. Group 3 adds a **whole extra syllable**, /ɪd/.\n\n**Count the syllables:** *worked* = 1 · *played* = 1 · *visited* = 3.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Perception only — hold them back from repeating. Play each group twice, then mix and have\n> them point to a group. They must be able to *hear* it before they try to say it.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**The ending you use depends on the sound just before it.**\n\n| If the verb ends in… | the *-ed* is | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| a **voiceless** sound (k, p, s, sh, ch, f) | **/t/** | worked · stopped · watched · helped |\n| a **voiced** sound or a vowel (l, n, r, v, b, g, y…) | **/d/** | played · cleaned · called · listened |\n| **/t/ or /d/** | **/ɪd/** | visited · wanted · started · needed |\n\n> **يعني إيه voiced وvoiceless؟** حط إيدك على رقبتك وقول **s** طويلة — مفيش رعشة، فده صوت **مهموس**.\n> بعدين قول **z** — هتحس رعشة، فده صوت **مجهور**. والقاعدة إن آخر الفعل بيفرض على الـ **-ed** تمشي معاه.\n\nYour mouth already does this — it is easier to keep the voicing the same than to switch it mid-word.\n\n> يعني إنت مش بتحفظ قاعدة، إنت بتلاحظ حاجة بقّك بيعملها لوحده.\n\n> ⚠️ **الجروب التالت بس** هو اللي بياخد مقطع زيادة، لأن الفعل خالص بـ **t** أو **d**. في أي حالة تانية مفيش حركة.\n\nTwo identical consonants cannot sit together, so a vowel has to separate them. Everywhere else, the ending is\na single consonant with no vowel at all.\n\n**The quick test:** can you say the ending **without** opening your mouth again? If yes, it is /t/ or /d/.\nIf the word would jam, it is /ɪd/.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~7 min.** Teach voiced/voiceless with the hand-on-throat test in Arabic — it is a physical idea and\n> lands instantly. Then let them predict the group for five new verbs before you confirm. Emphasise that\n> /ɪd/ is the **narrow** case, not the default.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك (recall)\n\n| Group | Sound | Verbs |\n|---|---|---|\n| after voiceless | **/t/** | worked · watched · stopped · helped · talked · finished |\n| after voiced / vowel | **/d/** | played · cleaned · called · listened · stayed · studied |\n| after t / d | **/ɪd/** | visited · wanted · started · needed · waited · decided |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق (the sort)\n\n**Step 1 — sort these fifteen verbs into the three columns.** Say each one as you place it.\n\n> cooked · played · wanted · walked · cleaned · started · helped · listened · visited\n> talked · called · needed · finished · stayed · waited\n\n**Step 2 — say each column as a chain**, keeping the rhythm even:\n- /t/ · *cooked, walked, helped, talked, finished*\n- /d/ · *played, cleaned, listened, called, stayed*\n- /ɪd/ · *wanted, started, visited, needed, waited*\n\n**Step 3 — the mixed sentence.** This is the real test:\n\n> *I **worked**, then I **played** with my son, and later I **visited** my mother.*\n\nThree verbs, three different endings, one breath.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare** the mixed sentence. Listen for the extra vowel you are trying to remove.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~8 min.** The full sort, out loud, then the mixed sentence with record-and-compare. Circulate during\n> step 1 — the sorting is where you hear who has the rule and who is guessing.\n> **Answers:** /t/ cooked, walked, helped, talked, finished · /d/ played, cleaned, listened, called, stayed\n> · /ɪd/ wanted, started, visited, needed, waited.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — which sound?** Write /t/, /d/ or /ɪd/.\n1. stopped ______ 2. decided ______ 3. cleaned ______ 4. watched ______ 5. waited ______ 6. studied ______\n\n**Exercise 2 — count the syllables.**\n- worked ______ · wanted ______ · played ______ · started ______\n\n**Exercise 3 — say it, then check.** Read aloud, then verify each ending:\n> *She called me, I waited, and then we walked home.*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** **Ex1:** /t/ · /ɪd/ · /d/ · /t/ · /ɪd/ · /d/. **Ex2:** 1 · 2 · 1 · 2.\n> **Ex3:** called /d/, waited /ɪd/, walked /t/ — all three groups in one sentence.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nRetell yesterday using **at least three verbs from different groups**.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it**, then listen back once and mark any ending you added a vowel to.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~3 min.** **Success check:** three endings from three groups, produced correctly in connected speech.\n> Log each learner's habitual wrong group — that flag drives the L05 clinic.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post a voice note with **one verb from each group**, and say which sound each one is.\n- Two minutes a day beats twenty minutes on Friday. These endings need **reps**, not study.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- Voiceless → **/t/** · voiced or vowel → **/d/** · after **t/d** → **/ɪd/**.\n- Only the **third** group adds a syllable.\n- *worked* is one syllable. *visited* is three.\n- Hand on the throat tells you voiced from voiceless.\n- *(These endings get re-drilled in every unit from here — they need many reps.)*\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 1) /t/ · 2) /ɪd/ · 3) /d/ · 4) /t/ · 5) /ɪd/ · 6) /d/\n- **Ex2:** worked 1 · wanted 2 · played 1 · started 2\n- **Sort:** /t/ cooked, walked, helped, talked, finished · /d/ played, cleaned, listened, called, stayed · /ɪd/ wanted, started, visited, needed, waited\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — ظبطت صوت واحد، فظبطت كل جملة ماضي هتقولها*\n","s1-u2-l03":"# Spelling Rules for *-ed*: stop → stopped, study → studied\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 2 · **Lesson 3**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L02\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit2-past-regular-verbs.md` · **S1-U2-L03***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 7 · Accent Lab 3 · Train 7 · Your Turn 3 · Feedback —.\n> **Objectives:** spell the regular past correctly (doubling · *y*→*i* · drop-*e*) · write 4–5 correct sentences · self-check with the rule card.\n> **Do not overload.** Most learners take \"just add -ed\" immediately. Spend your time on *y*→*ied* and the doubling rule, which produce nearly all the errors.\n> **Support:** rule card plus worked examples in front of them. **Stretch:** a 4–5 sentence mini-diary of last weekend.\n> **Arabic ~35%.** The doubling rule needs Arabic; the practice does not.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Spell the past correctly: *stop → stopped · study → studied · live → lived*.\n- Know **which** rule applies before you write.\n- Write **4–5 correct past sentences** and check them yourself. 🎙️\n\n> النطق ظبطناه في الدرس اللي فات. النهاردة الكتابة.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nEverything you write gets read by someone — a work message, a comment, an exam. A slip like *\"stoped\"* reads\nas beginner even when your speaking is solid.\n\n> والحاجة الحلوة إن القواعد **تلاتة بس**، وأغلب الأفعال مش محتاجة أي واحدة فيهم.\n\nThree small rules, and then everything else is simply *+ed*.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nSay **and spell** three regular past verbs from L01 and L02.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Spelling aloud, letter by letter. It surfaces exactly who guesses.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nRead this short paragraph and **underline every past verb**.\n\n> Last weekend I **stopped** working early. I **studied** for two hours, then I **tidied** my room.\n> Later I **lived** the quiet life — I **cooked**, I **watched** a film, and I **planned** my week.\n\n**Notice:** *stopped* has two **p**s. *studied* and *tidied* end in **-ied**. *lived* dropped its **e**.\n*cooked* and *watched* just added *-ed*.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Learners underline, then group the verbs by what happened to the spelling *before* you name\n> the rules. The categories are visible in the data.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**Rule 1 — double the last letter** when the verb ends *consonant · vowel · consonant* and the last part\nis stressed.\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| sto**p** → sto**pp**ed | pla**n** → pla**nn**ed |\n| sto**p** = C-V-C | st**o**p carries the stress |\n\n> القاعدة بتشتغل لما الفعل يخلص بـ **ساكن + علة + ساكن**، والمقطع الأخير عليه نبرة.\n\n**Why the letter doubles:** it protects the short vowel inside. Written *\"stoped\"*, a reader would say it\nwith a long vowel, like *\"hoped\"*. The double letter is a signal that the vowel stays short.\n\n**Rule 2 — *y* becomes *ied*** after a consonant.\n\n- stud**y** → stud**ied** · tr**y** → tr**ied** · tid**y** → tid**ied**\n- **But:** after a *vowel*, keep the y — pla**y** → pla**yed** · sta**y** → sta**yed**\n\n> خلي بالك: الفرق كله في الحرف اللي **قبل** الـ y. لو ساكن، الـ y بتتحول لـ **i** وبنكتب **ied**. لو\n> حرف علة، بنسيبها زي ما هي ونضيف **-ed** عادي. بص على الحرف اللي قبلها قبل ما تكتب، وماتخمنش.\n\n**Rule 3 — drop the silent *e***.\n\n- liv**e** → liv**ed** · arriv**e** → arriv**ed** · decid**e** → decid**ed**\n\n> الـ **e** الساكتة في الآخر بتوقع، عشان ماينفعش نكتب **ee** ورا بعض. فبنشيلها ونحط **-ed** مكانها.\n\n**Everything else — just add *-ed*.** *work → worked · clean → cleaned · visit → visited*\n\nMost verbs land here, so the three rules above are the exceptions rather than the norm.\n\n> وأسهل طريقة إنك تحفظ مثال واحد لكل قاعدة، وترجع له دايمًا وقت الشك بدل ما تخمّن.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~7 min.** Teach Rule 1 with the Arabic explanation of *why* the letter doubles — learners who\n> understand it stop guessing. Rules 2 and 3 are mechanical and need only examples.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| Rule | English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| double | stop → stopped | وقف | *I stopped early.* |\n| double | plan → planned | خطّط | *We planned the trip.* |\n| y → ied | study → studied | درس | *She studied all night.* |\n| y → ied | try → tried | حاول | *I tried twice.* |\n| keep y | play → played | لعب | *They played football.* |\n| drop e | live → lived | عاش | *He lived in Cairo.* |\n| drop e | arrive → arrived | وصل | *We arrived late.* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n**Changing the spelling does not change the sound.**\n\n- *stopped* still ends in **/t/** — the extra **p** is silent.\n- *studied* still ends in **/d/**.\n- *decided* still ends in **/ɪd/**, because the verb ends in **d**.\n\n> الحرف المزدوج مش بيتنطق مرتين. الإملاء اتغيرت، الصوت زي ما هو.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"I stopped, I studied, and I decided.\"* — /t/, /d/, /ɪd/.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~3 min.** Short and pointed. Learners commonly start pronouncing the doubled letter once they see it.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — spell the past.**\n1. stop → __________ 2. study → __________ 3. live → __________\n4. play → __________ 5. plan → __________ 6. try → __________\n\n**Exercise 2 — which rule?** Write *double*, *y→ied*, *drop e*, or *just -ed*.\n1. arrived ______ 2. tidied ______ 3. watched ______ 4. planned ______\n\n**Exercise 3 — find and fix the mistake.**\n1. *I stoped at the shop.* → __________________________\n2. *She studyed all evening.* → __________________________\n3. *We arrivved late.* → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 4 — write four sentences about last week**, one for each rule.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~7 min.** **Ex1:** stopped · studied · lived · played · planned · tried.\n> **Ex2:** drop e · y→ied · just -ed · double. **Ex3:** stopped · studied · arrived.\n> **Ex4:** peer-check with the rule card before you collect.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nWrite **four sentences about last week**, using a different spelling rule in each, then read them aloud.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record one of them** — the one you found hardest to spell.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~3 min.** **Success check:** correct spelling across the rule types, and the L02 sounds still intact.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post your four sentences, one per rule, plus a voice note.\n- **Track your reps:** one tick on a paper or a note for every day you practise. Seeing the row of ticks is\n  what keeps it going.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- **C-V-C + stress** → double the last letter: *stopped · planned*.\n- **consonant + y** → **-ied**: *studied*. **vowel + y** → keep it: *played*.\n- **silent e** → drop it: *lived*.\n- Everything else: **just add -ed**.\n- Spelling changes; the **sound** does not.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** stopped · studied · lived · played · planned · tried\n- **Ex2:** 1) drop e · 2) y→ied · 3) just -ed · 4) double\n- **Ex3:** 1) stopped · 2) studied · 3) arrived\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — تلات قواعد صغيرة، وباقي الأفعال مجرد -ed*\n","s1-u2-l04":"# Sequencing a Day: *first · then · after that · later*\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 2 · **Lesson 4**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L03\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit2-past-regular-verbs.md` · **S1-U2-L04***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 5 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 7 · Your Turn 4 · Feedback —.\n> **Objectives:** order past actions with sequence words · narrate a 4–6 sentence sequence · put heard events in order.\n> **This is the scaffold for storytelling in Unit 3.** Chaining actions is the skill; the sequence words are just the joints. Get them comfortable chaining now and U3 becomes easy.\n> **Support:** sequence-word cards plus the verb bank. **Stretch:** add clock times and a feeling.\n> **Arabic ~35%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Put past actions **in order**: *first · then · after that · later · finally*.\n- Narrate your day in **4–6 connected sentences**.\n- Follow someone else's sequence and put the events in order. 🎙️\n\n> عندك الأفعال، وعندك النطق والإملاء. ناقص إنك تربطهم في حكاية مترتبة.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\n> الفرق بين حد بيقول جمل متفرقة وحد بيحكي فعلاً هو الترتيب.\n\nSay *\"First I worked, then I cooked\"* and your listener walks through the time with you, step by step, at no\neffort. Drop the linking words and they have to reassemble the order themselves — which makes you harder to\nfollow even when every single word is correct.\n\nSequence words are cheap to learn and they instantly make you sound organised.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom L02 and L03: say four regular past verbs, with the right ending and the right spelling.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Fast round. Correct endings in passing — the reps are the point.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nListen to someone describe yesterday, then **put the events in order** (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **First** I **cleaned** the kitchen. **Then** I **studied** for an hour.\n> **After that** I **called** my brother. **Later** I **watched** a film.\n> **Finally** I **finished** my report and **stopped** for the night.\n\nEvents: *studied · cleaned · watched · called · finished* — what was the real order?\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| First… | الأول… |\n| Then… | بعدين… |\n| After that… | بعد كده… |\n| Later… | بعد شوية… |\n| Finally… | وفي الآخر… |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play once; learners number the events. Play again to confirm. The ordering task forces them\n> to actually process the sequence words rather than skim past them.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**Where the sequence words go: at the front of the sentence, with a comma.**\n\n> **First**, I cleaned the kitchen. **Then**, I studied.\n\nIn speech the comma is a **small pause**, and that pause is what makes you easy to follow.\n\n| Word | Use it for |\n|---|---|\n| **First** | the opening action |\n| **Then · After that · Next** | each following action |\n| **Later** | something further away in time |\n| **Finally** | the last action |\n\n> ⚠️ **خلي بالك:** *then* بتربط خطوة بخطوة، لكن *finally* بتتقال **مرة واحدة بس** في آخر الحكاية. لو\n> قلتها في النص السامع هيفتكر إنك خلصت، وبعدين يتلخبط لما تكمل.\n\n> استخدم **First** مرة واحدة في الأول، وبعدها كرر **then** و**after that** زي ما تحب — مش لازم تنوّع\n> في كل جملة، والتكرار هنا مش عيب.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** Keep it brief — the words are transparent. The two things worth saying are the front\n> position with a pause, and that *finally* is single-use.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| first | الأول | *First, I woke up.* |\n| then | بعدين | *Then I cooked.* |\n| after that | بعد كده | *After that, I studied.* |\n| next | وبعدها | *Next, I called him.* |\n| later | بعد شوية | *Later, I watched TV.* |\n| finally | وفي الآخر | *Finally, I stopped.* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n**Rhythm: the beat lands on the verb, not on the sequence word.**\n\n> First I **CLEANED** the kitchen, then I **STUDIED**.\n\nGive a **small pause** after the sequence word, then hit the verb. Sequence words are signposts — say them\nlightly and move on.\n\n> النبرة على الفعل، والوقفة الصغيرة بعد كلمة الترتيب.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"First I worked, then I cooked, and after that I rested.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Model a version with the stress wrongly on *then* — it sounds robotic, and hearing that\n> makes the correct rhythm obvious.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — put the story in order.** Number 1–5.\n- ______ Then I studied for an hour.\n- ______ Finally, I watched a film.\n- ______ First, I cleaned my room.\n- ______ Later, I cooked dinner.\n- ______ After that, I called my sister.\n\n**Exercise 2 — join these into one sequence.** Add sequence words.\n> *I woke up. I walked to work. I finished at five. I visited my aunt.*\n\n**Exercise 3 — fix the mistake.**\n> *First I worked. Finally I cooked. Then I studied. Finally I slept.*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~7 min.** **Ex1:** 2 · 5 · 1 · 4 · 3. **Ex2:** accept any correct ordering with at least three\n> sequence words. **Ex3:** *finally* appears twice — only the last action keeps it.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nNarrate **your yesterday** in 4–6 sentences, using at least **three** sequence words and correct *-ed*\nendings.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** **Success check:** a coherent ordered narration, 4–6 sentences, endings intact under the\n> pressure of longer speech. Endings often slip once sentences get longer — note who, for L05.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post a 4–6 sentence *\"my day yesterday\"* using sequence words.\n- **Stack it:** do your English right after a fixed daily anchor, not \"sometime today\".\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- **First · then · after that · next · later · finally.**\n- Front of the sentence, then a **small pause**.\n- **finally** only once, at the end.\n- The beat goes on the **verb**.\n- *(Sequencing carries straight into Unit 3 storytelling and Unit 8.)*\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 2 · 5 · 1 · 4 · 3\n- **Ex3:** the first *finally* is wrong — it should be *then* or *after that*\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — الترتيب هو اللي بيحوّل الجمل لحكاية*\n","s1-u2-l05":"# Unit Speaking Task: \"What Did You Do Yesterday?\" (+ Review)\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 2 · **Lesson 5** — *UNIT MILESTONE*\n⏱️ ~35 min · 📹 Recorded + live (assessment-style) · Prerequisite: L01–L04\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit2-past-regular-verbs.md` · **S1-U2-L05***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~35 min):** Warm-up 5 · Model 4 · Checklist 4 · Accent clinic 4 · Rehearse 7 · Record 5 · Feedback 2 · Assign —.\n> **Objectives:** narrate 6–8 sentences with correct regular past, endings and sequencing · ask a partner about their day · record the Unit-2 narration.\n> **Celebrate that they can now narrate.** The endings will not be perfect and that is fine — keep re-drilling in later units rather than holding this milestone hostage to them.\n> **Support:** six sentences with the card. **Stretch:** add a *because* reason and a question back.\n> **Arabic ~30%** — fading. Instructions and encouragement only.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك (milestone)\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Narrate what you did in **6–8 connected sentences**.\n- Keep the **-ed endings** right while you speak at length.\n- Ask someone about their day and respond.\n- **Record your Unit-2 narration.** 🎙️\n\n> النهاردة تجمع كل حاجة: الأفعال، النطق، الإملاء، والترتيب — في حكاية واحدة.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن (سلسلة)\nRapid chain, no pausing: a regular verb → its *-ed* sound → a spelling rule → a sequence word.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** Fast retrieval across all four lessons. Keep the pace slightly uncomfortable — that is what\n> makes it consolidate.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — النموذج\nA clean narration (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **First**, I **started** work at eight. I **worked** until two, and then I **walked** home because the\n> traffic **stopped** completely. **After that** I **cooked** lunch and **called** my mother.\n> **Later** I **studied** English for thirty minutes. **Finally** I **watched** one episode and **finished**\n> the day early. It **was** a normal day, but a good one.\n\n**Eight sentences.** Count the endings: how many /t/, how many /d/, how many /ɪd/?\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Play once for flow, once for endings. The counting task keeps L02 alive inside a fluency\n> lesson, which is where endings usually collapse.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 The Checklist — تشيك ليست الحكي الكويس\n1. ✅ **Three or more regular verbs** in the past\n2. ✅ **Correct endings** — /t/ · /d/ · /ɪd/\n3. ✅ **Two or more sequence words** — *first · then · after that · finally*\n4. ✅ **A time or a feeling** — *at eight · it was tiring*\n5. ⭐ **A reason with *because***\n6. ⭐ **A question back** — *What did you do yesterday?*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Put the checklist on the board and hand out the card. Learners holding the card produce\n> visibly longer and better-ordered narrations.\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — عيادة النطق\nDrill **your** weakest ending group from this unit:\n\n- **/t/** after voiceless — *worked · watched · stopped · helped*\n- **/d/** after voiced or a vowel — *played · cleaned · called · studied*\n- **/ɪd/** after t or d — *visited · wanted · started · needed*\n\nThen say the mixed sentence three times: *\"I worked, I played, I visited.\"*\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare** your weakest group only.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Personalised from the flags you logged in L02 and L04. One group each, not all three.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — التسجيل (milestone)\nRehearse with the checklist and a partner, then record.\n\nNarrate **what you did yesterday** in 6–8 sentences — three or more regular verbs, correct endings, at\nleast two sequence words — and finish by asking your partner about their day.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it and keep it.** 👑\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min recording + 2 feedback.** Rehearse in pairs first and record the second attempt.\n> **Success check (Unit-2):** connected 6–8 sentence narration, correct regular past, endings mostly\n> accurate (rubric: Fluency + Accuracy + Pronunciation at A2). Give feedback at group level on endings —\n> naming individuals here deflates a milestone.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- **Post your narration.**\n- **Complete the Unit-2 quiz.**\n- Play your Unit-1 recording, then this one. Unit 1 was states; this is actions.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر (Unit 2 wrap)\n- Regular verb + **-ed**, same form for every person.\n- Three endings: voiceless → **/t/** · voiced or vowel → **/d/** · after t/d → **/ɪd/**.\n- Three spellings: **double** · **y→ied** · **drop e** — everything else just takes *-ed*.\n- **first · then · after that · finally** turn sentences into a story.\n- *(All of it returns in Unit 3 with irregular verbs and questions.)*\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — Unit 2 mini-quiz\n1. *Yesterday I ___ (work) late.* → **worked**\n2. The *-ed* in *wanted* is → **/ɪd/**\n3. The *-ed* in *cleaned* is → **/d/**\n4. *stop* in the past → **stopped**\n5. *study* in the past → **studied**\n6. Which word comes last in a sequence? → **finally**\n\n---\n\n## 🎉 Unit 2 complete — بقيت تحكي\nYou can now say **what you did**, in order, with the endings a listener expects. Two units ago you could\nonly describe this moment.\n\n**Next → Unit 3: The Past (3) — Irregular Verbs & Questions.** The verbs that refuse to take *-ed*, and\nhow to ask *\"What did you do?\"* 👑\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — من جمل متفرقة لحكاية كاملة*\n","s1-u3-l01":"# Irregular Verbs (1): *went · had · did · saw · ate*\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 3 · **Lesson 1**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: Unit 2 (regular past)\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit3-past-irregular-and-questions.md` · **S1-U3-L01***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 6 · Your Turn 3 · Feedback 1.\n> **Objectives:** use five high-frequency irregular past forms · mix regular and irregular in one account · recognise that irregulars take no *-ed*.\n> **Expect *\"goed\"* and *\"eated\"*, and do not treat them as failure.** Over-regularising is a sign the learner has *internalised* the *-ed* rule and is applying it faithfully. Correct warmly and re-expose; frequency builds the memory, not explanation.\n> **Support:** verb card in hand. **Stretch:** combine with regular verbs and a sequence word.\n> **Arabic ~35%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Use five past forms that **break the rule**: *went · had · did · saw · ate*.\n- Mix them with the *-ed* verbs you already know.\n- Say **three true past sentences** using at least two of them. 🎙️\n\n> في الوحدة اللي فاتت كل الأفعال كانت بتاخد **-ed**. النهاردة هتقابل الأفعال اللي بترفض القاعدة.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nIrregular verbs are few in number but they are the **most used verbs in the language**. *go*, *have*, *see*\nand *eat* appear in almost everything anyone says, so you cannot tell a story without them.\n\nThe good news is that A2 needs only about **ten** of them, and they stick through hearing and repetition\nrather than through memorising a table.\n\n> بتتحفظ بالسمع والتكرار، مش بالحفظ من ورقة.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom Unit 2, say four regular past verbs with their correct endings.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Quick round. Keeps the *-ed* endings alive while you introduce verbs that refuse them.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nSomeone describes a good day (📹 in the portal).\n\n> Yesterday I **went** to work early. At one o'clock I **had** lunch with a friend.\n> After work I **saw** a film with my wife, and we **ate** dinner at a small restaurant.\n> I **did** nothing else — it **was** enough.\n\n**Notice:** not one of those verbs ends in **-ed**. They change shape completely.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| I went to work. | رحت الشغل |\n| I had lunch. | اتغديت |\n| I saw a film. | شفت فيلم |\n| We ate dinner. | اتعشينا |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second time learners try to guess each present form. The guessing exposes the\n> irregularity far better than a table does.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**These five verbs have their own past form. There is no rule — there is a list.**\n\n| Present | Past |\n|---|---|\n| go | **went** |\n| have | **had** |\n| do | **did** |\n| see | **saw** |\n| eat | **ate** |\n\n> ماتحاولش تلاقي قاعدة، لأن مفيش. الأفعال دي شكلها بيتغير تمامًا، ولازم تتحفظ زي ما هي. وأسرع طريقة\n> إنك تقولها في جمل حقيقية عن نفسك، مش تكررها لوحدها — العقل بيمسك الكلمة أحسن لما تكون في سياق.\n\n**And they never take *-ed*:**\n\n- ✅ *I **went** to work.* → ❌ *I goed to work.*\n- ✅ *We **ate** dinner.* → ❌ *We eated dinner.*\n\n> لو قلت *\"goed\"* أو *\"eated\"*، ده معناه إنك فهمت قاعدة الـ **-ed** صح وبتطبقها. المشكلة بس إن الأفعال\n> دي استثناء. الحل التكرار، مش المراجعة.\n\n**Careful with *did*.** It is the past of *do*, and it is also the question word you will meet in L03:\n\n- *I **did** my homework.* → the verb\n- *Where **did** you go?* → the question helper *(L03)*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Present the list, drill it orally, then contrast against a regular verb so the difference is\n> felt. Flag *did* as doing two jobs but do not teach the question use yet — that is L03.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| go → went | رحت | *I went home.* |\n| have → had | كان عندي / اتغديت | *I had lunch.* |\n| do → did | عملت | *I did my work.* |\n| see → saw | شفت | *I saw my friend.* |\n| eat → ate | اكلت | *We ate together.* |\n| early / late | بدري / متأخر | *I went early.* |\n| enough | كفاية | *It was enough.* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Target 1 — stress the main verb.** In a past sentence the verb carries the beat:\n\n> I **WENT** to work. · We **ATE** dinner. · I **SAW** a film.\n\n**Target 2 — keep these three vowels clean and distinct:**\n\n| Verb | Sound | Watch out |\n|---|---|---|\n| w**e**nt | /e/ — short, like *bed* | not \"wint\" |\n| s**aw** | /ɔː/ — long and open | not \"so\" |\n| **a**te | /eɪ/ — like *eight* | not \"at\" |\n\n> الفروق الصغيرة في حروف العلة دي هي اللي بتخلي الكلمة تتفهم من أول مرة.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"I went out, I saw a friend, and we ate together.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Verb stress first, then the three vowels as minimal contrasts. *saw* is usually the weakest.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — past form.**\n1. go → ______ 2. see → ______ 3. eat → ______ 4. have → ______ 5. do → ______\n\n**Exercise 2 — find and fix the mistake.**\n1. *Yesterday I goed to the market.* → __________________________\n2. *We eated at eight.* → __________________________\n3. *I seed him yesterday.* → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 3 — regular or irregular?** Put each past form in the right column.\n> worked · went · cooked · saw · had · watched · ate\n\n**Exercise 4 — true for you.** Two sentences using two different irregular verbs.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** **Ex1:** went · saw · ate · had · did. **Ex2:** went · ate · saw.\n> **Ex3:** regular — worked, cooked, watched · irregular — went, saw, had, ate.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nSay **three true sentences** about yesterday, using at least **two** irregular verbs.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record them.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~3 min.** **Success check:** two set-1 irregulars used correctly. Log who over-regularises — they need\n> exposure, not a rule.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post: *\"Yesterday I went ___, had ___, and saw ___.\"*\n- **Design your environment:** put one English cue where you cannot avoid seeing it — a phone wallpaper, a\n  note on the mirror, a label on the fridge.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- **went · had · did · saw · ate** — no *-ed*, ever.\n- There is no rule for these. There is a list, and repetition.\n- *goed* and *eated* mean your *-ed* rule is solid — now learn the exceptions.\n- Stress the **verb**; keep *went · saw · ate* distinct.\n- *(Five more irregulars next lesson.)*\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** went · saw · ate · had · did\n- **Ex2:** 1) went · 2) ate · 3) saw\n- **Ex3:** regular — worked, cooked, watched · irregular — went, saw, had, ate\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — الأفعال اللي بترفض القاعدة هي أكترها استخدامًا*\n","s1-u3-l02":"# Irregular Verbs (2): *bought · got · came · made · took*\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 3 · **Lesson 2**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L01\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit3-past-irregular-and-questions.md` · **S1-U3-L02***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 6 · Your Turn 4 · Feedback —.\n> **Objectives:** use five more irregular past forms · narrate a short sequence with sets 1 and 2 · self-check against the card.\n> **Ten irregulars is plenty for A2.** Resist the urge to hand out a fifty-verb table — add more only as they surface naturally. Fluency with ten beats recognition of fifty.\n> **Support:** reference card plus a frame. **Stretch:** a five-sentence mini-story with sequence words.\n> **Arabic ~35%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Use five more irregular pasts: *bought · got · came · made · took*.\n- Tell a short sequence mixing **all ten** irregulars you now know.\n- Check your own forms against the card instead of guessing. 🎙️\n\n> خمسة كمان، وبكده يبقى عندك عشرة أفعال شاذة — وده كفاية تحكي بيهم أي حكاية في المستوى ده.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nThese ten verbs cover most of what you actually do in a day — go, buy, take, make, come. A very small\namount of memorising opens a large amount of speech.\n\nWhat matters is **using** them often, not studying them often. Repetition in speech is what fixes them.\n\n> استخدمهم كتير، مش تحفظهم كتير.\n\nTen verbs, and you can describe most of an ordinary day.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom L01, use each of these in a sentence: *went · had · saw · ate · did*.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** All five, out loud, fast. This is the frequency that makes them stick.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nA trip to the market (📹 in the portal).\n\n> On Friday I **went** to the market and **bought** vegetables and fruit.\n> I **got** a good price because I **came** early. Then I **took** a taxi home\n> and **made** lunch for my family. They **ate** everything.\n\n**Notice:** six irregular verbs in four sentences. This is normal — irregulars are everywhere.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| I bought vegetables. | اشتريت خضار |\n| I got a good price. | خدت سعر كويس |\n| I came early. | جيت بدري |\n| I took a taxi. | خدت تاكسي |\n| I made lunch. | عملت الغدا |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice; learners count the irregulars. The density is the point — it justifies the drilling.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**Set 2 — five more forms to know by heart.**\n\n| Present | Past |\n|---|---|\n| buy | **bought** |\n| get | **got** |\n| come | **came** |\n| make | **made** |\n| take | **took** |\n\n> زي المرة اللي فاتت: مفيش قاعدة، دي قائمة. بس خد بالك إن بعضهم بيتشابه في الشكل، وده بيساعد في الحفظ\n> جدًا — لما تلاقي مجموعة بتتغير بنفس الطريقة، احفظهم مع بعض مش واحد واحد.\n\n**A pattern that helps** — some irregulars change in similar ways:\n\n- *buy → bought* · *think → thought* · *bring → brought* — the **-ought** family\n- *come → came* · *become → became* — vowel swap only\n\n> ⚠️ **خلي بالك من *got*:** الفعل ده معناه واسع جدًا في الإنجليزي — *got a price*، *got home*،\n> *got a message*. ماتترجمهاش حرفيًا كل مرة، خده كوحدة مع الكلمة اللي بعده.\n\n**Your ten so far:** *went · had · did · saw · ate · bought · got · came · made · took*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Present set 2, then show the *-ought* family — grouping is what turns a list into memory.\n> Warn about *got* being semantically wide; learners try to translate it one-to-one and get stuck.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| buy → bought | اشتريت | *I bought bread.* |\n| get → got | خدت / وصلت | *I got home late.* |\n| come → came | جيت | *She came early.* |\n| make → made | عملت | *He made coffee.* |\n| take → took | خدت | *We took a taxi.* |\n| price | سعر | *a good price* |\n| everything | كل حاجة | *They ate everything.* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Target 1 — the /ɔː/ in *bought*.** Long, open, and **no /g/ sound at the end**. The *gh* is silent.\n\n> b**ough**t = \"bort\" · not \"bowght\", not \"bogt\"\n\n**Target 2 — keep the clusters clean in *made* and *took*.**\nOne syllable each. Do not add a vowel: *took*, not \"to-ok\".\n\n**Target 3 — the verb still carries the beat:**\n\n> I **BOUGHT** vegetables and **MADE** lunch.\n\n> النقطة الأهم: الـ **gh** في *bought* ماتنطقش خالص.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"I bought it, I took it home, and I made dinner.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** The silent *gh* is the single highest-value fix here; learners routinely voice it.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — past form.**\n1. buy → ______ 2. take → ______ 3. come → ______ 4. make → ______ 5. get → ______\n\n**Exercise 2 — complete with a set-1 or set-2 verb.**\n1. I ______ a new phone last week.\n2. She ______ to my house at eight.\n3. We ______ a taxi because we were late.\n4. He ______ a cake for her birthday.\n\n**Exercise 3 — chain the story.** Add one sentence each, using a different irregular verb:\n> *On Saturday I went to the mall…*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** **Ex1:** bought · took · came · made · got. **Ex2:** bought · came · took · made.\n> **Ex3:** run it round the group; each learner must use a verb nobody has used yet.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nNarrate something real and small — something you **bought**, **made**, or somewhere you **went** — in\n3–4 sentences.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** **Success check:** three or more irregulars from either set, used correctly in connected\n> speech.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post 3–4 sentences about something you bought or made recently.\n- **Reps compound.** Ten verbs this week, ten more soon — and the first ten will already feel automatic.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- **bought · got · came · made · took.**\n- The **gh** in *bought* is silent.\n- *got* is wide — learn it with the word that follows it.\n- Ten irregulars now cover most of an ordinary day.\n- *(Next: how to ask about all of this with did.)*\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** bought · took · came · made · got\n- **Ex2:** 1) bought · 2) came · 3) took · 4) made\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — عشرة أفعال بتفتح يوم كامل*\n","s1-u3-l03":"# Past Questions & Negatives: *did · didn't*\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 3 · **Lesson 3**\n⏱️ ~35 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L02\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit3-past-irregular-and-questions.md` · **S1-U3-L03***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~35 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 7 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 7 · Your Turn 4 · Feedback 1.\n> **Objectives:** ask past questions with *did* + base verb · make negatives with *didn't* · hold a past mini-interview.\n> **This is the number-one A2 error: keeping the past form after *did* — \"Did you went?\"** It is worth more drilling than anything else in the unit, because it appears in every past question they will ever ask. Drill the rule until it is reflex, not knowledge.\n> **Support:** question frames and short-answer cards to read from. **Stretch:** add *wh-* follow-ups.\n> **Arabic ~30%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Ask about the past: *Did you go? · What did you do?*\n- Say what did **not** happen: *I didn't go.*\n- Interview someone about their day and answer back. 🎙️\n\n> عندك الأفعال. ناقص إنك تسأل بيها.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nUntil now you have only been **telling**. The moment you can ask, you are having a conversation.\n\nAnd there is a gift buried in this lesson: because the verb returns to its simple form after *did*, one rule\ncovers **every** verb, regular and irregular alike. You do not need to recall a single past form to ask a\npast question.\n\n> يعني القاعدة دي بتلغي صعوبة الأفعال الشاذة كلها في السؤال.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom L01 and L02, say five irregular past verbs in full sentences.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Fast round. You want the past forms fresh, because the lesson is about *removing* them.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nTwo friends catching up (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **A:** **What did you do** yesterday?\n> **B:** I **went** out with my cousin. We **had** dinner in Zamalek.\n> **A:** **Did you see** Ali there?\n> **B:** No, I **didn't**. He **didn't come** — he was working.\n> **A:** **Where did you go** after that?\n> **B:** Nowhere. I **got** home early.\n\n**Notice:** after **did** and **didn't**, the verb goes back to its **simple** form — *see*, not *saw*.\n*come*, not *came*.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| What did you do? | عملت إيه؟ |\n| Did you see Ali? | شفت علي؟ |\n| No, I didn't. | لأ |\n| He didn't come. | هو ماجاش |\n| Where did you go? | رحت فين؟ |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second time, learners mark every verb that follows *did/didn't* and notice none of\n> them is a past form. Let them find it.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**The one rule: *did* carries the past, so the verb does not.**\n\n| ✅ Correct | ❌ Wrong |\n|---|---|\n| Did you **go**? | Did you ~~went~~? |\n| Did you **see** him? | Did you ~~saw~~ him? |\n| I didn't **eat**. | I didn't ~~ate~~. |\n| She didn't **take** it. | She didn't ~~took~~ it. |\n\n> ⚠️ **دي أشهر غلطة في المستوى ده كله.** فكّر فيها كده: كلمة **did** هي اللي شايلة الماضي، فالفعل\n> بعدها مش محتاج يشيله تاني. الماضي بيتقال **مرة واحدة** في الجملة.\n\n**Questions — put *did* first:**\n\n- ***Did** you go?* → **Yes, I did.** / **No, I didn't.**\n- ***What* did** you do?* · ***Where* did** you go?* · ***Who* did** you see?*\n\n**Negatives — *didn't* + simple verb:**\n\n- *I **didn't go** to work.* · *We **didn't have** time.*\n\n**And the bonus:** because the verb loses its past form, you do **not** need to remember whether it was\nregular or irregular. *Did you go?* and *Did you work?* follow the identical pattern.\n\n> يعني القاعدة دي بتلغي كل صعوبة الأفعال الشاذة في السؤال والنفي، وده مكسب كبير بجهد صغير.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~7 min.** Teach the \"past is said once\" idea — it is the mental model that stops the error. Then drill\n> conversion: you say an affirmative, they turn it into a question and a negative. Twenty reps beats an\n> explanation.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Did you…? | إنت…؟ | *Did you eat?* |\n| Yes, I did. / No, I didn't. | أيوه / لأ | short answers |\n| What did you do? | عملت إيه؟ | *What did you do?* |\n| Where did you go? | رحت فين؟ | *Where did you go?* |\n| Who did you see? | شفت مين؟ | *Who did you see?* |\n| I didn't… | أنا ما…ش | *I didn't go.* |\n| nowhere | مكانش في حاجة | *I went nowhere.* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Target 1 — *did* is weak inside a *wh-* question.**\nIt shrinks almost to /dəd/ and runs into the next word:\n\n> *What did you* → **\"whaddya\"** · *Where did you* → **\"wheredya\"**\n\nThat is not lazy speech — it is normal speech. Saying each word fully makes you sound like a textbook.\n\n**Target 2 — *didn't* is the opposite: keep the final /t/ clear.**\nIt is what makes the sentence negative.\n\n> I **didn't** go. — drop the /t/ and your listener hears the opposite.\n\n> النقطة المهمة: **did** بتخف في السؤال، لكن **didn't** لازم تتنطق واضحة.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"What did you do yesterday? I didn't go out.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Model the reduced *whaddya* — learners are often surprised it is correct. Then contrast with\n> a crisp *didn't*.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — fix the mistake.**\n1. *Did you went to the party?* → __________________________\n2. *I didn't saw him.* → __________________________\n3. *Where did she took the bus?* → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 2 — make it a question.**\n1. You saw the film. → __________________________\n2. He came late. → __________________________\n3. They ate together. → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 3 — make it negative.**\n1. I bought the tickets. → __________________________\n2. She had breakfast. → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 4 — short answers.**\n1. *Did you sleep well?* → Yes, ________ / No, ________\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~7 min.** **Ex1:** Did you go · I didn't see · Where did she take.\n> **Ex2:** Did you see the film? · Did he come late? · Did they eat together?\n> **Ex3:** I didn't buy the tickets · She didn't have breakfast. **Ex4:** Yes, I did / No, I didn't.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\n**Interview a partner.** Ask three past questions — one *yes/no* and two *wh-* — then report one of their\nanswers back to the group.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record one exchange.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** **Success check:** *did/didn't* + simple verb, correct throughout, plus one short answer.\n> Any surviving *\"Did you went\"* goes on the L05 clinic list.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post a past question to a classmate, and answer one, using *did* or *didn't*.\n- **Never miss twice.** One skipped day is nothing. Two in a row is how the habit dies.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- **did / didn't + the simple verb.** The past is said **once**.\n- *Did you go?* — never *Did you went?*\n- Short answers: **Yes, I did. / No, I didn't.**\n- *did* goes weak in questions; **didn't** stays crisp.\n- This rule works for regular and irregular verbs alike.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 1) Did you go to the party? · 2) I didn't see him · 3) Where did she take the bus?\n- **Ex2:** 1) Did you see the film? · 2) Did he come late? · 3) Did they eat together?\n- **Ex3:** 1) I didn't buy the tickets · 2) She didn't have breakfast\n- **Ex4:** Yes, I did / No, I didn't\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — الماضي بيتقال مرة واحدة بس في الجملة*\n","s1-u3-l04":"# Telling a Story: a Small Event From Your Week\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 3 · **Lesson 4**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L03\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit3-past-irregular-and-questions.md` · **S1-U3-L04***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 5 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 7 · Your Turn 4 · Feedback —.\n> **Objectives:** tell a 5–7 sentence true story · use story language · ask one past question about a peer's story.\n> **Stories are the A2 fluency engine.** Prioritise communication and flow. Correct only the two patterned errors — *did* + past form, and dropped endings — and do it as delayed feedback at the end, never mid-story.\n> **Support:** story-skeleton card and the connector list. **Stretch:** add direct speech or a feeling.\n> **Arabic ~30%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Tell a **true story** in 5–7 sentences.\n- Use story language: *One day · Suddenly · In the end*.\n- Listen to someone's story and ask a past question about it. 🎙️\n\n> عندك الأفعال والسؤال والنفي. النهاردة تحكي.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nStorytelling is the most useful skill in real conversation. People do not speak in separate sentences — they\ntell you things that happened. Once you can do that, you can join any gathering and say something that\nbelongs to you.\n\nA story is not a grammar exercise. It is what conversation is actually made of, and it has a shape you can\nrely on.\n\n> والشكل ده هو اللي بتستند عليه لما تحكي.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom L03: ask the person next to you two past questions with *did*.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Pairs. Keeps *did* + simple verb alive right before it gets used under pressure.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nA small, ordinary story (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **One day** last week I **went** to work by bus. The bus **was** very late,\n> so I **waited** for thirty minutes. **Suddenly** I **saw** an old friend at the bus stop —\n> we **didn't** meet for five years! We **talked** and **took** the bus together.\n> **In the end** I **arrived** late, but I **was** happy.\n\n**The shape:** a beginning (*when*), a middle (*what happened*), and an end (*how it finished*).\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| One day… | مرة… |\n| The bus was late. | الأتوبيس اتأخر |\n| Suddenly… | وفجأة… |\n| In the end… | وفي الآخر… |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play once for enjoyment, once for shape. Have them name the three parts. The shape is what\n> they will lean on when they tell their own.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**A story has three parts, and each part has its own words.**\n\n| Part | Words you use | Job |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Beginning** | *One day · Last week · Yesterday* | set the scene |\n| **Middle** | *Then · After that · Suddenly · Because* | what happened |\n| **End** | *In the end · Finally · So* | how it finished |\n\n> **Suddenly** هي أهم كلمة في الحكاية، لأنها بتقول للسامع إن فيه حاجة غير متوقعة جاية. حطها قبل\n> المفاجأة، وهتلاقي الناس بتركز معاك.\n\n**Two things a good story always has:**\n\n1. **Past verbs throughout** — do not drift into the present halfway.\n2. **A reason somewhere** — *because* tells us **why** it mattered.\n\n> ⚠️ **خلي بالك:** لما تتحمس في الحكي، بيحصل إنك ترجع للمضارع في النص. خليك في الماضي من الأول للآخر.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** Keep it short — the words are transparent. Spend your words on *Suddenly* as a listener\n> signal, and on tense consistency, which is where longer speech breaks down.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| One day… | مرة… | *One day I lost my keys.* |\n| Suddenly | فجأة | *Suddenly it started to rain.* |\n| In the end | في الآخر | *In the end I found them.* |\n| so | فـ | *It was late, so I ran.* |\n| wait → waited | استنيت | *I waited an hour.* |\n| lose → lost | ضيّعت | *I lost my phone.* |\n| find → found | لقيت | *I found it.* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Narrative rhythm: beat on the verbs, pause at the connectors.**\n\n> One day, ‖ I **WENT** to work ‖ and the bus **WAS** late.\n> **Suddenly,** ‖ I **SAW** my friend.\n\nThe small pause after a connector does two jobs: it gives the listener a moment to follow, and it gives\n**you** a moment to plan the next sentence. Use it deliberately.\n\n> الوقفة الصغيرة دي مش ضعف — دي وقت بتاخده لنفسك تفكر في الجملة اللي جاية.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** the first two sentences of the model, with the pauses.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Frame the pause as a *fluency tool*, not hesitation. Learners who accept it stop filling\n> silence with \"ummm\".\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — put the story in order.** Number 1–5.\n- ______ Suddenly, my phone rang.\n- ______ In the end, I got there on time.\n- ______ One day I woke up late.\n- ______ It was my boss.\n- ______ So I took a taxi.\n\n**Exercise 2 — add the missing story words.**\n> *______ last month I travelled to Alexandria. The train was full, ______ I stood for an hour.\n> ______ a man offered me his seat. ______ it was a good trip.*\n\n**Exercise 3 — fix the tense.**\n> *One day I went to the market. I buy some fruit and I see my neighbour.*\n\n**Exercise 4 — plan yours.** Write only three notes: beginning, what happened, end.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~7 min.** **Ex1:** 3 · 5 · 1 · 4 · 2. **Ex2:** One day · so · Suddenly · In the end.\n> **Ex3:** *bought* and *saw* — the classic mid-story drift. **Ex4:** notes only; stop anyone writing a\n> full script, or they will read it aloud instead of telling it.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nTell **your own true story** in 5–7 sentences, from your notes — not a script.\n\nThen listen to a partner's story and **ask one past question** about it.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record your story.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** **Success check:** a coherent 5–7 sentence story, connectors present, past tense held\n> throughout. Give feedback on the two patterned errors only, and only after everyone has finished.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post your story as a voice note, and ask a question about a classmate's.\n- **Input diet:** pick **one** English show or podcast at your level and stay with it. One familiar source\n  beats five you keep switching between.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- Three parts: **beginning · middle · end**.\n- **One day · Suddenly · In the end** — cheap words, big effect.\n- Stay in the past the whole way through.\n- The pause after a connector is **your** thinking time.\n- *(Storytelling comes back in Unit 8 for trips, and in Unit 10.)*\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 3 · 5 · 1 · 4 · 2\n- **Ex2:** One day · so · Suddenly · In the end\n- **Ex3:** *I **bought** some fruit and I **saw** my neighbour*\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — الناس مابتتكلمش جمل، الناس بتحكي*\n","s1-u3-l05":"# Unit Speaking Task: \"Tell Me About Your Last Holiday\" (+ Review)\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 3 · **Lesson 5** — *UNIT MILESTONE · closes the past-tense block*\n⏱️ ~35 min · 📹 Recorded + live (assessment-style) · Prerequisite: L01–L04\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit3-past-irregular-and-questions.md` · **S1-U3-L05***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~35 min):** Warm-up 5 · Model 4 · Checklist 4 · Accent clinic 4 · Rehearse 7 · Record 5 · Feedback 2 · Assign —.\n> **Objectives:** narrate 7–9 sentences with regular and irregular past · answer a live follow-up question · record the Unit-3 story.\n> **This closes the past-tense block — Units 1, 2 and 3 together.** It is the biggest single milestone in A2. Compare informally against their Stage-0 clip; the growth is large and learners rarely notice it themselves.\n> **Support:** seven sentences with the card. **Stretch:** direct speech plus two follow-up answers.\n> **Arabic ~25%** — nearly all English now.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك (milestone)\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Narrate a past experience in **7–9 sentences**.\n- Mix regular and irregular verbs, with connectors.\n- **Answer a follow-up question** live, without preparing it.\n- **Record your Unit-3 story.** 🎙️\n\n> ده أكبر إنجاز في المستوى ده: تلات وحدات كاملة عن الماضي، بتتجمع في حكاية واحدة.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن (سلسلة)\nRapid chain: an irregular verb → a *did* question → a *didn't* negative → a connector.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** Fast retrieval across all three past units. Keep the pace high.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — النموذج\nA clean holiday narration (📹 in the portal).\n\n> Last summer I **went** to the North Coast with my family. We **took** the car and **left** very early,\n> **so** we **didn't** sit in traffic. **One day** we **went** to the beach and **ate** fish at a small\n> restaurant — it **was** delicious. **Suddenly** the weather **changed** and it **rained**, so we\n> **came** back to the hotel. I **didn't** swim much, **but** I **had** a really good week.\n> **In the end** it **was** the best holiday of the year.\n\n**Nine sentences.** Count them: how many irregular verbs? How many negatives?\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Play once for flow, once for the count. The counting keeps accuracy visible inside a fluency\n> task.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 The Checklist — تشيك ليست الحكاية الكاملة\n1. ✅ **Set the scene** — *when* and *where*\n2. ✅ **Three or more past verbs**, regular and irregular\n3. ✅ **One negative** with *didn't*\n4. ✅ **Two connectors** — *so · but · suddenly · in the end*\n5. ✅ **A feeling or an opinion**\n6. ⭐ **Answer a follow-up question** without preparing it\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Board the checklist and hand out the card. Point at item 6 — the unrehearsed answer is what\n> distinguishes a speaker from a reciter, and it is what the rubric rewards.\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — عيادة النطق\nDrill **your** weakest past sound from these three units:\n\n- **-ed endings** — /t/ · /d/ · /ɪd/\n- **irregular vowels** — *went · saw · ate · bought*\n- **weak *did*** in questions, crisp **didn't** in negatives\n- **narrative rhythm** — beat on the verb, pause at the connector\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare** your weakest one only.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Personalised from your flags across U1–U3. One target each.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — التسجيل (milestone)\nRehearse with the checklist and a partner, then record.\n\nTell me about your **last holiday or day off** in 7–9 sentences. Then answer **one question** I ask you\nabout it, live.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it and keep it.** 👑\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min recording + 2 feedback.** Ask each learner one genuine follow-up — *Who did you go with? Why\n> didn't you swim?* — and let them answer unscripted.\n> **Success check (Unit-3):** connected 7–9 sentence narration with mostly-correct past forms, plus a\n> handled follow-up (rubric: Fluency + Accuracy + Pronunciation at A2). Play them their Stage-0 graduation\n> clip afterwards if you have it — three months of past tense is audible.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- **Post your story.**\n- **Complete the Unit-3 quiz.**\n- Listen to your Unit-1 recording, then this one. Unit 1 was *where you were*. This is *what happened*.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر (Units 1–3 wrap)\n- **was / were** for states · **-ed** for regular actions · **a list** for irregulars.\n- **did / didn't + simple verb.** The past is said once.\n- **One day · Suddenly · so · but · In the end** hold a story together.\n- Stay in the past from the first sentence to the last.\n- *(The past block is now complete. Unit 4 turns to the future.)*\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — Unit 3 mini-quiz\n1. *Yesterday I ___ (go) to Cairo.* → **went**\n2. *I ___ (buy) a new phone.* → **bought**\n3. *___ you see him?* → **Did**\n4. *She ___ come to the party.* (negative) → **didn't**\n5. *Did you ___ dinner?* (eat) → **eat** *(not ate)*\n6. Which word warns of a surprise? → **Suddenly**\n\n---\n\n## 🎉 Unit 3 complete — الماضي خلص كله\nThree units ago you could only describe **now**. You can now say where you **were**, what you **did**, what\nyou **didn't** do, ask someone about their past, and tell a story with a beginning and an end.\n\n**Next → Unit 4: Future Plans.** *going to* and *will* — the stage turns from behind you to ahead of you. 👑\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — تلات وحدات، وبقيت تحكي*\n","s1-u4-l01":"# \"I'm Going To…\" — Plans & Intentions\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 4 · **Lesson 1**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: Units 1–3 (past) · present *to be*\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit4-future-plans.md` · **S1-U4-L01***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 6 · Your Turn 3 · Feedback 1.\n> **Objectives:** talk about plans with *be going to* · use future time expressions · state 3–4 real plans.\n> **The stage pivots here.** Three units of past, and now the future. Say that out loud — learners feel the shape of a course and it motivates them.\n> **The structural insight worth teaching:** they already own *am/is/are* from Stage 0. *going to* is bolted onto something they can already do, so this is assembly, not new grammar.\n> **Support:** the frame plus a verb bank. **Stretch:** add a reason with *because*.\n> **Arabic ~35%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Say your plans: *I'm going to study tonight.*\n- Use future time words: *tomorrow · next week · this weekend*.\n- State **3–4 real plans** of your own. 🎙️\n\n> تلات وحدات كنا في الماضي. من النهاردة بنبص لقدام.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nPlans are half of ordinary conversation. *What are you doing this weekend? · When are you going to\ntravel? · I'm going to look for a new job.*\n\n> والحاجة الحلوة إن التركيب ده مش جديد عليك. إنت عندك **am / is / are** من المستوى اللي فات، وكل اللي\n> بنعمله إننا نضيف عليها **going to** والفعل. يعني مش قاعدة جديدة، ده تركيب بيتبني على حاجة إنت عارفها\n> خلاص، وعشان كده هتلاقيه أسهل من اللي فات.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nSay three sentences about **yesterday**, using the past.\n\nNow notice: you have no way yet to say what happens **tomorrow**.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Deliberately create the gap before filling it. Learners take a structure faster when they\n> have just felt its absence.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nSomeone talks about their week ahead (📹 in the portal).\n\n> This weekend I **'m going to visit** my parents in Tanta.\n> Next week I **'m going to start** a new course, and I **'m going to look for** a better job.\n> My sister **'s going to travel** to Dubai next month. We **'re going to miss** her.\n\n**Notice:** *am/is/are* changes with the person, but **going to** never changes, and the verb after it stays\nsimple.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| I'm going to visit my parents. | هزور أهلي |\n| I'm going to start a course. | هبدأ كورس |\n| She's going to travel. | هي هتسافر |\n| We're going to miss her. | هنوحشنا |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second time learners mark *am*, *is*, *are* and see that only that word moves.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**The formula: *am / is / are* + **going to** + simple verb.**\n\n| Subject | | | |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| I | **am** (I'm) | going to | study |\n| he / she / it | **is** ('s) | going to | travel |\n| you / we / they | **are** ('re) | going to | start |\n\n> الجزء الوحيد اللي بيتغير هو **am / is / are** حسب الفاعل. أما **going to** والفعل بعدها فثابتين مهما\n> كان الكلام عن مين.\n\n**Use it for things you have already decided.** *going to* carries the sense of a **plan** — you have\nthought about it, maybe arranged it:\n\n- *I'm going to buy a car next year.* → I have decided\n- *We're going to get married in June.* → it is arranged\n\n**Future time expressions** go at the start or the end:\n\n- **tomorrow · tonight · next week / month / year · this weekend · in two days**\n\n> ⚠️ **خلي بالك:** الفعل بعد **going to** بيفضل في صيغته الأصلية. بنقول *going to **study***، مش\n> *going to studied* ولا *going to studying*.\n\n**Negative:** put *not* after *am/is/are* — *I'm **not** going to work tomorrow.*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Build the formula on the board with the three subject rows, drill orally, then the negative.\n> The \"already decided\" meaning matters for the L04 contrast with *will*, so plant it now.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| I'm going to… | أنا هـ… | *I'm going to study.* |\n| tomorrow / tonight | بكرة / الليلة | *tomorrow morning* |\n| next week / month | الأسبوع / الشهر الجاي | *next week* |\n| this weekend | الويكند ده | *this weekend* |\n| visit / travel | أزور / أسافر | *I'm going to travel.* |\n| start / finish | أبدأ / أخلص | *She's going to start.* |\n| look for | أدوّر على | *look for a job* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Target 1 — link it into one smooth phrase.** Nobody says four separate words:\n\n> *I'm going to study* → **\"I'm-gonna-study\"**\n\n**Target 2 — *gonna* is what you will HEAR.**\nIn fast speech *going to* becomes **gonna**. You do not have to say it that way, but you must be able to\n**recognise** it, or you will miss the future tense in real conversation.\n\n> إنت مش مطالب تقول *gonna*، بس لازم تعرفها وقت السمع. أغلب الناس بتقولها كده في الكلام العادي.\n\n**Target 3 — the stress goes on the main verb**, not on *going*:\n\n> I'm gonna **STUDY** tonight. · She's gonna **TRAVEL**.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"I'm going to study tonight and I'm going to finish early.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Frame *gonna* as receptive first — it removes the anxiety of \"am I allowed to say this?\" and\n> fixes a real listening gap.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — am, is or are?**\n1. I ______ going to call him tonight.\n2. She ______ going to start school in September.\n3. They ______ going to move to a new flat.\n4. We ______ going to be late.\n\n**Exercise 2 — make the sentence.**\n1. (I / visit / my uncle / tomorrow) → __________________________\n2. (she / not / work / next week) → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 3 — fix the mistake.**\n1. *I'm going to studied tonight.* → __________________________\n2. *He going to travel next month.* → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 4 — true for you.** Three real plans, with a time word in each.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** **Ex1:** am · is · are · are. **Ex2:** *I'm going to visit my uncle tomorrow* ·\n> *She's not going to work next week*. **Ex3:** *going to study* · *He's going to travel* — the missing\n> *is* is the frequent one.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nSay **three or four real plans** — this week, this month, this year — each with a time expression.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record them.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~3 min.** **Success check:** correct *be* form + *going to* + simple verb, with a time expression.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post three plans: *\"Next week I'm going to ___.\"*\n- **Set your A2 goal.** One concrete, real-world target — not \"improve my English\" but something you could\n  point at: order a meal in English, pass an interview, hold a ten-minute call.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- **am / is / are + going to + simple verb.**\n- Only *am/is/are* changes. *going to* never does.\n- Use it for things you have **already decided**.\n- You will **hear** *gonna* — recognise it.\n- *(Next: how to ask other people about their plans.)*\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 1) am · 2) is · 3) are · 4) are\n- **Ex2:** 1) I'm going to visit my uncle tomorrow · 2) She's not going to work next week\n- **Ex3:** 1) *I'm going to study tonight* · 2) *He's going to travel next month*\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — من النهاردة بنبص لقدام*\n","s1-u4-l02":"# Asking About Plans: \"What Are You Going To Do?\"\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 4 · **Lesson 2**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L01\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit4-future-plans.md` · **S1-U4-L02***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 6 · Your Turn 4 · Feedback —.\n> **Objectives:** ask about plans with *be going to* · use *wh-* forms · give short answers.\n> **This is the same inversion they learned in Unit 1 with *was/were*.** Name that link explicitly — it converts a new lesson into a familiar move and takes half the time.\n> **Support:** question frames to read from. **Stretch:** ask a follow-up and report the answer to the group.\n> **Arabic ~30%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Ask about plans: *Are you going to travel?*\n- Ask for detail: *What · Where · When are you going to…?*\n- Answer briefly: *Yes, I am. / No, I'm not.* 🎙️\n\n> عرفت تقول خططك. دلوقتي تسأل غيرك عن خططه.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nAsking about someone's plans is one of the friendliest things you can do in a language. It is how\ninvitations start, how arrangements get made, and how a conversation moves forward instead of stalling.\n\n> ولاحظ إن الحركة دي إنت عارفها خلاص: نفس القلب اللي عملته في الوحدة الأولى مع **was/were**. الفعل\n> بيتقدم قبل الفاعل، وخلاص.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom L01, say two of your own plans out loud.\n\nThen ask the person next to you — however you can — what **their** plan is.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Let them attempt the question before it is taught. Whatever they produce becomes the material\n> you correct against.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nTwo colleagues talking about the holiday (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **A:** **Are you going to travel** in Eid?\n> **B:** Yes, I am. I **'m going to go** to Hurghada with my family.\n> **A:** Nice! **When are you going to leave**?\n> **B:** On Thursday. **What about you? Are you going to stay** in Cairo?\n> **A:** No, I'm not. I **'m going to visit** my grandmother in Assiut.\n\n**Notice:** the question moves *are* to the front — *You are going* becomes *Are you going*.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| Are you going to travel? | هتسافر؟ |\n| Yes, I am. / No, I'm not. | أيوه / لأ |\n| When are you going to leave? | هتمشي امتى؟ |\n| What about you? | وإنت؟ |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second time learners mark the position of *are* in the statement versus the\n> question. Also flag *What about you?* — it is the cheapest conversation-extender they will learn.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**Yes/no questions — move *am/is/are* to the front.**\n\n| Statement | Question |\n|---|---|\n| You **are** going to travel | **Are** you going to travel? |\n| She **is** going to work | **Is** she going to work? |\n\n> نفس القاعدة بالظبط اللي اتعلمتها في الوحدة الأولى: الفعل الأول، بعدين الفاعل.\n\n***Wh-* questions — put the question word first, then invert:**\n\n- ***What** are you going to do this weekend?*\n- ***Where** are you going to go?*\n- ***When** are you going to start?*\n- ***Who** are you going to travel with?*\n\n**Short answers — repeat the *be* verb, never the whole sentence:**\n\n- *Are you going to come?* → **Yes, I am.** / **No, I'm not.**\n- *Is he going to help?* → **Yes, he is.** / **No, he isn't.**\n\n> ⚠️ **خلي بالك:** في الرد القصير بنقول *\"Yes, I am\"* ومابنقولش *\"Yes, I'm\"*. الاختصار مايجيش في آخر\n> الجملة خالص.\n\n**And one phrase worth more than any grammar here:** *What about you?* — it hands the conversation back.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Anchor to the Unit-1 inversion, then drill *wh-* forms. The *\"Yes, I'm\"* error is universal;\n> correct it on the spot, every time.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Are you going to…? | هتـ…؟ | *Are you going to eat?* |\n| What are you going to do? | هتعمل إيه؟ | *What are you going to do?* |\n| Where / When | فين / امتى | *When are you going to go?* |\n| Yes, I am. / No, I'm not. | أيوه / لأ | short answers |\n| What about you? | وإنت؟ | hands it back |\n| leave / stay | أمشي / أفضل | *Are you going to stay?* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Target 1 — *are you* melts together.**\nIn natural speech it is barely two words:\n\n> *Are you going to…* → **\"əyə-gonna…\"**\n\nYou do not need to produce it, but you must **recognise** it — otherwise a perfectly normal question will\nsound like noise to you.\n\n**Target 2 — rising intonation on yes/no, falling on *wh-*.**\n\n> Are you going to travel? ↗\n> What are you going to do? ↘\n\n> السؤال بـ **yes/no** نبرته بتطلع، والسؤال بـ **wh-** نبرته بتنزل. الفرق ده بيوصل للسامع نوع السؤال قبل الكلمات.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** both questions, with the two different tunes.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** The two-tune contrast is quick and memorable. Exaggerate both, then bring to natural.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — make it a question.**\n1. You are going to study tonight. → __________________________\n2. She is going to call him. → __________________________\n3. They are going to move house. → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 2 — add the question word.**\n1. ______ are you going to travel? (place)\n2. ______ are you going to leave? (time)\n3. ______ are you going to do? (activity)\n\n**Exercise 3 — short answers.**\n1. *Are you going to come?* → Yes, ________ / No, ________\n2. *Is he going to drive?* → Yes, ________ / No, ________\n\n**Exercise 4 — fix it.** *\"Yes, I'm.\"* → __________\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** **Ex1:** Are you going to study tonight? · Is she going to call him? · Are they going to\n> move house? **Ex2:** Where · When · What. **Ex3:** Yes, I am / No, I'm not · Yes, he is / No, he isn't.\n> **Ex4:** *Yes, I am.*\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nInterview a partner about their plans. Ask **one yes/no question** and **two *wh-* questions**, then finish\nwith *\"What about you?\"*\n\n> 🎙️ **Record one exchange.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** **Success check:** correct inversion, one *wh-* question, and a proper short answer.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Ask a classmate about their plans in the community channel, and answer one question.\n- **Say your goal out loud to someone.** A goal you have told a real person is much harder to abandon.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- Move **am/is/are** to the front — the same move as Unit 1.\n- *What · Where · When · Who* + inversion.\n- **Yes, I am.** — never *Yes, I'm.*\n- *What about you?* keeps a conversation alive.\n- Yes/no rises ↗ · *wh-* falls ↘.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 1) Are you going to study tonight? · 2) Is she going to call him? · 3) Are they going to move house?\n- **Ex2:** 1) Where · 2) When · 3) What\n- **Ex3:** 1) Yes, I am / No, I'm not · 2) Yes, he is / No, he isn't\n- **Ex4:** *Yes, I am.*\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — السؤال هو اللي بيكمّل الكلام*\n","s1-u4-l03":"# \"I'll…\" — Decisions, Offers & Predictions with *will*\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 4 · **Lesson 3**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L02\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit4-future-plans.md` · **S1-U4-L03***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 6 · Your Turn 4 · Feedback —.\n> **Objectives:** use *will* for decisions, offers and predictions · use the contraction *'ll* · use *won't*.\n> **Teach *will* by FUNCTION, not as \"the future tense\".** Learners who meet it as a tense confuse it with *going to* forever. Learners who meet it as *deciding now · offering · predicting* keep the two apart naturally — which is what L04 then formalises.\n> **Support:** the three function cards. **Stretch:** predict something about next year and justify it.\n> **Arabic ~30%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- **Decide** on the spot: *I'll take this one.*\n- **Offer** help: *I'll carry that for you.*\n- **Predict**: *I think it'll rain.* 🎙️\n\n> **will** مش مجرد \"زمن المستقبل\". دي بتستخدم في تلات مواقف محددة، ولو فهمتهم مش هتلخبط بينها وبين\n> **going to** خالص.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\n*will* is the most **social** future in English. It is what you say when you decide in the moment, when you\noffer to help someone, and when you guess about what is coming.\n\n> يعني دي اللي بتستخدمها في المواقف الحياتية السريعة: في مطعم، في محل، ولما حد يحتاج مساعدة.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom L01 and L02, say a plan and ask a partner about theirs.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Quick pairs. You want *going to* fresh, because *will* is about to sit beside it.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nThree short moments (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **1 — deciding now**\n> *Waiter:* Are you ready to order?\n> *Customer:* Yes — I **'ll have** the chicken, please.\n>\n> **2 — offering**\n> *A:* These bags are heavy.\n> *B:* Don't worry, I **'ll help** you.\n>\n> **3 — predicting**\n> *A:* Do you think it **'ll rain** tomorrow?\n> *B:* No, I think it **'ll be** sunny. But it **won't be** hot.\n\n**Notice:** in none of these had the speaker planned anything in advance. They decided, offered, or guessed\n**as they spoke**.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| I'll have the chicken. | هاخد الفراخ |\n| I'll help you. | هساعدك |\n| I think it'll rain. | أظن هتمطر |\n| It won't be hot. | مش هتكون حرارة |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play each moment separately and name its function before moving on. Three clean examples beat\n> one long dialogue here.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**Form is easy: *will* + simple verb, and it never changes for the person.**\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| I / you / he / she / we / they | **will** go · **'ll** go |\n| negative | **won't** go |\n\n> ولا حتى حرف واحد بيتغير حسب الفاعل. أسهل من **going to** بكتير من ناحية الشكل.\n\n**The three jobs of *will*:**\n\n| Job | When | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Decision** | you decide *now*, while speaking | *I'll take this one.* |\n| **Offer / promise** | you offer help | *I'll call you later.* |\n| **Prediction** | you guess about the future | *I think it'll be fine.* |\n\n**Contractions are the normal form.** In speech, *I will* is almost always **I'll**:\n\n- I'll · you'll · he'll · she'll · we'll · they'll · **won't**\n\n> ⚠️ **خلي بالك:** *won't* دي اختصار *will not*، ومش ليها علاقة بكلمة *want*. النطق مختلف تمامًا.\n\n**With predictions, add *I think*:** *I think it'll rain.* It softens the guess and sounds natural.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Form first — it is trivially easy and gives them a quick win. Then the three functions with an\n> oral example each. Do not contrast with *going to* yet; that is L04's whole job.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| I'll… | هـ… | *I'll call you.* |\n| won't | مش هـ… | *I won't be late.* |\n| I think… will | أظن هـ… | *I think he'll come.* |\n| I'll have… | هاخد… | *I'll have tea.* |\n| I'll help you | هساعدك | offering |\n| Don't worry | ماتقلقش | *Don't worry, I'll do it.* |\n| maybe | يمكن | *Maybe it'll rain.* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Target 1 — the contraction *'ll* is tiny. Do not skip it.**\nIt is a single /l/ sound joined to the pronoun:\n\n> *I'll* = \"ail\" · *he'll* = \"heel\" · *we'll* = \"weel\" · *they'll* = \"thail\"\n\nLearners often drop it entirely and say *\"I go\"* — which sounds like the present.\n\n**Target 2 — *won't* has a long /oʊ/.**\n\n> *won't* = \"wohnt\" — long, like *don't*. It is **not** *want* /wɒnt/.\n\n> الفرق بين *won't* و*want* بيغيّر المعنى تمامًا، فخد بالك من حرف العلة.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"I'll call you tonight, but I won't be late.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Drill *'ll* attached to each pronoun, then the *won't/want* contrast — that pair causes real\n> misunderstandings.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — which job?** Write *decision*, *offer* or *prediction*.\n1. *I'll get the door for you.* ______\n2. *I think she'll pass the exam.* ______\n3. *OK, I'll take the blue one.* ______\n\n**Exercise 2 — contract it.**\n1. I will help you. → __________________________\n2. He will not come. → __________________________\n3. We will see. → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 3 — respond with *will*.**\n1. *\"This box is very heavy.\"* → __________________________\n2. *\"Are you ready to order?\"* → __________________________\n3. *\"Do you think the traffic will be bad?\"* → __________________________\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** **Ex1:** offer · prediction · decision. **Ex2:** I'll help you · He won't come · We'll see.\n> **Ex3:** accept any natural offer, order and prediction — function matters more than wording.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nUse *will* three times, once for each job: **decide** something, **offer** something, **predict** something.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record all three.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** **Success check:** three correct *'ll* forms across the three functions, contraction audible.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post three sentences: one decision, one offer, one prediction.\n- **Visualise the win.** Picture yourself using English successfully — a real conversation you want to have.\n  Then say which one it is.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- **will + simple verb.** Never changes for the person.\n- Three jobs: **decide now · offer · predict**.\n- Say the contraction: **I'll**, not *I*.\n- **won't** is long — do not confuse it with *want*.\n- *(Next lesson: which one to choose, will or going to.)*\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 1) offer · 2) prediction · 3) decision\n- **Ex2:** 1) I'll help you · 2) He won't come · 3) We'll see\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — will بتاعة اللحظة، مش بتاعة التخطيط*\n","s1-u4-l04":"# *Going To* vs *Will* — Choosing Which\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 4 · **Lesson 4**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L03\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit4-future-plans.md` · **S1-U4-L04***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 6 · Your Turn 4 · Feedback —.\n> **Objectives:** choose between the two forms · switch between them in one conversation · justify the choice.\n> **The distinction is about WHEN THE DECISION HAPPENED, not about time.** *going to* = decided before you spoke. *will* = decided as you spoke. Every other explanation of this pair leaks; this one holds.\n> **Do not chase perfection.** Native speakers overlap them constantly and a wrong choice is almost never misunderstood. Reward correct *meaning* over correct form.\n> **Support:** the two-card sort. **Stretch:** a dialogue that uses both naturally.\n> **Arabic ~30%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Choose **going to** or **will** and know why.\n- Use both in the **same** conversation.\n- Hear the difference when other people use them. 🎙️\n\n> الفرق بينهم مش في الوقت — الاتنين عن المستقبل. الفرق في **القرار اتخد امتى**.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nThis is the one A2 choice that makes you sound like you are thinking in English rather than translating.\nAnd it is far simpler than most books make it.\n\nAnd if you pick the wrong one, you will almost never be misunderstood — so speak comfortably and let the\naccuracy arrive with time.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nSay one sentence with *going to* and one with *will*.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Both forms in the room before the contrast starts.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nListen for the moment the decision happens (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **A:** What are you doing tonight?\n> **B:** I **'m going to study** — I have an exam on Sunday.\n> **A:** Oh, I didn't know. Do you want some help?\n> **B:** Really? Then I **'ll come** to your place at seven.\n> **A:** Perfect. I **'ll make** some coffee.\n\n**Notice the shift.** *studying* was **already planned**, so B used *going to*. Coming at seven and making\ncoffee were **decided in that moment**, so both used *will*.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| I'm going to study. | كنت ناوي أدرس |\n| Then I'll come at seven. | يبقى هاجي سبعة |\n| I'll make some coffee. | هعمل قهوة |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second time, stop after each future form and ask: *had they decided this before\n> the conversation started?* That single question is the whole lesson.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**One question decides it: had you already decided before you started speaking?**\n\n| | **going to** | **will** |\n|---|---|---|\n| The decision | made **before** now | made **right now** |\n| Feels like | a plan | a reaction |\n| Example | *I'm going to travel in June.* | *I'll take this one.* |\n\n> اسأل نفسك: القرار كان موجود قبل ما أفتح بقي؟ لو أيوه، استخدم **going to**. لو لأ، وقررت في اللحظة،\n> استخدم **will**. سؤال واحد بيحل الموضوع كله، ومش محتاج تحفظ قوائم.\n\n**The classic pair:**\n\n- *(phone rings)* **I'll get it!** → decided this second\n- *I'm going to call the bank tomorrow.* → decided earlier, it is on your list\n\n**Two more places each one owns:**\n\n| Use **going to** for | Use **will** for |\n|---|---|\n| arrangements — *We're going to get married in June* | offers — *I'll help you* |\n| evidence you can see — *Look at those clouds, it's going to rain* | promises — *I won't tell anyone* |\n\n> ⚠️ **حالة تستاهل تتحفظ:** لما تشوف دليل بعينك — سحاب أسود، حد بيقع — بنستخدم **going to**، مش\n> **will**. لأن الدليل موجود قبل ما تتكلم.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Lead with the single question, then the classic *I'll get it* against *I'm going to call*.\n> Add the evidence case last — it is the one exception learners find genuinely surprising.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| I'll get it! | أنا هفتح | decided now |\n| I'm going to… | كنت ناوي… | already planned |\n| Look! It's going to… | بص! هـ… | evidence |\n| I promise | أوعدك | *I promise I'll come.* |\n| I've decided | قررت | *I've decided — I'm going to move.* |\n| Then I'll… | يبقى هـ… | reacting |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Switching between the two changes your rhythm**, and that is worth practising as a pair:\n\n> *going to* is **long** — three beats melted into \"gonna\".\n> *'ll* is **short** — one sound clipped onto the pronoun.\n\n> I'm **gonna** study tonight, so I**'ll** call you tomorrow.\n\nSay that sentence until the long form and the short form sit comfortably beside each other. In real speech\nyou will switch between them constantly.\n\n> جرّب الجملة دي كتير — فيها الشكلين ورا بعض، وده اللي بيحصل في الكلام الطبيعي.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** the mixed sentence above.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** One mixed sentence, many repetitions. Switching is the skill; either form alone is already\n> secure.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — going to or will?**\n1. *(the doorbell rings)* — I ______ get it!\n2. I've bought the tickets. We ______ leave on Friday.\n3. Look at the sky — it ______ rain.\n4. *\"I'm tired.\"* — Sit down, I ______ finish the work.\n5. She ______ start university in September.\n\n**Exercise 2 — why?** For each answer above, write *decided before* or *decided now*.\n\n**Exercise 3 — respond naturally.**\n1. *\"This bag is so heavy.\"* → __________________________\n2. *\"What are your plans for the summer?\"* → __________________________\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** **Ex1:** 1) 'll · 2) 're going to · 3) 's going to · 4) 'll · 5) 's going to.\n> **Ex2:** now · before · before *(evidence)* · now · before. **Ex3:** an offer with *will*, a plan with\n> *going to*.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nHave a short conversation with a partner about the weekend. Use **going to** for what you have already\nplanned, and **will** for anything you decide during the conversation itself.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record the exchange.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** **Success check:** both forms used, and used for the right reason. Ask one learner *why* they\n> chose each — the reasoning matters more than the form.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post two sentences: one plan with *going to*, one offer or prediction with *will*.\n- **Progress check:** you now handle the **past and the future** in English. Two units ago neither was\n  available to you.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- One question: **had you decided before you spoke?**\n- Yes → **going to** · No → **will**.\n- Evidence you can see → **going to**: *It's going to rain.*\n- Offers and promises → **will**: *I'll help.*\n- A wrong choice is almost never misunderstood — keep talking.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 1) I'll · 2) We're going to · 3) it's going to · 4) I'll · 5) She's going to\n- **Ex2:** now · before · before · now · before\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — السؤال الوحيد: القرار اتخد امتى*\n","s1-u4-l05":"# Unit Speaking Task: \"My Plans for Next Month\" (+ Review)\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 4 · **Lesson 5** — *UNIT MILESTONE*\n⏱️ ~35 min · 📹 Recorded + live (assessment-style) · Prerequisite: L01–L04\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit4-future-plans.md` · **S1-U4-L05***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~35 min):** Warm-up 5 · Model 4 · Checklist 4 · Accent clinic 4 · Rehearse 7 · Record 5 · Feedback 2 · Assign —.\n> **Objectives:** talk about plans for 6–8 sentences using both forms · answer a follow-up · record the Unit-4 milestone.\n> **Point out what they can now do:** past and future, both. At the start of this stage they had only the present. That is a genuinely large amount of grammar in four units.\n> **Support:** six sentences with the card. **Stretch:** a prediction plus a reason.\n> **Arabic ~25%** — nearly all English.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك (milestone)\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Talk about your plans in **6–8 sentences**.\n- Use **going to** for what is planned and **will** for what you decide or predict.\n- Answer a question about your plans, live.\n- **Record your Unit-4 milestone.** 🎙️\n\n> إنت دلوقتي بتتكلم عن الماضي والمستقبل. أربع وحدات بس وده اللي اتغير.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن (سلسلة)\nRapid chain: a *going to* plan → a *wh-* question about plans → an offer with *will* → a prediction.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** Fast retrieval across all four lessons.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — النموذج\nA clean plans narration (📹 in the portal).\n\n> Next month **is going to be** busy. I **'m going to start** a new English course on the fifth, and I\n> **'m going to look for** a part-time job at the same time. My brother **'s going to visit** us in the\n> middle of the month, so we **'re going to spend** a few days together. I think it **'ll be** tiring, but\n> I **'ll manage**. I **'m not going to travel** this month — I **'ll save** the money instead.\n> **In the end** I hope it **'ll be** a good month.\n\n**Eight sentences.** Which are *going to*, and which are *will*? Why is each one that way?\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Play once for flow, once for the sort. The *why* is what proves L04 landed.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 The Checklist — تشيك ليست خطتك\n1. ✅ **A time frame** — *next month · in two weeks*\n2. ✅ **Three or more plans** with *going to*\n3. ✅ **One negative** — *I'm not going to…*\n4. ✅ **One prediction** with *will* — *I think it'll be…*\n5. ✅ **A reason** with *because* or *so*\n6. ⭐ **Answer a follow-up question** unprepared\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Board it and hand out the card. Item 4 is the one learners skip — a plan list with no\n> prediction misses half the unit.\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — عيادة النطق\nDrill **your** weakest from this unit:\n\n- **linking** *going to* → *gonna*, with the stress on the main verb\n- **the contraction *'ll*** — audible, not swallowed\n- **won't** with its long vowel, distinct from *want*\n- **the two question tunes** — yes/no rises ↗, *wh-* falls ↘\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare** your weakest one only.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Personalised from your L01–L04 flags. One target each.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — التسجيل (milestone)\nRehearse with the checklist and a partner, then record.\n\nTalk about **your plans for next month** in 6–8 sentences — what you are going to do, one thing you are\n**not** going to do, and one prediction. Then answer **one question** I ask you.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it and keep it.** 👑\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min recording + 2 feedback.** Ask a real follow-up — *Why aren't you going to travel? Who are you\n> going to go with?*\n> **Success check (Unit-4):** 6–8 connected sentences, both future forms used appropriately, one handled\n> follow-up (rubric: Fluency + Accuracy + Pronunciation at A2). Judge the *choice* of form by meaning, not\n> by matching a key.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- **Post your plans.**\n- **Complete the Unit-4 quiz.**\n- **Commit to one plan this month** and say it in English to someone. A plan spoken in English starts to\n  feel real.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر (Unit 4 wrap)\n- **am/is/are + going to + verb** for plans already decided.\n- **will / 'll** for deciding now, offering, and predicting.\n- The test: **had you decided before you spoke?**\n- Evidence you can see → *going to*: *It's going to rain.*\n- You will **hear** *gonna*; say the *'ll*.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — Unit 4 mini-quiz\n1. *I ___ going to travel next week.* → **am**\n2. *(the phone rings)* — I ___ get it! → **'ll**\n3. *Look at the clouds — it ___ rain.* → **'s going to**\n4. *___ you going to study tonight?* → **Are**\n5. *Yes, I ___.* (short answer) → **am**\n6. *I ___ be late, I promise.* (negative) → **won't**\n\n---\n\n## 🎉 Unit 4 complete — الماضي والمستقبل بقوا عندك\nFour units ago you could describe only the present moment. You can now talk about what **happened**, what\nyou **did not** do, what you **are going to** do, and what you think **will** happen.\n\n**Next → Unit 5: Making Comparisons.** Bigger, better, the best — and how to say one thing is not like\nanother. 👑\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — أربع وحدات، وفتحت الزمن كله*\n","s1-u5-l01":"# Comparatives: \"Cairo Is Bigger Than…\"\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 5 · **Lesson 1**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: Unit 4\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit5-making-comparisons.md` · **S1-U5-L01***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 6 · Your Turn 3 · Feedback 1.\n> **Objectives:** form *-er* comparatives · use *than* · make 3 correct comparisons.\n> **The spelling rules are the same three from Unit 2** — doubling, *y→i*, and just-add. Say that; it turns new material into a second use of something already learned.\n> **Watch for:** *than* replaced by *from*, which is direct transfer from Arabic — *أكبر من*. It is the single most predictable error in this unit.\n> **Support:** adjective bank plus the frame. **Stretch:** two comparisons in one sentence with *but*.\n> **Arabic ~35%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Compare two things: *Cairo is **bigger than** Alexandria.*\n- Spell the *-er* form correctly.\n- Make **three real comparisons** of your own. 🎙️\n\n> هتقارن بين حاجتين — وده حوار بنعمله كل يوم بالعربي بدون ما نحس.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nComparing is how people explain choices. *Which phone is better? Which city is cheaper? Why did you pick\nthat one?* You cannot justify a decision in English without it.\n\n> والغلطة اللي بتحصل لكل المتعلمين العرب هنا إننا بنقول *\"أكبر **من**\"*، فبنترجمها لـ *\"bigger **from**\"*.\n\nEnglish uses a completely different word — **than** — and it has no connection to *from*. Watch for this from\nyour very first sentence, because it sets hard and fast.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom Unit 4, say one plan with *going to* and one prediction with *will*.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Keeps the future alive; the unit switches topic but the review queue does not.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nTwo people comparing where they live (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **A:** Cairo is much **bigger than** Alexandria, but Alexandria is **cooler** in summer.\n> **B:** True. And it's **cheaper than** Cairo — the flats are **smaller**, though.\n> **A:** For me Cairo is **busier**, so Alexandria feels **quieter** and **calmer**.\n\n**Notice:** every comparison word ends in **-er**, and when two things are named, **than** joins them.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| bigger than | أكبر من |\n| cheaper than | أرخص من |\n| cooler | أبرد |\n| quieter | أهدى |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second time learners underline every *-er* word and circle each *than*. Note that\n> *than* only appears when the second thing is mentioned.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**Short adjective + *-er* + *than*.**\n\n| Adjective | Comparative |\n|---|---|\n| big | **bigger** |\n| cheap | **cheaper** |\n| old | **older** |\n| fast | **faster** |\n\n> والكلمة اللي بتربط المقارنة هي **than**، ومعناها \"من\" في المقارنة بس. ماتستخدمش **from** أبدًا هنا،\n> وأسهل طريقة تفتكرها إنك تسمعها في جملة كاملة كتير لحد ما ودنك تستغرب لو قلت غيرها.\n\n**The spelling rules are the three you already know from Unit 2:**\n\n| Rule | Example |\n|---|---|\n| C-V-C → double the last letter | bi**g** → bi**gg**er · ho**t** → ho**tt**er |\n| consonant + **y** → **i** | eas**y** → eas**ier** · bus**y** → bus**ier** |\n| everything else | cheap → cheaper |\n\n> نفس قواعد **-ed** بالظبط. يعني إنت اتعلمتها مرة، وبتستخدمها تاني هنا.\n\n**You can drop *than*** when the second thing is already obvious:\n\n- *Alexandria is **cheaper**.* → cheaper than Cairo, which we just mentioned\n\n**Add *much* to make it stronger:** *Cairo is **much** bigger than Tanta.*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Teach the form, then link the spelling explicitly back to Unit 2. Drill *than* five times in\n> isolation — the *from* substitution needs active blocking, not just a warning.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| bigger than | أكبر من | *Cairo is bigger than Tanta.* |\n| cheaper / more expensive | أرخص / أغلى | *It's cheaper than that.* |\n| older / newer | أقدم / أحدث | *My phone is older.* |\n| faster / slower | أسرع / أبطأ | *The metro is faster.* |\n| quieter / busier | أهدى / أزحم | *This street is quieter.* |\n| much | بكتير | *much bigger* |\n| than | من (في المقارنة) | *bigger than* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Target 1 — the /ð/ in *than*.**\nThe tongue tip goes lightly **between the teeth** and the sound is **voiced** — your throat vibrates.\n\n- ✅ *than* — tongue between teeth\n- ❌ *dan* — tongue behind the teeth *(the common substitution)*\n\nSame sound as in *this*, *that*, *the*.\n\n**Target 2 — *than* is weak and unstressed.**\nIt shrinks to /ðən/ and disappears into the sentence. The **adjective** takes the stress:\n\n> Cairo is **BIG**ger than Tanta.\n\n> صوت **/ð/** ده موجود كمان في *this* و*that*، فلو ظبطته هنا بتكسب في كلمات كتير.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"This one is cheaper than that one.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** /ð/ first with the mirror, then the weak form. Learners who stress *than* sound robotic; it\n> should almost vanish.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — make the comparative.**\n1. cheap → ______ 2. big → ______ 3. easy → ______ 4. hot → ______ 5. new → ______\n\n**Exercise 2 — complete with a comparative + *than*.**\n1. A car is ______ a bicycle. (fast)\n2. Winter is ______ summer. (cold)\n3. My flat is ______ yours. (small)\n\n**Exercise 3 — fix the mistake.**\n1. *My brother is taller from me.* → __________________________\n2. *This bag is more cheap than that one.* → __________________________\n3. *Today is more hot than yesterday.* → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 4 — true for you.** Compare two cities you know.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** **Ex1:** cheaper · bigger · easier · hotter · newer.\n> **Ex2:** faster than · colder than · smaller than.\n> **Ex3:** *taller **than** me* · *cheaper* · *hotter* — the last two are the \"more + short adjective\"\n> error, which L02 then contrasts properly.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nMake **three real comparisons**: two cities, two phones, two foods — anything you actually have an opinion\nabout.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record them.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~3 min.** **Success check:** three correct *-er* forms with *than*, /ð/ audible.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post three comparisons about your city, your job, or your phone.\n- **The only fair comparison is you-today against you-on-Day-1.** Not you against a classmate, and not you\n  against a native speaker.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- Short adjective + **-er** + **than**.\n- **than**, never *from*.\n- Spelling: the same three rules as *-ed* — doubling, *y→i*, just add.\n- **much** makes it stronger: *much bigger*.\n- *than* is weak; the adjective takes the stress.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** cheaper · bigger · easier · hotter · newer\n- **Ex2:** 1) faster than · 2) colder than · 3) smaller than\n- **Ex3:** 1) taller **than** me · 2) **cheaper** than · 3) **hotter** than\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — than مش from*\n","s1-u5-l02":"# Longer & Irregular Comparatives: *more expensive · better · worse*\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 5 · **Lesson 2**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L01\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit5-making-comparisons.md` · **S1-U5-L02***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 6 · Your Turn 4 · Feedback —.\n> **Objectives:** use *more* with long adjectives · use *better* and *worse* · choose the right form.\n> **Give them the syllable test, not a list of \"long adjectives\".** One syllable takes *-er*; three or more take *more*. Two-syllable adjectives are genuinely mixed and not worth policing at A2 — accept either.\n> **Watch for the double comparative:** *\"more cheaper\"*, *\"more better\"*. It comes from over-applying the new form and disappears with correction plus reps.\n> **Support:** the two-column sort card. **Stretch:** compare three things in one turn.\n> **Arabic ~35%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Compare with long adjectives: *more expensive · more beautiful*.\n- Use the two that break every rule: *good → **better*** and *bad → **worse***.\n- **Choose** between *-er* and *more* without guessing. 🎙️\n\n> في الدرس اللي فات كل الصفات كانت قصيرة. النهاردة الصفات الطويلة، وليها طريقة تانية.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nMost of the adjectives you actually want in a real opinion are long ones — *interesting*, *comfortable*,\n*important*, *difficult*. Without *more* you are stuck comparing only sizes and prices.\n\nAnd long adjectives are actually **easier**: you change nothing about the word itself. You put *more* in\nfront and stop — no spelling change, no doubling, no letters swapped. The only difficulty is knowing which\nform to pick.\n\n> الصعوبة الوحيدة إنك تعرف تختار.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom L01, make two comparisons with *-er* and *than*.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Listen for *from*. If it has survived, block it again now before adding a second pattern.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nTwo friends choosing a phone (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **A:** This one is **more expensive than** that one, but it's **better**.\n> **B:** Is it? The cheaper one looks **more beautiful** to me.\n> **A:** Maybe, but the battery is **worse**. And this screen is **more comfortable** for reading.\n> **B:** OK, you're right. It's **more important** to have a good battery.\n\n**Notice:** the long adjectives do not change at all — *more* simply goes in front. And *better* and *worse*\nlook nothing like *good* and *bad*.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| more expensive than | أغلى من |\n| more beautiful | أجمل |\n| better | أحسن |\n| worse | أسوأ |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second time learners split what they hear into *-er* forms, *more* forms and\n> irregulars — three columns, discovered rather than given.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**Long adjective → *more* + adjective + *than*. The adjective itself never changes.**\n\n| Adjective | Comparative |\n|---|---|\n| expensive | **more expensive** |\n| beautiful | **more beautiful** |\n| difficult | **more difficult** |\n| important | **more important** |\n\n**How do you know which form to use? Count the syllables.**\n\n| Syllables | Use | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **one** | *-er* | cheap → cheaper |\n| **three or more** | *more* | expensive → more expensive |\n| **two** | either is usually fine | quiet → quieter *or* more quiet |\n\n> عُدّ المقاطع، ماتحفظش قوائم.\n\nTwo-syllable adjectives genuinely accept both forms, so there is nothing to worry about there.\n\n**Two adjectives ignore all of this:**\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| good | **better** |\n| bad | **worse** |\n\n> ⚠️ **الغلطة المتوقعة:** *\"more better\"* أو *\"more cheaper\"*. اختار **واحدة** بس — إما **more** أو\n> **-er**، وعمرك ما تجمع بينهم. والسبب إن الاتنين بيعملوا نفس الشغل، فلو جمعتهم بتبقى بتقول نفس المعنى\n> مرتين في نفس الكلمة، وده اللي بيخلي الجملة تبان غلط على ودن السامع فورًا.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Lead with the syllable test — it is a rule they can apply, unlike \"long adjectives\". Then the\n> two irregulars, then block the double comparative explicitly.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| more expensive | أغلى | *It's more expensive.* |\n| more beautiful | أجمل | *more beautiful than* |\n| more difficult | أصعب | *This is more difficult.* |\n| more important | أهم | *Family is more important.* |\n| more comfortable | أريح | *a more comfortable chair* |\n| better | أحسن | *This one is better.* |\n| worse | أسوأ | *The traffic is worse.* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Target 1 — long adjectives have a fixed stressed syllable. Put it in the wrong place and the word stops\nbeing recognisable.**\n\n| Adjective | Stress |\n|---|---|\n| ex**PEN**sive | on the middle |\n| **BEAU**tiful | on the first |\n| **DIF**ficult | on the first |\n| im**POR**tant | on the middle |\n| **COM**fortable | on the first |\n\n**Target 2 — *more* is unstressed.** It shrinks and hands the stress to the adjective:\n\n> It's more ex**PEN**sive.\n\n> النبرة في الكلمة الطويلة مش اختيارية — لو حطيتها غلط، الكلمة بتتغير وممكن ماتتفهمش.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"It's more expensive, but it's more comfortable.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Clap the stress for each adjective, then have them clap it back. Misplaced stress in long\n> adjectives is a bigger intelligibility problem at A2 than any vowel.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — *-er* or *more*?**\n1. cheap → ______ 2. interesting → ______ 3. old → ______ 4. comfortable → ______ 5. difficult → ______\n\n**Exercise 2 — good or bad?**\n1. My English is ______ than last year. (good)\n2. The traffic today is ______ than yesterday. (bad)\n\n**Exercise 3 — fix the mistake.**\n1. *This book is more better.* → __________________________\n2. *Cairo is more big than Tanta.* → __________________________\n3. *My car is expensiver than yours.* → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 4 — mark the stress.** ex-pen-sive · beau-ti-ful · im-por-tant\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** **Ex1:** cheaper · more interesting · older · more comfortable · more difficult.\n> **Ex2:** better · worse. **Ex3:** *better* · *bigger* · *more expensive*.\n> **Ex4:** exPENsive · BEAUtiful · imPORtant.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nCompare **two things you actually have an opinion about** — two jobs, two cities, two phones — using at\nleast one *more* adjective and either *better* or *worse*.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** **Success check:** correct form choice, one long adjective with correct stress, no double\n> comparative.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post a comparison using **more** and one using **better** or **worse**.\n- **Track your reps.** Consistency is the real \"better\" — a tick for every day you practise.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- One syllable → **-er** · three or more → **more** · two → either.\n- The adjective **never changes** after *more*.\n- **good → better** · **bad → worse**.\n- Never *more better* or *more cheaper*. Pick one.\n- Long adjectives have a **fixed stress** — get it right or the word blurs.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** cheaper · more interesting · older · more comfortable · more difficult\n- **Ex2:** 1) better · 2) worse\n- **Ex3:** 1) *more better* → **better** · 2) **bigger** · 3) **more expensive**\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — عُدّ المقاطع، ماتحفظش قوائم*\n","s1-u5-l03":"# Superlatives: \"The Biggest / The Most Beautiful\"\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 5 · **Lesson 3**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L02\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit5-making-comparisons.md` · **S1-U5-L03***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 6 · Your Turn 4 · Feedback —.\n> **Objectives:** form superlatives · always use *the* · say *the best* and *the worst*.\n> **The whole lesson is one transfer plus one new habit.** They already choose between *-er* and *more*; the same choice governs *-est* and *the most*. The genuinely new thing is the obligatory **the**, which Arabic does not require — so that is where the drilling goes.\n> **Support:** the pattern card. **Stretch:** add *in the world · in my city · I've ever…*\n> **Arabic ~30%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Say which one is **top of the group**: *the biggest · the most beautiful*.\n- Never forget **the**.\n- Use *the best* and *the worst*. 🎙️\n\n> المقارنة بين حاجتين خلصت. النهاردة حاجة واحدة **على كله**.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nSuperlatives are how people share what they love and recommend things. *The best restaurant in Cairo. The\nworst traffic of the week. The most beautiful place I've seen.*\n\nThe difference to feel: a comparative needs **two** things, while a superlative picks **one** out of a whole\ngroup.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom L01 and L02, compare two things — once with *-er*, once with *more*.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Two forms, fast. The superlative is a small step from here and they should feel that.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nSomeone recommending places (📹 in the portal).\n\n> Khan el-Khalili is **the oldest** market in Cairo, and for me it's **the most interesting** place in the\n> city. The food there is **the best** I've tried. **The worst** thing is the traffic — it's **the busiest**\n> area at the weekend. But it's still **the most beautiful** old part of Cairo.\n\n**Notice:** every single one begins with **the**. Not one exception.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| the oldest market | أقدم سوق |\n| the most interesting | الأكثر تشويقًا |\n| the best | الأحسن |\n| the worst | الأسوأ |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second time learners count how many superlatives appear and confirm that *the*\n> precedes all of them. Make the pattern feel absolute.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**The same syllable test as last lesson, plus an obligatory *the*.**\n\n| Adjective | Comparative | Superlative |\n|---|---|---|\n| big | bigger | **the biggest** |\n| cheap | cheaper | **the cheapest** |\n| expensive | more expensive | **the most expensive** |\n| beautiful | more beautiful | **the most beautiful** |\n\n> يعني القاعدة اللي اتعلمتها امبارح هي نفسها: مقطع واحد ياخد **-est**، وتلاتة أو أكتر ياخدوا\n> **the most**.\n\n**The two irregulars again:**\n\n| | Comparative | Superlative |\n|---|---|---|\n| good | better | **the best** |\n| bad | worse | **the worst** |\n\n> ⚠️ **دي الحاجة الجديدة الحقيقية:** كلمة **the** إجبارية قبل الـ superlative، مفيش استثناء. في العربي\n> بنقول \"أحسن مطعم\" من غير أداة، فبنميل ننسى الـ **the** في الإنجليزي. لازم تتقال دايمًا.\n\n**Spelling follows the same three rules:** *big → biggest · easy → easiest · new → newest*\n\n**Useful phrases that pair with it:**\n\n- *in the world · in Cairo · in my family · of the year*\n- *the best food **I've ever** tried*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Frame as transfer, then spend the time on *the*. Drill by prompting an adjective and requiring\n> the full phrase including *the*, every time; accept nothing without it.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| the biggest | الأكبر | *the biggest city* |\n| the cheapest | الأرخص | *the cheapest option* |\n| the most beautiful | الأجمل | *the most beautiful place* |\n| the best | الأحسن | *the best restaurant* |\n| the worst | الأسوأ | *the worst day* |\n| in the world | في العالم | *the best in the world* |\n| I've ever… | في حياتي | *the best I've ever had* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Target 1 — the *-est* ending is /ɪst/.**\nIt is a full extra syllable, and it must be audible:\n\n> big-g**est** · cheap**est** · old**est** — two syllables each\n\nLearners often clip it to *\"bigges\"*, which loses the ending entirely.\n\n**Target 2 — *the* before a vowel sound becomes /ði/.**\n\n> **the** biggest /ðə/ · **the** oldest /ði/ · **the** easiest /ði/\n\n**Target 3 — in *the most*, the stress lands on the adjective**, not on *most*:\n\n> the most ex**PEN**sive\n\n> الـ **-est** مقطع كامل، فلازم تسمعه. ولاحظ إن **the** بتتغير لـ /ði/ قبل حرف علة.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"It's the oldest and the most beautiful part of the city.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** The clipped *-est* is the priority; the /ði/ variation is a bonus that makes them sound\n> noticeably more fluent.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — make the superlative.**\n1. big → ______ 2. expensive → ______ 3. good → ______ 4. easy → ______ 5. bad → ______\n\n**Exercise 2 — complete the sentence.**\n1. Cairo is ______ city in Egypt. (big)\n2. This is ______ restaurant in the area. (good)\n3. Monday is ______ day of the week. (bad)\n\n**Exercise 3 — fix the mistake.**\n1. *It is biggest city in Egypt.* → __________________________\n2. *This is the most cheap hotel.* → __________________________\n3. *She is the goodest student.* → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 4 — true for you.** Name the best thing and the worst thing about your city.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** **Ex1:** the biggest · the most expensive · the best · the easiest · the worst.\n> **Ex2:** the biggest · the best · the worst. **Ex3:** *the biggest* — the missing article is the target\n> error · *the cheapest* · *the best*.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nSay **three superlatives** about your own life: the best place you have visited, the worst weather you\nremember, the most interesting thing you have done.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record them.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** **Success check:** three correct superlatives, **the** present in all three, *-est* audible.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post: the best and the worst thing about where you live.\n- **Name your best English win so far.** Out loud, to someone. Not the biggest thing you cannot do yet.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- One syllable → **the …-est** · three or more → **the most …**\n- **the** is obligatory. Every time, no exceptions.\n- **the best · the worst.**\n- The **-est** is a full syllable — say it.\n- *the* becomes /ði/ before a vowel: *the oldest*.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** the biggest · the most expensive · the best · the easiest · the worst\n- **Ex2:** 1) the biggest · 2) the best · 3) the worst\n- **Ex3:** 1) **the** biggest · 2) the **cheapest** · 3) the **best**\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — الـ the إجبارية، مفيش استثناء*\n","s1-u5-l04":"# *as…as* · *too* · *enough* — Degree & Preference\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 5 · **Lesson 4**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L03\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit5-making-comparisons.md` · **S1-U5-L04***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 6 · Your Turn 4 · Feedback —.\n> **Objectives:** use *(not) as…as* · use *too* and *enough* · give a preference with a reason.\n> **The word-order split is the whole difficulty:** *too* goes **before** the adjective, *enough* goes **after** it. Teach it as a position rule and drill it physically — hands showing before and after — rather than explaining it twice.\n> **Support:** the frames card. **Stretch:** a preference with two reasons.\n> **Arabic ~30%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Say two things are the same: *as expensive as*.\n- Say one falls short: *not as good as*.\n- Use *too* and *enough* — and give a **preference with a reason**. 🎙️\n\n> لحد دلوقتي كنت بتقول إيه أكبر وإيه الأحسن. النهاردة بتقول إيه **زي** إيه، وإيه **زيادة**، وإيه **كفاية**.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nThis is the language of real decisions. *It's too expensive. The room isn't big enough. This one isn't as\ngood as that one, so I prefer the other.*\n\n> و**too** و**enough** مش بيوصفوا الحاجة بس — بيقولوا إنها مناسبة أو لأ.\n\n*too expensive* does not mean expensive. It means expensive **beyond what works for you**.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom L03, name the best and the worst of something.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Quick round; also a last check that *the* has stuck.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nTwo people looking at flats (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **A:** This flat is **as expensive as** the other one, but it's **not as big as** it.\n> **B:** And it's **too far** from the metro. My car isn't **fast enough** for that traffic every morning.\n> **A:** True. The kitchen isn't **big enough** either.\n> **B:** So **I prefer** the first one, **because** it's closer and it has **enough space**.\n\n**Notice the positions:** *too* comes **before** the adjective. *enough* comes **after** it.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| as expensive as | غالي زي |\n| not as big as | مش كبير زي |\n| too far | بعيد أوي |\n| big enough | كبير كفاية |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second time learners mark where *too* and *enough* sit relative to the adjective.\n> The positions are the lesson; let them see the pattern before you state it.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**1) *as … as* — the two things are equal.**\n\n> *This phone is **as good as** that one.* → the same\n\n**2) *not as … as* — the first one is less.**\n\n> *This phone is **not as good as** that one.* → the second is better\n\n> خد بالك: **not as good as** معناها الأولى **أقل**. ودي طريقة مؤدبة تقول إن حاجة أضعف من غير ما تقولها\n> بصراحة.\n\n**3) *too* — more than you want. It goes BEFORE the adjective.**\n\n> *It's **too expensive**.* · *It's **too far**.* → a problem\n\n**4) *enough* — as much as you need. It goes AFTER the adjective.**\n\n> *It's **big enough**.* · *It isn't **fast enough**.* → sufficient, or not\n\n| | Position | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **too** | before the adjective | *too small* |\n| **enough** | after the adjective | *small enough* |\n\n> ⚠️ **دي الغلطة المتوقعة:** *\"enough big\"*. الترتيب معكوس — **too** قبل الصفة، و**enough** بعدها.\n> اتخيل إيدك بتشاور: **too** على الشمال، **enough** على اليمين. وثبّت الحركة دي في دماغك، لأن القاعدة\n> دي مالهاش منطق تحفظه، هي مجرد ترتيب لازم يتعود عليه.\n\n**With a noun, *enough* goes before it:** *enough **money*** · *enough **time***\n\n**5) Give a preference:** *I **prefer** this one **because** it's cheaper.*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Four short blocks, each with two oral examples. Use hands for the *too/enough* positions —\n> a physical cue outperforms a second explanation here.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| as … as | زي | *as big as* |\n| not as … as | مش زي | *not as good as* |\n| too | زيادة عن اللزوم | *too expensive* |\n| enough | كفاية | *big enough* |\n| enough money / time | فلوس / وقت كفاية | *enough time* |\n| I prefer | أنا أفضّل | *I prefer this one.* |\n| because | لأن | *because it's cheaper* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Target 1 — the rhythm of *as … as*.**\nBoth *as* words are **weak** — /əz/. The adjective between them carries everything:\n\n> as **BIG** as · not as **GOOD** as\n\nSay it as one connected run, not three separate words.\n\n**Target 2 — *too* is long, *to* is weak.**\n\n| Word | Sound |\n|---|---|\n| **too** | long /uː/, and stressed — *too expensive* |\n| **to** | weak /tə/, unstressed — *going to work* |\n\n> الفرق بينهم مهم: **too** طويلة وعليها نبرة، و**to** خفيفة ومابتتنطقش كاملة.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"It's not as big as I wanted, and it's too far.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** The weak *as* is what makes the structure sound native. The *too/to* contrast prevents a real\n> ambiguity.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — *too* or *enough*?**\n1. I can't buy it. It's ______ expensive.\n2. The room is small. It isn't big ______.\n3. I can't drink this — it's ______ hot.\n4. Do we have ______ time?\n\n**Exercise 2 — rewrite with *not as … as*.**\n1. *Tanta is smaller than Cairo.* → Tanta ______________________ Cairo.\n2. *This film is worse than that one.* → This film ______________________ that one.\n\n**Exercise 3 — fix the mistake.**\n1. *The bag isn't enough big.* → __________________________\n2. *This is too much expensive.* → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 4 — state a preference.** Two options, your choice, and a reason with *because*.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** **Ex1:** too · enough · too · enough. **Ex2:** *isn't as big as* · *isn't as good as*.\n> **Ex3:** *big enough* · *too expensive*. **Ex4:** insist on the *because* clause.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nCompare **two real options** — two phones, two jobs, two places to live. Use at least **two** of today's\nstructures, then say which you prefer and **why**.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** **Success check:** two structures used correctly with the right word order, plus a preference\n> with a stated reason.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post a preference between two things, with a reason.\n- **\"Good enough today\" beats \"perfect never.\"** Send the voice note even when it is imperfect — that is the\n  rep that counts.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- **as … as** = equal · **not as … as** = less.\n- **too** goes **before** the adjective · **enough** goes **after** it.\n- With a noun: **enough money**, **enough time**.\n- **I prefer … because …** is the sentence that wins arguments politely.\n- Both *as* words are weak; **too** is long, **to** is not.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 1) too · 2) enough · 3) too · 4) enough\n- **Ex2:** 1) isn't as big as · 2) isn't as good as\n- **Ex3:** 1) *big enough* · 2) *too expensive*\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — too قبل الصفة، enough بعدها*\n","s1-u5-l05":"# Unit Speaking Task: \"Compare Two Cities\" (+ Review)\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 5 · **Lesson 5** — *UNIT MILESTONE*\n⏱️ ~35 min · 📹 Recorded + live (assessment-style) · Prerequisite: L01–L04\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit5-making-comparisons.md` · **S1-U5-L05***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~35 min):** Warm-up 5 · Model 4 · Checklist 4 · Accent clinic 4 · Rehearse 7 · Record 5 · Feedback 2 · Assign —.\n> **Objectives:** compare across 6–8 sentences with correct forms · justify a preference · record the milestone.\n> **The reasoned preference is what the rubric rewards**, more than form accuracy. A learner who says *\"I prefer Alexandria because it's quieter and the sea is close\"* is performing at A2 even with a wrong comparative in it.\n> **Support:** six sentences with the card. **Stretch:** three items compared, or a concession with *but*.\n> **Arabic ~25%** — nearly all English.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك (milestone)\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Compare two things across **6–8 sentences**.\n- Mix *-er*, *more*, superlatives and *as…as*.\n- **Justify a preference** with real reasons.\n- **Record your Unit-5 milestone.** 🎙️\n\n> النهاردة تجمع كل أدوات المقارنة في رأي واحد متكامل.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن (سلسلة)\nRapid chain: an *-er* comparison → a *more* comparison → a superlative → *not as … as*.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** Fast retrieval across the unit's four patterns.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — النموذج\nA clean comparison (📹 in the portal).\n\n> I lived in Cairo for ten years, and now I live in Alexandria. Cairo is much **bigger** and **busier**,\n> and it's **the best** city for work — there are **more** opportunities. But it's **more expensive**, and\n> the traffic is **the worst** in Egypt. Alexandria is **quieter** and the air is **better**, although it\n> isn't **as busy as** Cairo, so there is less work. For me Alexandria is **more comfortable**, and it's\n> **not as stressful as** Cairo. **I prefer** Alexandria **because** I sleep better and the sea is close.\n\n**Eight sentences.** Find one of each: an *-er* form, a *more* form, a superlative, an *as…as*.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Play once for flow, once for the hunt. Note how the model **concedes** a point with *but* and\n> *although* — that balance is what makes an opinion sound mature.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 The Checklist — تشيك ليست المقارنة\n1. ✅ **Name the two things** and where they stand\n2. ✅ **Two *-er* comparisons**\n3. ✅ **One *more* comparison**\n4. ✅ **One superlative** with *the*\n5. ✅ **A concession** — *but · although*\n6. ✅ **A preference with a reason** — *I prefer … because …*\n7. ⭐ ***not as … as*** somewhere\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Board it and hand out the card. Item 5 is the one that lifts a list into an argument; point at\n> it.\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — عيادة النطق\nDrill **your** weakest from this unit:\n\n- **/ð/ in *than*** — tongue between the teeth, and the word stays weak\n- **long-adjective stress** — ex**PEN**sive · **BEAU**tiful · im**POR**tant\n- **the *-est* ending** — a full syllable, not clipped\n- **weak *as … as*** — the adjective carries it\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare** your weakest one only.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Personalised from your L01–L04 flags. One target each.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — التسجيل (milestone)\nRehearse with the checklist and a partner, then record.\n\n**Compare two cities** — or two jobs, two phones, two anything you have a real opinion about — in 6–8\nsentences, and finish by saying which you prefer and **why**.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it and keep it.** 👑\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min recording + 2 feedback.** Ask one follow-up: *So would you move back?*\n> **Success check (Unit-5):** 6–8 connected sentences, comparative and superlative forms mostly correct,\n> and a preference with genuine reasons (rubric: Fluency + Accuracy + Pronunciation at A2). Weight the\n> reasoning above the forms.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- **Post your comparison.**\n- **Complete the Unit-5 quiz.**\n- **Compare your English now to Stage 0.** Not to a classmate. Play your Day-1 recording — that is the only\n  comparison that means anything.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر (Unit 5 wrap)\n- One syllable → **-er / the -est** · three or more → **more / the most**.\n- **than**, never *from*. **the** before every superlative.\n- **good → better → the best** · **bad → worse → the worst**.\n- **too** before the adjective · **enough** after it.\n- **I prefer … because …** — the reason is what makes it an opinion.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — Unit 5 mini-quiz\n1. *Cairo is ___ than Tanta.* (big) → **bigger**\n2. *This phone is ___ than that one.* (expensive) → **more expensive**\n3. *It's ___ restaurant in the city.* (good) → **the best**\n4. *My flat isn't ___ big ___ yours.* → **as … as**\n5. *I can't buy it — it's ___ expensive.* → **too**\n6. *The room isn't big ___.* → **enough**\n\n---\n\n## 🎉 Unit 5 complete — بقيت تقارن وتفضّل\nYou can now say what is bigger, better, the best, not as good, too expensive or big enough — and, most\nimportantly, **why you prefer one over the other**. That is opinion, not just description.\n\n**Next → Unit 6: Food, Quantity & Restaurants.** Ordering, asking for amounts, and handling a real\nrestaurant in English. 👑\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — الرأي بيبدأ من السبب*\n","s1-u6-l01":"# Countable vs Uncountable; *some / any*\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 6 · **Lesson 1**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: Unit 5\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit6-food-quantity-restaurants.md` · **S1-U6-L01***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 6 · Your Turn 3 · Feedback 1.\n> **Objectives:** sort food into countable and uncountable · use *some* and *any* correctly.\n> **Teach it as \"can you count them one by one?\"** not as a grammatical category. *Three rices* is wrong for the same reason *three waters* is — you count the container, not the substance.\n> **The Arabic transfer to expect:** Arabic pluralises many of these freely, so *\"I bought some breads\"* and *\"many informations\"* are structural, not careless.\n> **Support:** the sort card. **Stretch:** name ten fridge items in the right column.\n> **Arabic ~35%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Tell which food words take a plural and which never do.\n- Use **some** in statements and **any** in questions and negatives.\n- Talk about what is in your kitchen right now. 🎙️\n\n> في الإنجليزي فيه كلمات أكل بتتعد وكلمات مابتتعدش، والفرق ده بيغيّر الجملة كلها.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nGet this wrong and every sentence about shopping, cooking or ordering sounds off. Get it right and a whole\nunit of quantity language becomes straightforward.\n\n> والمشكلة إن العربي بيجمع كلمات كتير الإنجليزي مابيجمعهاش، زي \"خبزات\" و\"معلومات\".\n\nSo we translate directly and produce *breads* and *informations*, which are wrong. This is not carelessness —\nit is a real difference between the two languages, and it only has to be learned once.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom Unit 5, compare two foods you like.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Bridges the units and keeps comparatives in circulation.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nSomeone checking the kitchen before shopping (📹 in the portal).\n\n> We have **some rice** and **some bread**, but we don't have **any milk**.\n> There are **some eggs** — four, I think — and **some tomatoes**.\n> Do we have **any sugar**? … No, there isn't **any**. And we need **some water**.\n\n**Notice:** *eggs* and *tomatoes* have an **-s**. *rice*, *bread*, *milk*, *sugar* and *water* never do.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| some rice | شوية رز |\n| some eggs | شوية بيض |\n| We don't have any milk. | مفيش لبن |\n| Do we have any sugar? | فيه سكر؟ |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second time learners sort what they heard into two columns. Let the pattern emerge\n> before you name it.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**The test: can you count them one by one?**\n\n| **Countable** — you can count them | **Uncountable** — you cannot |\n|---|---|\n| an egg → two eggs | rice |\n| an apple → three apples | bread |\n| a tomato → tomatoes | water · milk · sugar |\n\n> اسأل نفسك: أقدر أعدها واحدة واحدة؟ لو أيوه، تبقى معدودة وبتتجمع. لو لأ، تفضل زي ما هي. والفكرة إن\n> الحاجات غير المعدودة بنتعامل معاها كمادة واحدة، مش كقطع منفصلة، وعشان كده مالهاش جمع.\n\n**Uncountable words follow three fixed habits:**\n\n1. **No plural.** *rice*, never *rices*.\n2. **No *a* or *an*.** Not *a bread* — say *some bread*.\n3. **They take a singular verb.** *The water **is** cold.*\n\n**To count them, count the container instead:**\n\n> *two **bottles of** water* · *three **pieces of** bread* · *a **kilo of** rice*\n\n**Now *some* and *any*:**\n\n| | Use | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| ✅ statements | **some** | *We have some bread.* |\n| ❌ negatives | **any** | *We don't have any bread.* |\n| ❓ questions | **any** | *Do we have any bread?* |\n\n> ⚠️ **استثناء تستاهل تعرفه:** لما تطلب حاجة أو تعرض حاجة، بنستخدم **some** في السؤال:\n> *\"Would you like **some** tea?\"* — لأن ده عرض، مش سؤال عن وجود الحاجة.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Lead with the counting test, then the three habits, then *some/any*. The offer exception is\n> worth including — learners meet it constantly and it otherwise looks like a broken rule.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| rice / bread | رز / عيش | *some bread* |\n| water / milk / sugar | ماية / لبن / سكر | *any milk* |\n| eggs / apples / tomatoes | بيض / تفاح / طماطم | *some eggs* |\n| vegetables / fruit | خضار / فاكهة | *some vegetables* |\n| a bottle of | إزازة | *a bottle of water* |\n| a piece of | حتة | *a piece of bread* |\n| a kilo of | كيلو | *a kilo of rice* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Target — consonant clusters in food words, with no vowel pushed inside.**\n\nArabic does not usually allow these clusters, so a vowel tends to slip in:\n\n| Word | ✅ | ❌ |\n|---|---|---|\n| bread | *bred* — one syllable | \"be-read\" |\n| fruit | *froot* — one syllable | \"fe-root\" |\n| vegetables | **VEJ**-ta-bulz — three | \"ve-ge-ta-bles\" |\n| milk | *milk* — one | \"mi-lik\" |\n\n> الحرفين الساكنين في أول الكلمة بيتنطقوا ورا بعض على طول، من غير حركة بينهم.\n\nNote that *vegetables* is **three** syllables, not four — the second *e* disappears entirely.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"I bought some bread, fruit and vegetables.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** *vegetables* is the highest-value fix — learners almost universally give it four syllables.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — countable or uncountable?**\n> rice · egg · water · apple · bread · tomato · sugar · milk\n\n**Exercise 2 — *some* or *any*?**\n1. We have ______ rice at home.\n2. I don't want ______ sugar in my tea.\n3. Do you have ______ eggs?\n4. Would you like ______ coffee?\n\n**Exercise 3 — fix the mistake.**\n1. *I bought some breads.* → __________________________\n2. *She drank a water.* → __________________________\n3. *There are many informations.* → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 4 — your kitchen.** Three things you have, one thing you don't.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** **Ex1:** countable — egg, apple, tomato · uncountable — rice, water, bread, sugar, milk.\n> **Ex2:** some · any · any · some *(the offer)*. **Ex3:** *some bread* · *some water / a glass of water* ·\n> *a lot of information*.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nSay what is in your kitchen: **three things you have** with *some*, and **one thing you don't** with *any*.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~3 min.** **Success check:** correct *some/any*, no plural on uncountables, clusters clean.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post four sentences about your fridge, using *some* and *any*.\n- **Real-world rep:** tonight, name everything in your fridge in English, out loud. Nobody has to hear it.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- Can you count them one by one? Then they take a plural.\n- Uncountables: **no plural · no *a/an* · singular verb**.\n- Count the **container**: *a bottle of water*.\n- **some** in statements · **any** in negatives and questions · **some** in offers.\n- *bread · fruit · milk* are one syllable. *vegetables* is three.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** countable — egg, apple, tomato · uncountable — rice, water, bread, sugar, milk\n- **Ex2:** 1) some · 2) any · 3) any · 4) some\n- **Ex3:** 1) some **bread** · 2) some **water** / a glass of water · 3) a lot of **information**\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — اسأل نفسك: أقدر أعدها واحدة واحدة*\n","s1-u6-l02":"# *much · many · a lot of · a few · a little*\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 6 · **Lesson 2**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L01\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit6-food-quantity-restaurants.md` · **S1-U6-L02***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 6 · Your Turn 4 · Feedback —.\n> **Objectives:** choose the right quantifier · use *a lot of* in statements · use *a few* and *a little*.\n> **The shortcut worth giving them:** *a lot of* works with **everything**. Teach that first so they always have a safe option, then refine. A learner who never risks a quantity sentence learns nothing.\n> **Watch for:** *much* used in statements — *\"I have much work\"*. It is grammatically odd rather than wrong-meaning, and *a lot of* fixes it instantly.\n> **Support:** the two-column card. **Stretch:** contrast *a few* against *few*.\n> **Arabic ~30%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Say **how much** of something there is, without a number.\n- Use *a lot of* — which works with everything.\n- Tell *a few* from *a little*. 🎙️\n\n> بعد ما عرفت المعدود وغير المعدود، دلوقتي تقول الكمية.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nMost of the time you do not give an exact number. You say *a lot*, *a few*, *not much*. That is how quantity\nactually gets discussed.\n\n> والمفتاح هنا إن اختيار الكلمة بيعتمد على اللي اتعلمته في الدرس اللي فات: معدود ولا غير معدود.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom L01, name three countable and three uncountable foods.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** This lesson stands entirely on that sort, so confirm it holds before adding anything.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nTwo people planning a dinner (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **A:** How is the kitchen? Do we have **a lot of** food?\n> **B:** We have **a lot of** rice and **a few** eggs, but there isn't **much** milk.\n> **A:** How **many** people are coming?\n> **B:** Six. So we need **a lot of** bread and **a little** more sugar for the tea.\n> **A:** OK. There aren't **many** tomatoes either — I'll buy some.\n\n**Notice:** *many* and *a few* go with things you can count. *much* and *a little* go with things you cannot.\n*a lot of* goes with both.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| a lot of rice | رز كتير |\n| a few eggs | شوية بيض |\n| not much milk | لبن مش كتير |\n| a little sugar | شوية سكر |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second time learners note which quantifier attaches to which kind of noun — the\n> table below should confirm what they already spotted.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**Start with the safe one: *a lot of* works with everything.**\n\n> *a lot of rice* · *a lot of eggs* · *a lot of work* · *a lot of people*\n\n**Then the pairs that split:**\n\n| | Countable | Uncountable |\n|---|---|---|\n| a large amount | **many** | **much** |\n| a small amount | **a few** | **a little** |\n\n- ***many** eggs · **a few** eggs*\n- ***much** milk · **a little** milk*\n\n> يعني كل كلمة ليها توأم: **many/much**، و**a few / a little**. الاختيار بينهم بيرجع للمعدود وغير المعدود.\n\n**One habit that matters:** *much* and *many* live mostly in **questions and negatives**. In a positive\nstatement, use *a lot of*:\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| ✅ *I have **a lot of** work.* | ❌ *I have much work.* |\n| ✅ *Do you have **much** work?* | ✅ *I don't have **much** work.* |\n\n> ⚠️ الغلطة المتوقعة إنك تقول *\"I have much work\"*. مش غلط في المعنى، بس مش كده اللي بيتقال. استخدم\n> **a lot of** في الإثبات وخلاص.\n\n**And *a few* is not *few*:**\n\n- *I have **a few** friends.* → some, and that is fine\n- *I have **few** friends.* → not many, and that is a complaint\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Give *a lot of* first as the always-safe option — it removes the fear of the choice. Then the\n> two pairs, then the statement habit. The *a few / few* contrast is a stretch item; include it only if the\n> group is comfortable.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| a lot of | كتير | *a lot of people* |\n| many | كتير (معدود) | *many eggs* |\n| much | كتير (غير معدود) | *not much time* |\n| a few | شوية (معدود) | *a few days* |\n| a little | شوية (غير معدود) | *a little sugar* |\n| not much / not many | مش كتير | *There isn't much.* |\n| more | أكتر | *a little more* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Target 1 — *of* almost disappears.**\nIn *a lot of* it reduces to /əv/, and often to little more than a /v/:\n\n> *a lot of rice* → **\"a-LOT-ə-rice\"**\n\nSay all three words fully and you sound like you are reading. The stress belongs on **lot**.\n\n**Target 2 — *a few* and *a little* have their own rhythm.**\nBoth are three quick beats with the stress in the middle:\n\n> a **FEW** days · a **LIT**-tle sugar\n\n> كلمة **of** بتخف جدًا في الكلام، والنبرة بتقع على **lot**.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"There's a lot of rice but only a little sugar.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** The reduced *of* is what makes the phrase sound natural; it is a quick, high-return fix.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — *much* or *many*?**\n1. How ______ eggs do we need?\n2. There isn't ______ water in the bottle.\n3. I don't have ______ friends here.\n4. We don't have ______ time.\n\n**Exercise 2 — *a few* or *a little*?**\n1. I need ______ sugar in my coffee.\n2. She stayed for ______ days.\n3. Can I have ______ more rice?\n4. I have ______ questions.\n\n**Exercise 3 — fix the mistake.**\n1. *I have much homework today.* → __________________________\n2. *There are much people here.* → __________________________\n3. *Can I have a few water?* → __________________________\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** **Ex1:** many · much · many · much. **Ex2:** a little · a few · a little · a few.\n> **Ex3:** *a lot of homework* · *a lot of people / many people* · *a little water*.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nDescribe your week using quantity words: something you have **a lot of**, something you have **a few** of,\nand something there **isn't much** of.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** **Success check:** three quantifiers matched correctly to countable or uncountable nouns,\n> with the reduced *of* audible.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post three sentences about quantities in your life — work, time, friends, money.\n- **A little every day beats a lot once.** That is true of rice, and it is true of practice.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- **a lot of** works with everything — your safe choice.\n- Countable: **many · a few** · Uncountable: **much · a little**.\n- **much / many** live in questions and negatives; use **a lot of** in statements.\n- *a few* = some, and fine. *few* = not enough.\n- **of** shrinks to /əv/; the stress sits on **lot**.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 1) many · 2) much · 3) many · 4) much\n- **Ex2:** 1) a little · 2) a few · 3) a little · 4) a few\n- **Ex3:** 1) *a lot of homework* · 2) *a lot of people* · 3) *a little water*\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — a lot of بتنفع مع كل حاجة*\n","s1-u6-l03":"# *How Much? / How Many?* — Quantities & Containers\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 6 · **Lesson 3**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L02\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit6-food-quantity-restaurants.md` · **S1-U6-L03***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 6 · Your Turn 4 · Feedback —.\n> **Objectives:** ask *How much* and *How many* correctly · use container words · ask and understand a price.\n> **The double life of *How much?* is the useful insight:** it asks about an uncountable amount **and** about price. Learners find that reassuring rather than confusing once it is named — one phrase, two jobs.\n> **Support:** container flashcards. **Stretch:** a full market exchange with a price negotiation.\n> **Arabic ~30%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Ask **How much** and **How many** with the right noun.\n- Use container words: *a bottle · a cup · a piece · a kilo*.\n- Ask a price and **understand the answer**. 🎙️\n\n> النهاردة تسأل عن الكمية والسعر — وده أهم سؤال في أي محل.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nAsking a price is probably the most useful transaction sentence in any language. And containers are how you\nput a number on things you cannot count.\n\nAnd notice that **How much?** does two jobs: it asks about an uncountable amount, and it asks a price. One\nphrase, two uses.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom L02, use *a lot of*, *a few* and *a little* in three sentences.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Fast round; the quantifiers feed straight into today's questions.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nAt a small grocery (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **Customer:** **How much** is a **kilo of** tomatoes?\n> **Seller:** Twenty-five pounds.\n> **Customer:** OK. And **how many** eggs are in the box?\n> **Seller:** Thirty. **How much** rice do you want?\n> **Customer:** Two **kilos**, please. And a **bottle of** water and three **pieces of** bread.\n> **Seller:** That's ninety pounds altogether.\n\n**Notice:** *How much* appears twice — once for a **price** and once for an **uncountable amount**.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| How much is a kilo? | الكيلو بكام؟ |\n| How many eggs? | كام بيضة؟ |\n| a bottle of water | إزازة ماية |\n| altogether | الإجمالي |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second time learners identify which *How much* is which. Also drill *altogether* —\n> it appears in every real transaction.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**The choice follows the noun, exactly as in L02.**\n\n| Question | Use with | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **How many** | countable | *How many eggs?* |\n| **How much** | uncountable | *How much rice?* |\n| **How much** | **price** | *How much is it?* |\n\n> يعني نفس القاعدة: معدود ياخد **many**، وغير معدود ياخد **much**. والزيادة الجديدة إن السعر كمان بياخد\n> **How much**.\n\n**Container words let you count the uncountable:**\n\n| Container | Goes with |\n|---|---|\n| a **bottle** of | water · milk · oil |\n| a **cup / glass** of | tea · coffee · water |\n| a **piece** of | bread · cake · cheese |\n| a **kilo** of | rice · meat · tomatoes |\n| a **packet** of | sugar · biscuits |\n\n**Note the plural sits on the container, not the food:** *two **bottles** of water*, never *two waters*.\n\n> ⚠️ **خلي بالك:** الجمع بيتحط على العلبة أو الوحدة، مش على الأكل نفسه. بنقول *three pieces of bread*،\n> مابنقولش *three breads*. والسبب إن اللي بيتعد هو الحتة أو الإزازة، مش المادة نفسها.\n\n**Asking a price, three ways:**\n\n- *How much is it?* · *How much are they?* · *How much does it cost?*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Frame as transfer, then the containers, then the price forms. The plural-on-the-container rule\n> is the one worth drilling — it is where the L01 uncountable rule actually gets used.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| How much is it? | بكام؟ | *How much is it?* |\n| How many…? | كام…؟ | *How many eggs?* |\n| a bottle of | إزازة | *a bottle of water* |\n| a cup / glass of | كوباية | *a cup of tea* |\n| a piece of | حتة | *a piece of bread* |\n| a kilo of | كيلو | *two kilos of rice* |\n| altogether | الإجمالي | *That's 90 altogether.* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Target 1 — numbers have to be unmistakable, because money depends on them.**\nThe pairs that cause real trouble:\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| thir**TEEN** vs **THIR**ty | stress at the end vs the beginning |\n| four**TEEN** vs **FOUR**ty | same |\n| fif**TEEN** vs **FIF**ty | same |\n\nThe **-teen** numbers stress the **end**. The **-ty** numbers stress the **beginning**. That stress is the\nonly difference, and getting it wrong changes the price.\n\n> النبرة هي الفرق الوحيد بين **13** و**30**. لو حطيتها غلط، السعر بيتغير.\n\n**Target 2 — the question tune.** *How much is it?* falls at the end ↘, like every *wh-* question.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"How much is thirteen kilos? … No, thirty.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** The teen/ty contrast is the priority — it has real consequences and learners are usually\n> unaware they are producing it ambiguously.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — *How much* or *How many*?**\n1. ______ sugar do you want?\n2. ______ people are coming?\n3. ______ is this bag?\n4. ______ bottles do we need?\n\n**Exercise 2 — add the container.**\n1. two ______ of water 2. a ______ of tea 3. three ______ of bread 4. a ______ of rice\n\n**Exercise 3 — fix the mistake.**\n1. *How many rice do you want?* → __________________________\n2. *Can I have three breads?* → __________________________\n3. *How much eggs are there?* → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 4 — say these clearly.** 13 · 30 · 14 · 40 · 15 · 50\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** **Ex1:** How much · How many · How much · How many.\n> **Ex2:** bottles · cup · pieces · kilo. **Ex3:** *How much rice* · *three pieces of bread* ·\n> *How many eggs*. **Ex4:** check the stress placement on every one.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nRole-play a small shop with a partner. Ask about **two prices** and **two quantities**, using at least two\ncontainer words.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record one exchange.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** **Success check:** correct *How much/How many*, two containers used, numbers unambiguous.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post a short shop dialogue you write yourself, with a price in it.\n- **Use it in the wild:** ask a price in English this week. A shop, a café, anywhere. One sentence is enough.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- **How many** + countable · **How much** + uncountable · **How much** for price.\n- Containers let you count: *a bottle of · a piece of · a kilo of*.\n- The plural goes on the **container**: *two bottles of water*.\n- **thirTEEN** against **THIRty** — the stress is the price.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 1) How much · 2) How many · 3) How much · 4) How many\n- **Ex2:** 1) bottles · 2) cup · 3) pieces · 4) kilo\n- **Ex3:** 1) How **much** rice · 2) three **pieces of** bread · 3) How **many** eggs\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — النبرة هي اللي بتحدد السعر*\n","s1-u6-l04":"# At the Restaurant: Ordering Politely\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 6 · **Lesson 4**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L03\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit6-food-quantity-restaurants.md` · **S1-U6-L04***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 6 · Your Turn 4 · Feedback —.\n> **Objectives:** order with *Could I have* and *I'll have* · ask for the menu and the bill · sound polite.\n> **Politeness in English is carried by GRAMMAR and INTONATION, not by extra words.** *Give me a coffee* is grammatically perfect and socially rude. Learners rarely realise they are being blunt, so name it directly — it protects them in real situations.\n> **Support:** the phrase card to read from. **Stretch:** handle a problem — a wrong order, a missing item.\n> **Arabic ~30%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Order food politely: *Could I have the chicken, please?*\n- Ask for the menu, and ask for the bill.\n- Handle the whole visit from sitting down to paying. 🎙️\n\n> النهاردة موقف كامل من أوله لآخره — وده أول موقف حياتي بتقدر تديره بالإنجليزي لوحدك.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nOrdering a meal is a complete, self-contained win. It has a beginning, an end, and a fixed set of sentences\n— which means you can be genuinely good at it after one lesson.\n\n> والأدب في الإنجليزي مش بيتعمل بكلمات زيادة، بيتعمل بالتركيب والنبرة.\n\n*\"Give me a coffee\"* is one hundred per cent correct grammar and lands badly. Learners usually have no idea\nthey are coming across as blunt, which is exactly why it is worth saying out loud.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom L03, ask a partner two prices.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Price questions reappear in the role-play, so keep them warm.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nA full restaurant visit (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **Waiter:** Good evening. A table for two?\n> **Customer:** Yes, please. **Could we see** the menu?\n> **Waiter:** Of course. … Are you ready to order?\n> **Customer:** Yes. **Could I have** the grilled chicken, please? And **I'll have** a bottle of water.\n> **Waiter:** Anything else?\n> **Customer:** **Could we have** some bread as well, please?\n> **Waiter:** Certainly.\n> *(later)*\n> **Customer:** That was lovely, thank you. **Could we have the bill**, please?\n\n**Notice:** almost every request begins with **Could I / Could we**, and almost every one ends with\n**please**.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| Could we see the menu? | ممكن نشوف المنيو؟ |\n| Could I have the chicken? | ممكن آخد الفراخ؟ |\n| Anything else? | حاجة تانية؟ |\n| Could we have the bill? | ممكن الحساب؟ |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second time learners count the *Could* requests. Then say a blunt version aloud —\n> *\"Give me chicken\"* — and let them hear the difference for themselves.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**The polite request pattern: *Could I have …, please?***\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Could I have** | the chicken, please? |\n| **Could we have** | the bill, please? |\n| **Could I see** | the menu, please? |\n\n> **Could** هي مفتاح الأدب في الإنجليزي كله، مش في المطعم بس. استخدمها في أي طلب — في الشغل، في محل،\n> مع حد ماتعرفهوش. وهي بتشتغل لأنها بتحوّل الأمر لسؤال، فبتسيب للطرف التاني مساحة يرد.\n\n**Two other ways to order, both natural:**\n\n| Phrase | Feel |\n|---|---|\n| ***I'll have** the fish.* | deciding now — this is *will* from Unit 4 |\n| ***I'd like** the soup.* | slightly more formal, always safe |\n\n**The four things that make a request polite:**\n\n1. **Could**, not *give me*\n2. **please** — at the end, or after *Could I*\n3. **rising intonation** ↗\n4. **thank you** afterwards\n\n> ⚠️ **الغلطة الشائعة:** *\"I want the chicken\"*. مش غلط قواعديًا، بس بتوصل مثل أمر. استخدم **Could I\n> have** أو **I'll have**.\n\n**The visit, in order:** *A table for two → Could we see the menu? → Could I have…? → Anything else? →\nCould we have the bill?*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Teach *Could I have* as the workhorse, then the two alternatives. Make the four politeness\n> markers explicit — learners often have the words but flat intonation, which undoes them.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Could I have…? | ممكن آخد…؟ | *Could I have the fish?* |\n| I'll have… | هاخد… | *I'll have tea.* |\n| I'd like… | عايز… (بأدب) | *I'd like the soup.* |\n| the menu | المنيو | *Could we see the menu?* |\n| the bill | الحساب | *the bill, please* |\n| Anything else? | حاجة تانية؟ | waiter's question |\n| for two | لاتنين | *A table for two.* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Target 1 — the request must RISE at the end.** This is where politeness actually lives:\n\n> Could I have the chicken, please? ↗\n\nSay the same words with a **falling** tone and it becomes an instruction. The words are identical; only the\ntune changed.\n\n**Target 2 — *Could* has a silent *l*.**\n\n> *could* = /kʊd/ — like *good*, with a **k**. Not \"kold\".\n\n**Target 3 — *please* is one syllable** with a long /iː/ and a soft /z/ at the end: *pleaz*.\n\n> النبرة الصاعدة هي اللي بتخلي الطلب مؤدب. نفس الكلمات بنبرة نازلة بتبقى أمر.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"Could I have the bill, please?\"* — once rising, once falling. Hear the\n> difference in yourself.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** The rise-versus-fall demonstration is the single most valuable thing in this lesson. Have\n> every learner produce both and identify which sounds rude.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — make it polite.**\n1. *Give me a coffee.* → __________________________\n2. *I want the menu.* → __________________________\n3. *Bring the bill.* → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 2 — put the visit in order.** Number 1–5.\n- ______ Could we have the bill, please?\n- ______ A table for two, please.\n- ______ Could I have the chicken, please?\n- ______ Could we see the menu?\n- ______ Anything else? — No, thank you.\n\n**Exercise 3 — complete the order.** You want soup, grilled fish and a bottle of water.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** **Ex1:** *Could I have a coffee, please?* · *Could I see the menu, please?* ·\n> *Could we have the bill, please?* **Ex2:** 5 · 1 · 3 · 2 · 4.\n> **Ex3:** check *please* is present and the tone rises.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nRole-play with a partner: one waiter, one customer. Run the **whole visit** — menu, order, one extra\nrequest, then the bill.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record your order.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** **Success check:** two polite requests with *Could*, *please* present, rising intonation\n> audible.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post your restaurant order as a voice note — and listen back for the rise.\n- **Confidence:** ordering in English is a small, doable, real win. Take it the next chance you get.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- **Could I have …, please?** is the polite workhorse — everywhere, not just restaurants.\n- **I'll have** = deciding now · **I'd like** = always safe.\n- *I want* sounds like an order. Avoid it.\n- Politeness lives in the **rising tone** as much as in the words.\n- **could** rhymes with *good*; the *l* is silent.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 1) Could I have a coffee, please? · 2) Could I see the menu, please? · 3) Could we have the bill, please?\n- **Ex2:** 5 · 1 · 3 · 2 · 4\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — الأدب في النبرة زي ما هو في الكلمات*\n","s1-u6-l05":"# Unit Speaking Task: Role-Play Ordering a Meal (+ Review)\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 6 · **Lesson 5** — *UNIT MILESTONE*\n⏱️ ~35 min · 📹 Recorded + live (assessment-style) · Prerequisite: L01–L04\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit6-food-quantity-restaurants.md` · **S1-U6-L05***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~35 min):** Warm-up 5 · Model 4 · Checklist 4 · Accent clinic 4 · Rehearse 7 · Record 5 · Feedback 2 · Assign —.\n> **Objectives:** run a full restaurant exchange · handle quantities and a price · record the milestone.\n> **This is the first milestone that is a real-world TRANSACTION rather than a description.** Frame it that way: they are not describing a restaurant, they are handling one. That reframing is what makes learners take it outside the classroom.\n> **Support:** the phrase card, and let them be the customer. **Stretch:** handle a problem — a wrong dish, a missing item.\n> **Arabic ~25%** — nearly all English.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك (milestone)\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Run a **complete restaurant conversation**, start to finish.\n- Order politely, ask a quantity, ask a price.\n- **Record your Unit-6 milestone.** 🎙️\n\n> ده أول موقف حقيقي بتديره لوحدك بالإنجليزي من أول ما تقعد لحد ما تدفع.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن (سلسلة)\nRapid chain: *some/any* → a quantifier → *How much* or *How many* → a polite request.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** Fast retrieval across the four lessons.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — النموذج\nA complete visit, with a small complication (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **Waiter:** Good evening. **How many** people?\n> **Customer:** Three, please. Could we sit near the window?\n> **Waiter:** Of course. Here's the menu.\n> **Customer:** Thank you. … **Could I have** the grilled chicken, please? And **could we have**\n> **some** bread for the table?\n> **Waiter:** Certainly. Anything to drink?\n> **Customer:** Two **bottles of** water, please. **How much** is the fresh juice?\n> **Waiter:** Thirty pounds.\n> **Customer:** Then **I'll have** one as well.\n> *(later)*\n> **Customer:** Excuse me — I didn't order this. **Could we have** the bill, please?\n\n**Notice:** the customer stayed polite even when correcting a mistake. *Excuse me* does that work.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Play once whole, once for the complication. That final turn is the stretch target — polite\n> correction is a genuinely advanced social move at A2.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 The Checklist — تشيك ليست الموقف الكامل\n1. ✅ **Open it** — *A table for three, please*\n2. ✅ **Ask for the menu** — *Could we see the menu?*\n3. ✅ **Order politely** — *Could I have … , please?*\n4. ✅ **A quantity or a container** — *two bottles of water · some bread*\n5. ✅ **Ask a price** — *How much is …?*\n6. ✅ **Close it** — *Could we have the bill, please?*\n7. ⭐ **Handle a problem** — *Excuse me, I didn't order this*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Board it and hand out the card. Seven steps is a lot to hold, so let them keep the card during\n> the recording — the target is the transaction, not memorisation.\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — عيادة النطق\nDrill **your** weakest from this unit:\n\n- **rising intonation** on requests ↗ — this is where politeness lives\n- **food-word clusters** — *bread · fruit · vegetables* with no vowel inside\n- **numbers** — thir**TEEN** against **THIR**ty, because it is money\n- **the reduced *of*** — *a lot of · a bottle of*\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare** your weakest one only.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Personalised from your L01–L04 flags. If in doubt, choose the intonation — it carries the most\n> social weight.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — التسجيل (milestone)\nRehearse with a partner, swap roles, then record.\n\nRun the **whole restaurant visit** as the customer: open, menu, order with a quantity, ask one price, close\nwith the bill.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it and keep it.** 👑\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min recording + 2 feedback.** You play the waiter and introduce one unscripted turn — *\"Sorry, we\n> don't have any fish today\"* — so they have to adapt live.\n> **Success check (Unit-6):** a complete transaction with two polite requests, one quantity or container, one\n> price question, and audible rising intonation (rubric: Fluency + Accuracy + Pronunciation + **Interaction**\n> at A2). Interaction matters most here — this is a two-way task.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- **Post your order.**\n- **Complete the Unit-6 quiz.**\n- **Do it for real.** Order something in English this week — a coffee, a sandwich, anything. You are ready\n  for that now, and it is the whole point of the unit.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر (Unit 6 wrap)\n- Countable takes a plural; uncountable never does. Count the **container**.\n- **some** in statements · **any** in negatives and questions · **some** in offers.\n- **a lot of** works with everything · **many/a few** countable · **much/a little** uncountable.\n- **How many** + countable · **How much** + uncountable and for price.\n- **Could I have …, please?** with a **rising** tone.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — Unit 6 mini-quiz\n1. *We don't have ___ milk.* → **any**\n2. *There are ___ eggs in the box.* (12) → **a lot of / twelve**\n3. *___ rice do you want?* → **How much**\n4. *___ people are coming?* → **How many**\n5. *Two ___ of water, please.* → **bottles**\n6. Polite version of *\"Give me the bill\"* → **Could we have the bill, please?**\n\n---\n\n## 🎉 Unit 6 complete — بقيت تدير موقف كامل\nFive units ago you could describe the present. You can now walk into a restaurant, read a menu, order\npolitely, ask what something costs, correct a mistake, and pay — entirely in English.\n\n**Next → Unit 7: Health & Advice.** Saying what hurts, and telling someone what they should do. 👑\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — موقف حقيقي من أوله لآخره*\n","s1-u7-l01":"# Body & Symptoms: \"What's the Matter?\"\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 7 · **Lesson 1**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: Unit 6\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit7-health-and-advice.md` · **S1-U7-L01***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 6 · Your Turn 3 · Feedback 1.\n> **Objectives:** name body parts · describe symptoms with *have* · use *I feel* + adjective.\n> **The pattern split is the lesson:** English uses **have** for a symptom you possess and **feel** for a state. *I have a headache* against *I feel tired*. Learners who blur them produce *\"I have tired\"* and *\"I feel headache\"*.\n> **This is also the /θ/–/ð/ consolidation point.** Health vocabulary is unusually thick with those sounds — *throat, teeth, mouth, health, breathe* — which is why the syllabus puts them here.\n> **Support:** the body diagram. **Stretch:** describe a symptom you actually had recently.\n> **Arabic ~30%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Name the parts of the body you might need to talk about.\n- Say what is wrong: *I have a headache.*\n- Say how you feel: *I feel tired.* 🎙️\n\n> لو مرضت بره، الجمل دي هي اللي بتوصل مشكلتك. مش رفاهية.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nBeing unable to say what hurts is one of the few moments where missing vocabulary is genuinely serious. It\nis also, at A2, a small and closed set of words — so it is quick to fix.\n\n> وفي الوحدة دي بنرجع لصوتين مهمين: **/θ/** و**/ð/**. كلمات الصحة مليانة بيهم — *throat, teeth, mouth,\n> health* — فدي أحسن فرصة تظبطهم فيها. وكلها كلمات معدودة.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom Unit 6, order something politely with *Could I have*.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Keeps the polite request pattern alive; it returns in L04 at the pharmacy.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nA phone call to a friend (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **A:** You don't sound well. **What's the matter**?\n> **B:** I **have** a bad **cold**. My **throat** hurts and I **have** a **headache**.\n> **A:** Oh no. **Do you have** a **temperature**?\n> **B:** A little. I just **feel** very **tired** and **weak**.\n> **A:** You **should** rest. Don't go to work tomorrow.\n\n**Notice the two patterns:** *I **have** a headache* — a thing you possess. *I **feel** tired* — a state you\nare in.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| What's the matter? | مالك؟ |\n| I have a cold. | أنا مبرد |\n| My throat hurts. | زوري بيوجعني |\n| I feel tired. | حاسس بتعب |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second time learners sort what they hear into *have* sentences and *feel*\n> sentences. That sort is the grammar of the lesson.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**Three patterns cover almost everything.**\n\n**1) *have* + a symptom** — for a thing you have got:\n\n> *I **have** a headache · a cold · a temperature · a sore throat · a stomach ache*\n\n**2) *feel* + an adjective** — for a state:\n\n> *I **feel** tired · sick · dizzy · weak · better*\n\n**3) *… hurts*** — for a specific part:\n\n> *My **back hurts**. · My **tooth hurts**.*\n\n> ولاحظ إن الفعل هنا بيتغير على حسب العضو نفسه، عضو واحد وعضوين مش زي بعض.\n\n> ⚠️ **دي الغلطة المتوقعة:** خلط الاتنين. بنقول *I have a headache* و*I feel tired*، مش *I have tired*\n> ولا *I feel headache*. القاعدة البسيطة: **have** بتيجي مع اسم، و**feel** بتيجي مع صفة.\n\n**Body parts you will need:**\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| head · throat · stomach · back | tooth → **teeth** |\n| eye · ear · hand · leg | foot → **feet** |\n\n**And the question that opens all of this:** *What's the matter?* or *What's wrong?*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Teach the three patterns with two oral examples each, then contrast *have* + noun against\n> *feel* + adjective directly. Flag the two irregular plurals — *teeth* and *feet* — since both are common\n> in health talk.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| a headache | صداع | *I have a headache.* |\n| a cold | برد | *She has a cold.* |\n| a sore throat | التهاب زور | *I have a sore throat.* |\n| a temperature | حرارة | *Do you have a temperature?* |\n| tired / sick / dizzy | تعبان / تعبان جدًا / دايخ | *I feel dizzy.* |\n| … hurts | بيوجعني | *My back hurts.* |\n| teeth / feet | سنان / رجلين | *My teeth hurt.* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Two sounds, one tongue position — and health words are full of both.**\n\n| Sound | Voiced? | Words |\n|---|---|---|\n| **/θ/** | no — no vibration | **th**roat · tee**th** · mou**th** · heal**th** |\n| **/ð/** | yes — throat vibrates | **th**is · **th**e · brea**th**e · **th**ere |\n\nThe tongue tip sits lightly **between the teeth** for both. The only difference is whether your voice is\nswitched on.\n\n**The test:** hand on your throat. *teeth* → still. *breathe* → vibrating.\n\n> نفس مكان اللسان في الاتنين، والفرق بس إن الصوت شغال ولا لأ. حط إيدك على رقبتك وجرّب الكلمتين ورا بعض.\n\n**Watch for the two substitutions:** /θ/ becoming /s/ — *\"sroat\"* — and /ð/ becoming /d/ — *\"dis\"*.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"My throat and my teeth hurt, and I can't breathe well.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Mirror plus hand-on-throat. This pair was introduced back in Stage 0; this is the\n> consolidation, so expect progress rather than a first attempt.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — *have* or *feel*?**\n1. I ______ a headache.\n2. I ______ tired today.\n3. She ______ a cold.\n4. He ______ much better now.\n\n**Exercise 2 — complete with a body part.**\n1. I can't eat — my ______ hurts. (tooth)\n2. I sat all day and now my ______ hurts. (back)\n3. I can't speak well — my ______ is sore. (throat)\n\n**Exercise 3 — fix the mistake.**\n1. *I have tired.* → __________________________\n2. *I feel a headache.* → __________________________\n3. *My tooths hurt.* → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 4 — true for you.** Describe how you feel right now, in two sentences.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** **Ex1:** have · feel · has · feels. **Ex2:** tooth · back · throat.\n> **Ex3:** *I feel tired* · *I have a headache* · *My teeth hurt*.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nDescribe a time you were ill: **two symptoms** with *have*, and **one feeling** with *feel*.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~3 min.** **Success check:** correct *have/feel* split, one body part, /θ/ audible in at least one word.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post two sentences about how you feel today.\n- **Never miss twice.** One low day is normal and human. Two in a row is where a habit quietly ends.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- **have** + a noun: *a headache · a cold*.\n- **feel** + an adjective: *tired · dizzy · better*.\n- **… hurts** for a part: *My back hurts*.\n- **tooth → teeth · foot → feet.**\n- /θ/ and /ð/ share a tongue position; only the voice differs.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 1) have · 2) feel · 3) has · 4) feels\n- **Ex2:** 1) tooth · 2) back · 3) throat\n- **Ex3:** 1) *I feel tired* · 2) *I have a headache* · 3) *My teeth hurt*\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — have مع الاسم، feel مع الصفة*\n","s1-u7-l02":"# Giving Advice: *should / shouldn't*\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 7 · **Lesson 2**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L01\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit7-health-and-advice.md` · **S1-U7-L02***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 6 · Your Turn 4 · Feedback —.\n> **Objectives:** give advice with *should* · advise against with *shouldn't* · ask for advice.\n> **This is a social skill, not only a structure.** Being able to advise a friend is one of the first genuinely *warm* things a learner can do in English, and they usually feel that. Say so.\n> **The form is the easy part** — no *to*, no change for person. Spend the time on softeners, because bare *should* can sound bossy and learners do not hear that.\n> **Support:** the advice-verb bank. **Stretch:** two pieces of advice with a reason each.\n> **Arabic ~30%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Tell someone what to do: *You should rest.*\n- Advise against something: *You shouldn't work today.*\n- **Ask** for advice: *What should I do?* 🎙️\n\n> عرفت تقول إيه اللي وجعك. النهاردة تنصح غيرك — ودي مهارة اجتماعية حقيقية.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nAdvice is one of the warmest things you can offer in another language. It says *I am paying attention to you*\n— and it takes one small word.\n\n> والصعوبة مش في التركيب — ده أسهل جزء. الصعوبة إنك تقولها نصيحة مش أوامر.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom L01, describe two symptoms.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** The symptoms become the material the advice responds to.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nA friend giving advice (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **A:** I have a terrible headache and I feel dizzy.\n> **B:** You **should** drink more water — **maybe** you're dehydrated. And you **shouldn't** look at your\n> phone so much.\n> **A:** **What should I do** about the headache now?\n> **B:** I **think** you **should** rest for an hour. If it doesn't stop, you **should** see a doctor.\n> **A:** OK, thanks.\n\n**Notice:** the advice is softened. *maybe*, *I think* — they make a suggestion instead of an order.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| You should drink water. | لازم تشرب ماية |\n| You shouldn't work today. | ماينفعش تشتغل النهاردة |\n| What should I do? | أعمل إيه؟ |\n| I think you should rest. | أنا شايف إنك ترتاح |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second time learners mark every softener. Then say a bare version — *\"Drink\n> water. Rest.\"* — so they hear how different it lands.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**Form: *should* + the simple verb. Nothing else changes, ever.**\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| I / you / he / she / we / they | **should** rest |\n| negative | **shouldn't** work |\n\n> مفيش **s** مع *he* و*she*، ومفيش **to** بعد **should**. الفعل زي ما هو.\n\n**Three uses:**\n\n| Use | Example |\n|---|---|\n| advice | *You should see a doctor.* |\n| advice against | *You shouldn't drink coffee at night.* |\n| asking for advice | *What should I do?* |\n\n**Now the part that matters socially — soften it.** Bare *should* can sound like an instruction:\n\n| Softer | |\n|---|---|\n| *I think you should…* | the most useful one |\n| *Maybe you should…* | gentle |\n| *You could…* | a suggestion, not advice |\n\n> ⚠️ **خلي بالك:** *\"You should sleep\"* لوحدها ممكن توصل كأمر. حط **I think** أو **maybe** قبلها،\n> وهتوصل نصيحة مش تعليمات.\n\n**Watch the two form errors:** ❌ *He shoulds rest* · ❌ *You should to rest*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Form in two minutes — it is genuinely trivial. Spend the remaining four on softeners and have\n> them re-say bare advice with *I think* in front, aloud.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| You should… | لازم / الأفضل إنك | *You should rest.* |\n| You shouldn't… | ماينفعش | *You shouldn't smoke.* |\n| What should I do? | أعمل إيه؟ | asking |\n| I think you should… | أنا شايف إنك | softener |\n| Maybe you should… | يمكن الأفضل | softener |\n| see a doctor | تروح لدكتور | *You should see a doctor.* |\n| take medicine | تاخد دوا | *take some medicine* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Target 1 — the stress goes on the ADVICE VERB, not on *should*.**\n\n> You should **REST**. · You shouldn't **WORK** today.\n\n*should* is weak and short. Stress it instead and you sound as if you are arguing.\n\n**Target 2 — *should* has a silent *l*.**\n\n> *should* = /ʃʊd/ — like *good* with a \"sh\". Not \"shold\".\n> Same family as *could* and *would*, all with the silent *l*.\n\n**Target 3 — *shouldn't* keeps its final /t/**, exactly like *didn't* and *wasn't*.\n\n> النبرة على الفعل مش على **should**. ولاحظ إن الـ **l** ساكتة في *should* و*could* و*would*.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"I think you should rest, and you shouldn't go to work.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** The silent *l* generalises across three high-frequency modals, so it is efficient to teach\n> here.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — advise them.**\n1. *\"I have a headache.\"* → __________________________\n2. *\"I can't sleep at night.\"* → __________________________\n3. *\"I feel tired all the time.\"* → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 2 — *should* or *shouldn't*?**\n1. You ______ drink water when you have a temperature.\n2. You ______ eat heavy food when your stomach hurts.\n3. You ______ see a doctor if it doesn't stop.\n\n**Exercise 3 — fix the mistake.**\n1. *He shoulds rest.* → __________________________\n2. *You should to see a doctor.* → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 4 — soften it.** *\"Sleep more.\"* → __________________________\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** **Ex1:** accept any sensible advice with correct form. **Ex2:** should · shouldn't · should.\n> **Ex3:** *He should rest* · *You should see a doctor*. **Ex4:** *I think you should sleep more.*\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nA partner tells you a problem. Give **two pieces of advice** — one with *should*, one with *shouldn't* —\nand soften at least one.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record your advice.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** **Success check:** correct form both times, one softener present, stress on the verb.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Reply to a classmate's post with real advice.\n- **Advise yourself.** What should *you* do every day? Say it out loud as advice, in the second person. It\n  lands differently.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- **should / shouldn't + simple verb.** No *to*, no *-s*.\n- *What should I do?* asks for advice.\n- **I think · maybe** turn an order into a suggestion.\n- Stress the **verb**, not *should*.\n- The **l** is silent: *should · could · would*.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex2:** 1) should · 2) shouldn't · 3) should\n- **Ex3:** 1) *He should rest* · 2) *You should see a doctor*\n- **Ex4:** *I think you should sleep more.*\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — النصيحة بتتقال، مش بتتأمر*\n","s1-u7-l03":"# Obligation & Necessity: *have to · don't have to · must*\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 7 · **Lesson 3**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L02\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit7-health-and-advice.md` · **S1-U7-L03***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 6 · Your Turn 4 · Feedback —.\n> **Objectives:** express necessity with *have to* · say something is unnecessary with *don't have to* · use *must*.\n> **The single most valuable thing in this lesson is that *don't have to* does NOT mean *shouldn't*.** It means *there is no need*. Learners assume any negative obligation is a prohibition, and the misunderstanding is genuine — *\"You don't have to come\"* heard as *\"You mustn't come\"* changes a welcome into a rejection.\n> **Support:** the three-card sort. **Stretch:** contrast a rule at their work with a personal choice.\n> **Arabic ~30%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Say something is necessary: *I have to work on Saturday.*\n- Say something is **not** necessary: *You don't have to come.*\n- Use *must* for something strong. 🎙️\n\n> **should** كانت نصيحة. النهاردة الكلام عن الضروري واللي مش ضروري — وده فرق مهم.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nThis is how you talk about rules, work, and anything outside your control. *I have to be there at eight. You\ndon't have to pay. You must take the medicine.*\n\n> والنقطة اللي بتفرق: **don't have to** معناها **مش ضروري**، مش ممنوع.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom L02, give a partner one piece of advice with *should*.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** *should* fresh in the room makes the contrast with *have to* land immediately.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nTwo colleagues comparing their weeks (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **A:** I **have to** work on Saturday this week. The manager asked everyone.\n> **B:** Really? I **don't have to** — my shift changed.\n> **A:** Lucky. And I **have to** finish the report before Thursday.\n> **B:** You **must** be tired. You **should** ask for a day off.\n> **A:** I can't. But I **don't have to** come in early, at least.\n\n**Notice:** *have to* is about a rule from **outside** — the manager, the schedule. *should* was advice from a\nperson.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| I have to work on Saturday. | لازم أشتغل السبت |\n| I don't have to. | مش مضطر |\n| You must be tired. | لازم تكون تعبان |\n| I don't have to come early. | مش لازم أجي بدري |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second time ask *who decided?* for each obligation. External source is what\n> distinguishes *have to* from *should*.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**1) *have to* + simple verb — it is necessary, usually because of a rule.**\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| I / you / we / they | **have to** go |\n| he / she / it | **has to** go |\n\n> ⚠️ خد بالك: مع *he* و*she* بتبقى **has to**. دي الحاجة الوحيدة اللي بتتغير.\n\n**2) *don't have to* — it is NOT necessary. You may, if you like.**\n\n> *You **don't have to** come.* → no obligation, you are free\n> *He **doesn't have to** pay.* → it is not required\n\n**3) *must* — strong, and usually written or formal.**\n\n> *You **must** take the medicine.* · *Passengers **must** wear a seatbelt.*\n\n**The three compared:**\n\n| | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| *You **should** rest.* | advice — my opinion |\n| *You **have to** rest.* | necessary — a rule or a real need |\n| *You **don't have to** rest.* | not necessary — your choice |\n\n> ⚠️ **أهم نقطة في الدرس:** *don't have to* ≠ *shouldn't*. الأولى معناها **مش ضروري**، والتانية معناها\n> **ماينفعش**. لو خلطتهم، بتقول لحد \"ممنوع\" وإنت قصدك \"مفيش إلزام\".\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Build the three-way table on the board and drill it by giving a situation and asking which one\n> fits. The *don't have to* against *shouldn't* contrast deserves at least four examples of its own.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| have to / has to | لازم / مضطر | *I have to go.* |\n| don't have to | مش ضروري | *You don't have to wait.* |\n| must | لازم (قوي) | *You must stop smoking.* |\n| a rule | قاعدة | *It's a rule.* |\n| take a day off | تاخد يوم أجازة | *I have to take a day off.* |\n| be on time | تكون في الميعاد | *We have to be on time.* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Target 1 — *have to* is pronounced /hæftə/.**\nThe *v* becomes an **f**, and *to* reduces to /tə/:\n\n> *I have to go* → **\"I HAF-tə go\"**\n\nThis is not sloppy — it is the standard pronunciation. And *has to* becomes **/hæstə/**.\n\n> دي مش لهجة عامية، ده النطق الطبيعي. الـ **v** بتتحول لـ **f**، ودي مفاجأة لأغلب المتعلمين.\n\n**Target 2 — the stress goes on the main verb:**\n\n> I have to **WORK** on Saturday.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"I have to work, but I don't have to start early.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** The /f/ substitution surprises learners and immediately improves both their listening and their\n> naturalness. Worth the full four minutes.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — *have to* or *has to*?**\n1. I ______ leave at six.\n2. She ______ work on Friday.\n3. They ______ wear a uniform.\n\n**Exercise 2 — necessary, or not necessary?**\n1. *You don't have to bring food.* → ______________\n2. *You have to show your ID.* → ______________\n3. *You mustn't smoke here.* → ______________\n\n**Exercise 3 — *should*, *have to*, or *don't have to*?**\n1. It's a rule at work — I ______ wear a shirt.\n2. You look ill. I think you ______ rest.\n3. It's free — you ______ pay.\n\n**Exercise 4 — fix the mistake.** *\"He have to go.\"* → __________\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** **Ex1:** have to · has to · have to. **Ex2:** not necessary · necessary · prohibited —\n> use the third to sharpen the contrast. **Ex3:** have to · should · don't have to. **Ex4:** *He has to go.*\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nSay **two things you have to do** this week and **one thing you don't have to do**. Then give a partner one\npiece of advice with *should*.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** **Success check:** correct *have/has to*, one accurate *don't have to*, and *should* kept\n> distinct from it.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post two things you have to do and one you don't.\n- **\"I have to\" against \"I choose to.\"** Practising English is not an obligation someone placed on you. Say\n  *\"I choose to\"* about it once, out loud, and notice the difference.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- **have to / has to** + simple verb — necessary, usually an outside rule.\n- **don't have to** = **not necessary**, not forbidden.\n- **must** = strong, often written.\n- **should** = advice · **have to** = necessity.\n- *have to* sounds like **/hæftə/**.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 1) have to · 2) has to · 3) have to\n- **Ex2:** 1) not necessary · 2) necessary · 3) prohibited\n- **Ex3:** 1) have to · 2) should · 3) don't have to\n- **Ex4:** *He has to go.*\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — don't have to معناها مش ضروري، مش ممنوع*\n","s1-u7-l04":"# Health Habits & a Visit to the Doctor\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 7 · **Lesson 4**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L03\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit7-health-and-advice.md` · **S1-U7-L04***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 5 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 7 · Your Turn 4 · Feedback —.\n> **Objectives:** talk about health habits · handle a doctor or pharmacy exchange · combine symptoms, advice and necessity.\n> **This is an integration lesson, not a new-grammar lesson.** Everything is already known; the skill is assembling it under the pressure of a real exchange. Keep your corrections for the assembly, not the parts.\n> **Support:** the exchange script to read from. **Stretch:** ask the pharmacist a follow-up question.\n> **Arabic ~25%** — nearly all English.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Talk about health habits: *I exercise twice a week.*\n- Handle a **pharmacy or doctor** exchange from start to finish.\n- Put symptoms, advice and necessity together in one conversation. 🎙️\n\n> النهاردة مفيش قاعدة جديدة. النهاردة بنجمع كل اللي اتعلمناه في موقف واحد حقيقي.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nA pharmacy visit is short, predictable, and entirely within your reach now. It is also the situation where\nnot having the words is most stressful — so having them is worth a lot.\n\n> والموقف ده تقريبًا ثابت: بتقول عندك إيه، بتسمع نصيحة، بتسأل عن الدوا. جمل محدودة، فممكن تتقنها.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom L01–L03: one symptom, one piece of advice, one thing you have to do.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Three patterns, one round. This is exactly what the lesson will ask them to combine.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nAt a pharmacy (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **Pharmacist:** Good morning. How can I help you?\n> **Customer:** Good morning. I **have** a bad **sore throat** and a **temperature**.\n> **Pharmacist:** How long **have you had** it?\n> **Customer:** Since Sunday. I **feel** very **tired** too.\n> **Pharmacist:** You **should** take these twice a day, after food. And you **have to** finish all of them\n> — **don't** stop when you feel better.\n> **Customer:** **Do I have to** stay at home?\n> **Pharmacist:** You **don't have to**, but you **should** rest. If the temperature **doesn't** go down in\n> two days, you **should** see a doctor.\n> **Customer:** Thank you very much.\n\n**Notice** how the pharmacist used all three: **should** for advice, **have to** for the necessary part, and\n**don't have to** for what is optional.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| How can I help you? | أقدر أساعدك؟ |\n| twice a day, after food | مرتين في اليوم، بعد الأكل |\n| You have to finish all of them. | لازم تكملهم كلهم |\n| Do I have to stay at home? | لازم أقعد في البيت؟ |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second time learners label each modal by function. Point out *How long have you\n> had it?* as receptive only — the present perfect belongs to Stage 2.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**The exchange has a fixed shape. Learn the shape and you can improvise inside it.**\n\n| Step | What you say |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | *I have a … / I feel …* — your symptom |\n| 2 | *Since Sunday. / For two days.* — how long |\n| 3 | *What should I do? / Do I have to …?* — your question |\n| 4 | *Thank you very much.* — close it |\n\n> والترتيب ده بيوفر عليك التفكير، فتركز في الكلام بس.\n\n**Health-habit language, for talking about prevention:**\n\n- *I **exercise** twice a week.* · *I try to **sleep** eight hours.*\n- *I **don't eat** much sugar.* · *I should **drink** more water.*\n\n**Frequency phrases you will need:** *once · twice · three times* + *a day / a week*\n\n> ⚠️ **خلي بالك:** بنقول **once** و**twice**، مش *one time* و*two times*. ومن تلاتة وطالع بنقول\n> *three times*.\n\n**Two useful questions:** *How many times a day?* · *Before or after food?*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** The four-step shape is the deliverable. *once/twice* is the one genuinely new item — learners\n> reliably produce *one time*.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| How can I help you? | أقدر أساعدك؟ | pharmacist |\n| twice a day | مرتين في اليوم | *Take it twice a day.* |\n| after food | بعد الأكل | *after food* |\n| medicine / tablets | دوا / حبوب | *take these tablets* |\n| exercise | أتمرن | *I exercise twice a week.* |\n| Do I have to…? | لازم…؟ | *Do I have to rest?* |\n| get better | أتحسن | *I hope you get better.* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Connected clarity — the whole exchange, not single words.**\n\nThree things to hold together under pressure:\n\n1. **/θ/–/ð/** in the health words — *throat · teeth · health*\n2. **/hæftə/** for *have to*\n3. **rising intonation** on your questions — *Do I have to stay at home?* ↗\n\n> اللي بيحصل إن المتعلم يظبط الكلمة لوحدها، وأول ما يدخل في محادثة حقيقية يفقد الاتنين. فالتمرين هنا على\n> الجملة الكاملة، مش على الكلمة.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"I have a sore throat. Do I have to take anything?\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Drill the full sentence rather than isolated words. Accuracy under connected-speech pressure is\n> the actual target of this lesson.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — once, twice or three times?**\n1. I go to the gym on Monday and Thursday. → ______ a week\n2. I take this tablet in the morning only. → ______ a day\n3. Morning, afternoon and night. → ______ a day\n\n**Exercise 2 — complete the pharmacy exchange.**\n> **Pharmacist:** How can I help you?\n> **You:** ______________________________________\n> **Pharmacist:** How long have you had it?\n> **You:** ______________________________________\n> **Pharmacist:** Take these twice a day.\n> **You:** ______________________________________ *(ask if you have to stay home)*\n\n**Exercise 3 — *should*, *have to*, or *don't have to*?**\n1. You ______ finish all the tablets — it's important.\n2. You ______ stay at home, but it's better if you rest.\n3. I think you ______ drink more water.\n\n**Exercise 4 — your habits.** Two things you do for your health, and one you should do more.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~7 min.** **Ex1:** twice · once · three times. **Ex2:** check the four-step shape.\n> **Ex3:** have to · don't have to · should.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nRole-play the pharmacy with a partner. Give **two symptoms**, ask **one question** with *Do I have to*, and\nclose politely.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record your side of it.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** **Success check:** two symptoms in the correct pattern, one *have to* question with a rising\n> tone, polite close.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post your pharmacy dialogue.\n- **Health is the base of consistency.** Sleep and energy decide whether the daily twenty minutes happens at\n  all. Protect them and the practice looks after itself.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- The shape: **symptom → how long → your question → thank you**.\n- **once · twice · three times** + *a day / a week*.\n- **should** advice · **have to** necessary · **don't have to** optional.\n- *have to* sounds like **/hæftə/**.\n- Hold the /θ/ and the rising tone **inside** the conversation, not just in drills.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 1) twice · 2) once · 3) three times\n- **Ex3:** 1) have to · 2) don't have to · 3) should\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — الموقف شكله ثابت، فاتقنه*\n","s1-u7-l05":"# Unit Speaking Task: Give a Friend Health Advice (+ Review)\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 7 · **Lesson 5** — *UNIT MILESTONE*\n⏱️ ~35 min · 📹 Recorded + live (assessment-style) · Prerequisite: L01–L04\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit7-health-and-advice.md` · **S1-U7-L05***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~35 min):** Warm-up 5 · Model 4 · Checklist 4 · Accent clinic 4 · Rehearse 7 · Record 5 · Feedback 2 · Assign —.\n> **Objectives:** respond to a problem with advice · distinguish advice from necessity · record the milestone.\n> **This milestone is a social act, not a monologue.** They are being kind to someone in a second language. That is a real achievement and worth naming — learners who see it that way perform noticeably warmer.\n> **Support:** the advice bank plus the softener list. **Stretch:** three pieces of advice, each with a reason.\n> **Arabic ~25%** — nearly all English.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك (milestone)\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Listen to a problem and **respond with advice**.\n- Separate *should* from *have to* and *don't have to*.\n- Sound warm rather than bossy.\n- **Record your Unit-7 milestone.** 🎙️\n\n> النهاردة بتساعد حد بالإنجليزي. دي مهارة اجتماعية حقيقية، مش تمرين.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن (سلسلة)\nRapid chain: a symptom → advice with *should* → something with *have to* → something with *don't have to*.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** Fast retrieval across the four lessons.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — النموذج\nA friend responding to a problem (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **A:** I feel terrible. I have a headache every day this week, and I can't sleep.\n> **B:** That doesn't sound good. **How long have** you felt like this?\n> **A:** About five days.\n> **B:** **I think you should** see a doctor — five days is a long time. And **maybe you should** stop\n> drinking coffee after six, **because** it stays in your body for hours.\n> **A:** Do you think I **have to** take a day off?\n> **B:** You **don't have to**, but honestly you **should** rest. You **shouldn't** work twelve hours when\n> you feel like this. And you **should** drink much more water.\n> **A:** Thanks. That helps.\n\n**Notice:** every piece of advice is **softened** and one carries a **reason**. That is what makes it help\nrather than instruct.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Play once for warmth, once for structure. Ask which line felt most helpful — it is always the\n> one with *because*.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 The Checklist — تشيك ليست النصيحة الكويسة\n1. ✅ **React first** — *That doesn't sound good · I'm sorry to hear that*\n2. ✅ **Ask one question** — *How long? · Have you taken anything?*\n3. ✅ **Two pieces of advice** — one *should*, one *shouldn't*\n4. ✅ **Soften at least one** — *I think · maybe*\n5. ✅ **Give a reason** — *because …*\n6. ⭐ **Separate necessity from advice** — *You don't have to …, but you should …*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Board it and hand out the card. Item 1 is the one learners skip, and skipping it is what makes\n> advice sound cold.\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — عيادة النطق\nDrill **your** weakest from this unit:\n\n- **/θ/ and /ð/** in health words — *throat · teeth · health · breathe*\n- **stress on the advice verb** — *You should **REST***\n- **/hæftə/** for *have to*\n- **the silent l** in *should · could · would*\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare** your weakest one only.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Personalised from your L01–L04 flags. One target each.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — التسجيل (milestone)\nRehearse with a partner, swap roles, then record.\n\nA friend tells you a health problem. **React, ask one question, then give two pieces of advice** — softened,\nwith a reason. Separate what they *have to* do from what they *should* do.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it and keep it.** 👑\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min recording + 2 feedback.** You play the friend and give them a real problem — *\"I've had a\n> stomach ache for three days and I have an important exam tomorrow\"* — so the advice has to be specific.\n> **Success check (Unit-7):** a reaction, a question, two correctly-formed pieces of advice with a softener\n> and a reason (rubric: Fluency + Accuracy + Pronunciation + **Interaction** at A2). Warmth counts here.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- **Post your advice.**\n- **Complete the Unit-7 quiz.**\n- **Give one piece of real advice in English this week** — to a classmate, in the channel, anywhere. Advice\n  is the most useful social English there is.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر (Unit 7 wrap)\n- **have** + a noun · **feel** + an adjective · *My back **hurts***.\n- **should / shouldn't** + simple verb — advice. No *to*, no *-s*.\n- **I think · maybe** stop advice sounding like an order.\n- **have to** = necessary · **don't have to** = **not necessary**, not forbidden.\n- /θ/ and /ð/ share a tongue position; *have to* sounds like /hæftə/.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — Unit 7 mini-quiz\n1. *I ___ a headache.* → **have**\n2. *I ___ tired.* → **feel**\n3. *You ___ see a doctor.* (advice) → **should**\n4. *He ___ work on Saturday.* (necessary) → **has to**\n5. *You ___ pay — it's free.* → **don't have to**\n6. Softer version of *\"Rest more.\"* → **I think you should rest more.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎉 Unit 7 complete — بقيت تساعد بالإنجليزي\nYou can now say what hurts, ask someone what is wrong, advise them, tell them what is necessary and what is\nnot, and handle a pharmacy on your own.\n\n**Next → Unit 8: Travel & Transport.** Getting around, buying tickets, and asking for directions in a place\nyou do not know. 👑\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — أول مرة تستخدم الإنجليزي عشان تساعد حد*\n","s1-u8-l01":"# Transport & Getting Around: \"How Do I Get To…?\"\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 8 · **Lesson 1**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: Unit 7 · Stage-0 directions (U8)\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit8-travel-and-transport.md` · **S1-U8-L01***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 6 · Your Turn 3 · Feedback 1.\n> **Objectives:** name common transport and travel places · ask *How do I get to…?* and follow the answer · describe how you usually travel.\n> **Teach the formula, do not parse it.** *How do I get to…?* is one chunk. Learners who try to analyse *get to* stall; learners who memorise the frame use it on day one.\n> **The real error set is the two patterns for transport:** *go **by** bus* (no article) against ***take the** bus*. Cross them and you get ❌ *\"I go by the bus\"* — extremely common and very audible.\n> ***on foot*, not *by foot*.** It is the one member of the set that breaks the pattern, so teach it as an exception rather than hoping it lands.\n> **Support:** transport flashcards + the question frame. **Stretch:** give a two-step route with sequence words.\n> **Arabic ~30%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Name the transport and the places you need in a station or an airport.\n- Ask **How do I get to…?** and follow the answer.\n- Say how you usually travel. 🎙️\n\n> الوحدة دي بتخليك تتحرك في بلد مش بلدك من غير ما تستنى حد يساعدك.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nEverything in Unit 7 was about your body and your health. This unit is about your **feet** — being able to\narrive somewhere. Ten words and one question frame are the difference between waiting for help and moving.\n\n> والحلو إن الردود اللي هتسمعها محدودة وشبه بعضها: خد كذا، وانزل في كذا. لو عرفت شكل الرد، هتفهمه حتى لو\n> سمعت كلمة متعرفهاش.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom Stage 0, Unit 8: *turn left · turn right · go straight · it's near · it's far*. Direct a partner from\nthe door to their chair.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** This is deliberate spaced retrieval — those direction words are eight units old and they are\n> exactly what today's answers are built from.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nAsking a stranger for a route (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **A:** Excuse me. **How do I get to** the **airport**?\n> **B:** **Take the metro** to Sadat, then **take the** number 27 **bus**.\n> **A:** And where do I **get off**?\n> **B:** **Get off at** the second **stop** after the bridge. It's about forty minutes.\n> **A:** Thank you very much.\n> **B:** No problem.\n\n**Notice the shape of the answer:** *Take the …* → *then take the …* → *Get off at …*. Almost every answer\nyou will ever hear is built from those three moves.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| How do I get to…? | أوصل لـ… إزاي؟ |\n| Take the metro. | اركب المترو |\n| Get off at… | انزل في… |\n| It's about forty minutes. | بتاخد حوالي ٤٠ دقيقة |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. On the second pass learners write only the three route moves, not the words — the\n> point is that the answer has a predictable skeleton.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**1) The question is one fixed chunk. Memorise it whole.**\n\n> ***How do I get to*** + a place?\n> *How do I get to the station? · How do I get to the airport?*\n\n**2) The answer has three moves.**\n\n| Move | English |\n|---|---|\n| which transport | *Take the bus / the metro / the train.* |\n| where to change | *Then take the number 27.* |\n| where to leave | *Get off at the third stop.* |\n\n> والتلات حركات دي هي كل الرد، فمش لازم تفهم كل كلمة.\n\n**3) Now the part learners get wrong — there are two ways to name your transport, and they do not mix.**\n\n| Pattern | Example | |\n|---|---|---|\n| **by** + transport, **no *the*** | *I go to work **by bus**.* | ✅ |\n| **take the** + transport | *I **take the** bus to work.* | ✅ |\n| the two crossed | *I go to work by the bus.* | ❌ |\n\n> ⚠️ **دي أشهر غلطة في الدرس:** إما **by** لوحدها من غير **the**، أو **take the**. ماينفعش تخلط الاتنين.\n\n**4) One exception, and it is worth learning as its own item:** walking is ***on* foot**, never *by foot*.\n\n> *I go **on foot**.* ✅ · *I go by foot.* ❌\n\n> والمشي هو الاستثناء الوحيد في القايمة.\n\n**Places you will need:** *a station · a (bus) stop · a platform · an airport · a taxi rank*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Drill the frame chorally first, then the two transport patterns as a contrast pair, then *on\n> foot* alone so it does not get absorbed into the *by* set.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| How do I get to…? | أوصل لـ… إزاي؟ | *How do I get to the airport?* |\n| take the bus / metro | اركب الأتوبيس / المترو | *Take the metro to Sadat.* |\n| get off at… | انزل في… | *Get off at the next stop.* |\n| a station / a stop | محطة / موقف | *The station is near here.* |\n| a platform | رصيف القطر | *Platform four.* |\n| by bus / by train | بالأتوبيس / بالقطر | *I go by train.* |\n| on foot | ماشي | *I go on foot.* |\n| How long does it take? | بتاخد قد إيه؟ | *How long does it take?* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Part 1 — three words that become one.** *Get off at* is never said as three separate words:\n\n> **get off at** → *ge-TOF-fat* — the /t/ jumps forward and joins *off*.\n\nSay it slowly three times, then at speed. If a listener hears three words, it is too slow to sound natural.\n\n**Part 2 — your own city's name in English.** This is the one that costs people. English stresses these\ndifferently from Arabic:\n\n| Place | English says | not |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cairo | **KY**-roh | *KAY-roh* |\n| Alexandria | a-lig-**ZAN**-dree-uh | *a-lex-AN-dria* |\n| Dubai | doo-**BYE** | *DOO-bye* |\n| Doha | **DOH**-huh | *do-HA* |\n\n> ودي مهمة أكتر من شكلها: لو قلت اسم بلدك بالنطق العربي، اللي بيسمعك ساعات مش بياخد باله إنه اسم مكان\n> أصلاً، فالجملة كلها بتتوه.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"How do I get to the airport? I want to get off at the last stop.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Two minutes on the linking, two on place names. Have every learner say their own city out\n> loud — it is the place name they will use most and the one they have never heard said in English.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — *by*, *the*, or *on*?**\n1. I go to school ______ bus.\n2. I take ______ metro every morning.\n3. My office is close, so I go ______ foot.\n4. She goes to Alexandria ______ train.\n\n**Exercise 2 — build the answer.** Someone asks *\"How do I get to the museum?\"* Give a three-move answer.\n\n**Exercise 3 — fix the mistake.**\n1. *I go to work by the car.* → __________________________\n2. *How I get to the station?* → __________________________\n3. *I go by foot every day.* → __________________________\n4. *Get off in the third stop.* → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 4 — true for you.** How do you usually travel to work or school? Two sentences.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** **Ex1:** by · the · on · by. **Ex3:** *by car* · *How **do** I get to the station?* · *on\n> foot* · *get off **at***. Ex2 is the one to spend time on — check for all three moves.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nDescribe how you usually get to work or school: **the transport**, **how long it takes**, and **one place**\nyou pass or change at.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~3 min.** **Success check:** correct transport pattern (*by* or *take the*, not crossed), one travel\n> place, and a linked *get off at* if it appears.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post: *\"I usually go to ___ by ___.\"* plus one *How do I get to…?* question for the group to answer.\n- **Switch your maps app to English this week.** It is the cheapest input you will ever get: you already\n  know where you are going, so you cannot get lost — you can only learn the words.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- ***How do I get to…?*** — one chunk, learn it whole.\n- The answer is three moves: **take the …** → **then take …** → **get off at …**\n- **by bus** (no *the*) **or** **take the bus** — never both.\n- **on foot**, not *by foot*.\n- *get off at* sounds like **ge-TOF-fat**.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 1) by · 2) the · 3) on · 4) by\n- **Ex3:** 1) *I go to work by car.* · 2) *How **do** I get to the station?* · 3) *I go **on** foot every day.* · 4) *Get off **at** the third stop.*\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — by bus أو take the bus، مش الاتنين*\n","s1-u8-l02":"# Requests & Permission: *Can / Could I…?*\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 8 · **Lesson 2**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L01\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit8-travel-and-transport.md` · **S1-U8-L02***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 7 · Your Turn 3 · Feedback —.\n> **Objectives:** make polite requests and ask permission with *Can/Could I…?* and *Could you…?* · respond to a request · handle common travel requests.\n> **The split that carries the lesson:** ***I*** asks about **me** — permission. ***you*** asks the other person to **act** — a request. Same modal, two jobs, and learners mix them: ❌ *\"Could I help me with my bag?\"*\n> **Intonation is not decoration here.** Said flat, *Could you help me* is a command. This is the single most consequential thing in the lesson and it cannot be taught on paper — model the rise, out loud, repeatedly.\n> **This is Unit 7's softening lesson again in a new setting.** *Should* needed *I think*; requests need *could* plus a friendly rise. Name that link — it is the same social skill twice.\n> **Support:** the request frames card. **Stretch:** *\"Excuse me, could you possibly…?\"*\n> **Arabic ~30%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Ask permission: **Can I…? · Could I…?**\n- Ask someone to help: **Could you…?**\n- Answer a request politely — yes or no. 🎙️\n\n> جملة واحدة مؤدبة بتفتح أبواب. ودي حاجة بتتعلم مرة وتستخدمها طول عمرك.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nWhen you travel you need things from strangers: help with a bag, a different seat, a repeated instruction.\nThe words are easy. What decides whether you get help is **how the request sounds**.\n\n> والفرق بسيط ومحسوس: **could** أدب أعلى من **can**، والصوت الطالع لفوق في آخر السؤال بيحوّل الطلب من أمر\n> لطلب.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom L01: three transport words, and one *How do I get to…?* question with a full three-move answer.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Fast pair work. Today's requests happen in the same station.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nTwo small requests on a train (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **A:** Excuse me, **could you** help me with my bag? It's very heavy.\n> **B:** **Of course.** Here you go.\n> **A:** Thank you so much. And **can I** sit here?\n> **B:** **Sorry, you can't** — someone is sitting there. But that seat is free.\n> **A:** Great, thanks. **Could I** open the window?\n> **B:** **Sure**, no problem.\n\n**Notice:** *could **you*** asks **B** to do something. *can/could **I*** asks whether **A** is allowed. And\nnotice that the refusal is still friendly — *Sorry, you can't* plus an alternative.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| Could you help me? | تقدر تساعدني؟ |\n| Can I sit here? | أقدر أقعد هنا؟ |\n| Of course. / Sure. | طبعًا |\n| Sorry, you can't. | آسف، مش ممكن |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second pass: learners label each request *I* or *you*. That sort is the grammar.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**One rule of form, and it never changes:** *can* and *could* are followed by the **simple verb**. No *to*,\nno *-s*.\n\n**1) Asking about yourself — permission.**\n\n> ***Can I*** *sit here?* · ***Could I*** *open the window?*\n\n**2) Asking the other person — a request.**\n\n> ***Could you*** *help me?* · ***Can you*** *say that again?*\n\n> ⚠️ **الفرق كله في الضمير:** **I** معناها إذن لنفسي، و**you** معناها طلب من الشخص التاني. لو خلطتهم بتقول\n> جملة مالهاش معنى زي ❌ *\"Could I help me?\"*\n\n**3) *could* is more polite than *can*.** Both are correct. *Could* is safer with a stranger.\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| *Can you help me?* | fine — friendly, direct |\n| *Could you help me?* | more polite — use with strangers |\n| *Excuse me, could you possibly help me?* | very polite ⭐ |\n\n**4) The answers, which you need as much as the questions.**\n\n| Yes | No |\n|---|---|\n| *Sure. · Of course. · No problem.* | *Sorry, you can't. · I'm afraid not.* |\n\n> وخد بالك: الرفض بالإنجليزي بييجي معاه **sorry** واقتراح بديل. الرفض الجاف لوحده بيبان وحش.\n\n**Two form errors to avoid:** ❌ *Could you to help me?* · ❌ *Can she helps me?*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Two minutes on form — it is trivial. Four on the *I*/*you* split and the refusal frame, both\n> of which are where the real errors live.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Could you…? | تقدر…؟ (طلب) | *Could you help me?* |\n| Can I…? / Could I…? | أقدر…؟ (إذن) | *Could I sit here?* |\n| Excuse me… | لو سمحت | *Excuse me, could you…?* |\n| Of course. / Sure. | طبعًا | *Sure, no problem.* |\n| I'm afraid not. | للأسف لأ | *I'm afraid not.* |\n| Could you say that again? | تقدر تعيد؟ | *Could you say that again, please?* |\n| Could you speak more slowly? | تقدر تتكلم أبطأ؟ | *Could you speak more slowly?* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Part 1 — the rise. This is the lesson.**\n\nA request goes **up** at the end. Flat means an order.\n\n> *Could you help me?* ↗️ — a request\n> *Could you help me.* ➡️ — an instruction\n\nSay the same four words both ways until you can hear yourself switch.\n\n> نفس الكلمات بالحرف، والفرق بس في الصوت آخر الجملة. طالع لفوق يبقى طلب، ومستوي يبقى أمر.\n\n**Part 2 — *could you* becomes one word.**\n\n> **could you** → /kʊdʒə/ — *\"koo-juh\"*\n> **could I** → /kʊdaɪ/ — the /d/ links straight into *I*\n\nNative speakers almost never separate them. *\"Could you\"* said as two clean words sounds careful, not wrong —\nbut hearing /kʊdʒə/ is what lets you understand the reply.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"Excuse me, could you help me with my bag?\"* — with the rise.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Do the flat-versus-rising contrast as a whole group, exaggerated, then normal. Learners\n> genuinely cannot hear that they sound blunt until they hear both versions back to back.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — *Could I* or *Could you*?**\n1. ______ open the window? (I want to open it)\n2. ______ pass me the water? (I want them to pass it)\n3. ______ say that again? (I didn't understand)\n4. ______ pay by card? (I want to know if it's allowed)\n\n**Exercise 2 — make it more polite.**\n1. *Give me a pen.* → __________________________\n2. *Speak slowly.* → __________________________\n3. *Move your bag.* → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 3 — fix the mistake.**\n1. *Could you to help me?* → __________________________\n2. *Can I helps you?* → __________________________\n3. *Could I help me with my bag?* → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 4 — refuse politely.** Someone asks *\"Can I sit here?\"* but the seat is taken. Answer in two\nsentences: refuse, then offer something else.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~7 min.** **Ex1:** Could I · Could you · Could you · Could I. **Ex3:** drop *to* · *Can I help you?* ·\n> *Could you help me with my bag?* Ex4 matters most — insist on *sorry* plus an alternative.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nYou are at an airport. Make **three** polite requests: one with *Could I…?*, one with *Could you…?*, and one\nstarting *Excuse me*.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it** — and mind the rise.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~3 min.** **Success check:** correct *I*/*you* split, correct bare verb, and audible rising intonation on\n> at least two of the three.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post two polite requests you might genuinely make while travelling.\n- **Use one of them in real life this week** — in English, to anyone, even in a text. A phrase you have used\n  once is worth ten you have only practised.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- **can / could** + simple verb. No *to*, no *-s*.\n- **Can/Could I…?** = permission for **me**. **Could you…?** = a request of **you**.\n- **could** is politer than **can**; *Excuse me, could you possibly…* is politer still.\n- Refuse with **sorry** and an alternative, never bare.\n- The rise ↗️ is what makes it a request. Flat is an order.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 1) Could I · 2) Could you · 3) Could you · 4) Could I\n- **Ex2 (model):** 1) *Could you pass me a pen?* · 2) *Could you speak more slowly?* · 3) *Could you move your bag, please?*\n- **Ex3:** 1) *Could you help me?* · 2) *Can I help you?* · 3) *Could I help **me**…* → *Could you help me with my bag?*\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — could you طلب، could I إذن*\n","s1-u8-l03":"# Booking & Rules: Buying a Ticket; *You Have To / You Can't*\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 8 · **Lesson 3**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L02 · Unit 7 (*have to*)\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit8-travel-and-transport.md` · **S1-U8-L03***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 7 · Your Turn 3 · Feedback —.\n> **Objectives:** buy a ticket (price, times, single/return) · state travel rules with *have to / can't* · handle a short booking exchange.\n> **No new grammar.** *Have to* came from Unit 7 and *can't* from Stage 0. This lesson is assembly under pressure, which is a different skill — expect learners who were accurate in Unit 7 to drop pieces inside a live booking.\n> **The three-way meaning check is worth protecting:** *have to* = necessary · *don't have to* = **not** necessary · *can't* = **forbidden**. Unit 7 taught the first two; today adds the third, and *don't have to* against *can't* is the confusion to hunt.\n> ***can* against *can't* is the hardest sound in this stage for Arabic speakers** — the final /t/ is often released so weakly that the two become identical, which inverts the meaning of the sentence. Drill the minimal pair honestly; do not accept \"close enough\".\n> **Support:** the booking-phrase card. **Stretch:** handle a complication — the train is sold out, get an alternative.\n> **Arabic ~30%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Buy a ticket: ask the price, the time, single or return.\n- Say what is required and what is forbidden.\n- Get through a booking window without switching languages. 🎙️\n\n> شباك التذاكر موقف قصير ومتكرر. لو حفظت شكله، بتخلص منه في تلاتين ثانية.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nA booking is a small, closed conversation — perhaps six lines, and they come in the same order every time.\nThe rules half matters for a different reason: *can't* and *don't have to* look similar and mean opposite\nthings.\n\n> والخلطة الخطيرة هنا: **can't** معناها ممنوع، و**don't have to** معناها مش لازم. لو قلت للواحد ممنوع\n> وإنت قصدك مش لازم، الكلام كله بيتقلب.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom L02, two polite requests. From Unit 6 and Stage 0: *How much is it? · What time…?*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Both come back inside the booking in four minutes' time — that is why they are the warm-up.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nAt a ticket window (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **A:** Hello. **A return ticket** to Alexandria, please.\n> **B:** Sure. **What time** do you want to travel?\n> **A:** The morning, if possible. **How much is it**?\n> **B:** Two hundred and forty. The next one leaves at nine forty, from **platform** three.\n> **A:** Perfect. Do I **have to** book a seat?\n> **B:** Yes, you **have to** — and you **can't** change the ticket after you buy it.\n> **A:** Understood. Thank you.\n\n**Notice the order:** what you want → when → how much → which platform → the rules. It is the same every\ntime, which is exactly why you can prepare for it.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| A return ticket to… | تذكرة ذهاب وعودة لـ… |\n| What time does it leave? | بتقوم الساعة كام؟ |\n| How much is it? | بكام؟ |\n| You have to book. | لازم تحجز |\n| You can't change it. | ماينفعش تغيرها |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice; on the second pass learners write the five steps in order. The skeleton is the\n> takeaway, not the vocabulary.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**1) Buying the ticket — four phrases cover it.**\n\n| You need | You say |\n|---|---|\n| the ticket | *A **single** / a **return** ticket to Cairo, please.* |\n| the time | *What time does it **leave** / **arrive**?* |\n| the price | *How much is it?* |\n| the place | *Which **platform** / **gate**?* |\n\n**single** = one direction. **return** = there and back.\n\n> وأربع جمل بس هما كل اللي محتاجه على الشباك، والباقي بتسمعه مش بتقوله.\n\n**2) The rules — three meanings, and they are not interchangeable.**\n\n| English | Meaning | |\n|---|---|---|\n| *You **have to** check in.* | necessary — required | 🔒 |\n| *You **don't have to** book.* | **not** necessary — your choice | 🆓 |\n| *You **can't** smoke here.* | **forbidden** | ⛔ |\n\n> ⚠️ **أهم نقطة في الدرس:** **can't** ≠ **don't have to**. الأولى ممنوع، والتانية مش ضروري. الاتنين شكلهم\n> قريب والمعنى عكس بعضه.\n\n**3) Form, in one line:** *have to* + simple verb · *has to* for *he/she/it* · *can't* + simple verb, and\n*can't* never changes for anybody.\n\n> *He **has to** show his passport.* · *She **can't** take that bag on the plane.*\n\n**Asking about a rule:**\n\n> ***Do I have to*** *book a seat?* · ***Can I*** *take this bag?*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Build the three-row rules table on the board and drill by naming a situation for learners to\n> classify: *required*, *your choice*, or *forbidden*. Four examples of *can't* against *don't have to*.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| a single / a return | ذهاب / ذهاب وعودة | *A return to Cairo, please.* |\n| What time does it leave? | بتقوم الساعة كام؟ | *What time does it leave?* |\n| a platform / a gate | رصيف / بوابة | *Platform three.* |\n| to book | يحجز | *Do I have to book?* |\n| to check in | يعمل تسجيل وصول | *You have to check in online.* |\n| You have to… | لازم… | *You have to wear a seatbelt.* |\n| You can't… | ممنوع… | *You can't smoke here.* |\n| It's sold out. | التذاكر خلصت | *Sorry, it's sold out.* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**This is the most important pronunciation minute in Stage 1.** *can* and *can't* are opposites, and if a\nlistener cannot tell them apart, your sentence means the reverse of what you intended.\n\n| | Sound | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| **can** | /kən/ in a sentence | weak, almost swallowed |\n| **can't** | /kænt/ | fuller vowel **and** a real /t/ |\n\nTwo things change together: *can't* has a **clearer vowel** and a **final /t/** you must actually release.\n\n**Minimal pair drill** — say each pair until a partner can tell them apart with eyes closed:\n\n> *I **can** go.* — *I **can't** go.*\n> *You **can** park here.* — *You **can't** park here.*\n\n> ودي مش تفصيلة صغيرة: لو الـ**t** مش طالعة، اللي قصادك بيسمع العكس تمامًا. اقفل الكلمة بالتاء بوضوح.\n\n**Also keep numbers clean** in a booking — times and prices are the two things nobody can guess from context.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"I can travel on Monday, but I can't travel on Tuesday.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Eyes-closed pair testing is the only honest check. If a partner cannot tell, the learner has\n> not done it, however confident they feel.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — *have to*, *don't have to*, or *can't*?**\n1. You ______ show your passport at the airport. (required)\n2. You ______ pay for children under six. (not necessary)\n3. You ______ use your phone during take-off. (forbidden)\n4. He ______ check in online. (required)\n\n**Exercise 2 — complete the booking.**\n1. ______ ticket to Aswan, please. (there and back)\n2. What time ______ it leave?\n3. ______ is it?\n4. Which ______ ?\n\n**Exercise 3 — fix the mistake.**\n1. *You have to check in online, isn't it?* → __________________________\n2. *She have to book a seat.* → __________________________\n3. *You don't have to smoke here.* (you mean: forbidden) → __________________________\n4. *What time it leaves?* → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 4 — role-play.** Buy a ticket. Your partner tells you it is sold out. Ask for an alternative.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~7 min.** **Ex1:** have to · don't have to · can't · has to. **Ex2:** A return · does · How much · platform.\n> **Ex3:** *…online, **don't you**?* · *She **has to***… · *You **can't** smoke here.* · *What time **does**\n> it leave?* Ex4 is the stretch — do not let them settle for \"OK, thank you\".\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nBook a ticket in **four lines**: what you want, when, the price, and one question about a rule. Then state\n**one rule** of a place you know — with *have to* or *can't*.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~3 min.** **Success check:** a coherent booking, one correctly-formed rule, and an audible /t/ on any\n> *can't*.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post a short booking exchange, **or** two travel rules for a place you have been.\n- **Plan your reps like a trip.** Know your next three steps before you start — the lesson you will do\n  tomorrow, the one after, and when. Vague plans are how streaks die.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- **single** = one way · **return** = there and back.\n- *What time does it **leave**? · How much is it? · Which **platform**?*\n- **have to** = required · **don't have to** = not required · **can't** = forbidden.\n- *has to* for *he/she*; *can't* never changes.\n- **can** /kən/ against **can't** /kænt/ — release that /t/ or you say the opposite.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 1) have to · 2) don't have to · 3) can't · 4) has to\n- **Ex2:** 1) A return · 2) does · 3) How much · 4) platform\n- **Ex3:** 1) *…don't you?* · 2) *She **has to** book a seat.* · 3) *You **can't** smoke here.* · 4) *What time **does** it leave?*\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — can't ممنوع، don't have to مش لازم*\n","s1-u8-l04":"# A Past Trip: Narrate a Journey\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 8 · **Lesson 4**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L03 · Units 1–3 (past simple)\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit8-travel-and-transport.md` · **S1-U8-L04***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 5 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 7 · Your Turn 4 · Feedback 1.\n> **Objectives:** narrate a past trip with past simple + sequence + travel vocabulary · say where, how, and what you did · react to a peer and ask a past question.\n> **This lesson exists for the spaced-review payoff.** Units 1–3 built the past tense in isolation; this is the first time it carries real content the learner cares about. Name that out loud — learners badly underestimate how much past tense they now control, and seeing it work in a story is the proof.\n> **Six verbs do most of the work:** *went · took · stayed · visited · saw · was/were*. Four of the six are irregular, which is why the past-tense block came first.\n> **The one form error to hunt:** past tense marked twice after *didn't* — ❌ *\"I didn't went\"*. It reappears under narrative pressure even in learners who had it right in Unit 3.\n> **Support:** the trip-skeleton card plus the verb bank. **Stretch:** add a feeling and a superlative — *\"the best part was…\"*.\n> **Arabic ~25%** — the story is theirs to tell in English.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Tell the story of a real trip: where, how, who with, what you did.\n- Use *There was / There were* to describe the place.\n- Ask someone a question about their trip. 🎙️\n\n> النهاردة بتجمع كل الماضي اللي اتعلمته في حكاية حقيقية. دي أول مرة تشوف تعبك بيشتغل.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nThis is where the past tense stops being an exercise. You built it in Units 1, 2 and 3 — irregular verbs,\n*did/didn't*, *was/were* — and today it carries something you actually want to say.\n\n> ولاحظ حاجة: الحكاية دي كنت مستحيل تحكيها قبل ٦ أسابيع.\n\nTelling a story is also the single most useful conversational move there is. It buys you a whole turn.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nRapid retrieval: five irregular pasts (*go · take · see · buy · eat*), then two *There was / There were*\nsentences about the room you are in.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Both feed straight into the model. Keep it to four minutes — the verbs should be fast by now.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nSomeone telling a short trip story (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **Last summer** I **went** to Luxor with my brother. We **took** the night train — it **was** long, about\n> ten hours, but it **was** cheap. We **stayed** in a small hotel near the river. **On the first day** we\n> **visited** the temple, and **then** we **saw** the tombs. **There were** a lot of tourists, so it **was**\n> very busy. We **didn't have** much time, so we **didn't go** to the museum. **The best part was** the\n> train at night — I **slept** with the window open. I **loved** it.\n\n**Notice three things:** the time phrase comes **first** (*Last summer*), the sequence words carry you\nforward (*On the first day · then*), and the story ends with a **feeling**, not a fact.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| Last summer I went to… | الصيف اللي فات رحت… |\n| We took the night train. | ركبنا قطر الليل |\n| We stayed in a hotel. | قعدنا في فندق |\n| The best part was… | أحلى حاجة كانت… |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second pass: learners mark the time phrase, the sequence words, and the feeling —\n> the three things that turn a list of facts into a story.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**A trip story has five slots. Fill them and you have a narration.**\n\n| # | Slot | Language |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **when + where** | *Last summer I **went** to…* |\n| 2 | **how + who with** | *I **took** the train with…* |\n| 3 | **where you stayed** | *We **stayed** in / at…* |\n| 4 | **what you did** | *We **visited**… · we **saw**… · **There were**…* |\n| 5 | **how it felt** | *It **was** amazing. · **The best part was**…* |\n\n> والخمس خانات دي شكل أي حكاية، مش رحلة بس.\n\n**Your six workhorse verbs:**\n\n| Present | Past | |\n|---|---|---|\n| go · take · see | **went · took · saw** | irregular |\n| stay · visit | **stayed · visited** | regular *-ed* |\n| is/are | **was / were** | irregular |\n\n**Sequence words that hold the story together:** *First · then · after that · in the end*\n\n> ⚠️ **الغلطة اللي بترجع تاني هنا:** بعد **didn't** الفعل بيرجع أساسي. *I didn't **go***، مش *I didn't\n> went*. الماضي مرة واحدة بس في الجملة.\n\n**And the time phrase goes at the front:** *Last week · Two years ago · When I was a student…*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** Board the five slots and leave them up for the whole lesson — learners build directly onto\n> them in Train. Re-drill *didn't* + base verb even though Unit 3 covered it; narration reliably breaks it.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| I went to… | رحت… | *I went to Luxor.* |\n| I took the train / a taxi | ركبت القطر / تاكسي | *We took the night train.* |\n| we stayed in / at… | قعدنا في… | *We stayed in a small hotel.* |\n| we visited… | زرنا… | *We visited the temple.* |\n| There was / There were… | كان فيه… | *There were a lot of tourists.* |\n| a trip / a journey | رحلة | *It was a long journey.* |\n| The best part was… | أحلى حاجة كانت… | *The best part was the train.* |\n| in the end | في الآخر | *In the end we went home.* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Narrative rhythm — the thing that makes a story sound like a story.**\n\nYou do not stress every word. You stress the **content** — the place, the transport, the feeling — and let\nthe small words run underneath:\n\n> *We **TOOK** the **NIGHT TRAIN** to **LUXOR**.*\n\nSay that with all five words equally stressed, then again with only the capitals. The second one sounds like\nsomeone telling you something; the first sounds like reading.\n\n**Keep the endings clean under speed.** When learners speed up, *-ed* is the first thing to disappear:\n\n> *stay**ed*** /steɪd/ · *visit**ed*** /ˈvɪzɪtɪd/ · *walk**ed*** /wɔːkt/\n\nRemember the three *-ed* sounds from Unit 2: /d/, /ɪd/, /t/. *Visited* has the extra syllable; *walked* does\nnot.\n\n> ولما بتسرع، أول حاجة بتضيع هي آخر الفعل. اسمع نفسك.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"We took the night train and we stayed in a small hotel near the river.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Two minutes rhythm, two on *-ed* survival at speed. The equal-stress-versus-content-stress\n> contrast is worth doing as a whole group; it lands instantly.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — past forms.**\n1. Last year I ______ (go) to Dubai.\n2. We ______ (take) a taxi from the airport.\n3. There ______ (be) a lot of people at the station.\n4. I ______ (not / visit) the museum.\n5. We ______ (stay) with my cousin.\n\n**Exercise 2 — put the trip in order.**\n> a) *We visited the old city.* b) *Last April I went to Amman.* c) *In the end we flew home.*\n> d) *We stayed in a small flat.* e) *I took the bus from the border.*\n\n**Exercise 3 — fix the mistake.**\n1. *I didn't went to the museum.* → __________________________\n2. *There was a lot of tourists.* → __________________________\n3. *We stay in a hotel last week.* → __________________________\n4. *I went to Luxor last summer with the train.* → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 4 — build from the skeleton.** Use the five slots to tell a trip in five sentences — one per slot.\nThen your partner asks you **one past question** about it.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~7 min.** **Ex1:** went · took · were · didn't visit · stayed. **Ex2:** b, e, d, a, c.\n> **Ex3:** *didn't **go*** · *There **were*** · *We **stayed*** · ***by** train*. Ex4 is the rehearsal for\n> Your Turn — make sure the partner question actually gets asked and answered.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nNarrate a **real** trip in **five or six sentences**, using all five slots. End with how it felt.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min recording + 1 feedback.** **Success check:** a coherent 5–6 sentence narration, correct past\n> forms (especially after *didn't*), at least three travel words, and a closing feeling. If they only manage\n> four slots, the missing one is almost always #5 — push for it, because it is what makes it a story.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post your 5–6 sentence **\"my last trip\"** — and ask **one classmate one question** about theirs.\n- **Look back six weeks.** You could not have told this story then. Not because you were lazy — because you\n  did not have the tense yet. That is what accumulation looks like.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- Five slots: **when/where · how/who · where you stayed · what you did · how it felt.**\n- **went · took · saw · stayed · visited · was/were** — six verbs carry the whole story.\n- After **didn't**, the verb goes back to base: *didn't **go***.\n- **There was** + singular · **There were** + plural.\n- Stress the content words; keep *-ed* alive at speed.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 1) went · 2) took · 3) were · 4) didn't visit · 5) stayed\n- **Ex2:** b → e → d → a → c\n- **Ex3:** 1) *I didn't **go**…* · 2) *There **were** a lot of tourists.* · 3) *We **stayed** in a hotel last week.* · 4) *…**by** train.*\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — الماضي مرة واحدة في الجملة، وبعد didn't الفعل أساسي*\n","s1-u8-l05":"# Unit Speaking Task: \"Tell Me About a Trip You Took\" (+ Review)\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 8 · **Lesson 5** — *UNIT MILESTONE*\n⏱️ ~35 min · 📹 Recorded + live (assessment-style) · Prerequisite: L01–L04\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit8-travel-and-transport.md` · **S1-U8-L05***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~35 min):** Warm-up 5 · Model 4 · Checklist 4 · Accent clinic 4 · Rehearse 7 · Record 5 · Feedback 2 · Assign —.\n> **Objectives:** narrate a trip in 7–9 sentences mixing past narration, travel language, and a request or a rule · answer a follow-up and ask one · record the Unit-8 milestone.\n> **This is a strong candidate for a mid-A2 proof clip.** It is personal, integrative, and audibly harder than anything from Stage 0 — put it beside their Unit-0 recording and the distance is obvious to the learner, not just to you.\n> **The integration is the difficulty, not the parts.** Each learner can do past narration, requests, and rules separately. Under a 7–9 sentence load, two of the three will slip. Correct the assembly.\n> **Give them a real follow-up question live** — not a scripted one. Handling an unplanned question is the A2 interaction threshold, and it is the part they cannot rehearse.\n> **Support:** 7 sentences with the checklist card. **Stretch:** include a request you actually made, a rule of the place, and two follow-up answers.\n> **Arabic ~25%** — nearly all English.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك (milestone)\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Narrate a trip in **7–9 connected sentences**.\n- Fold in **one request** you made or **one rule** of the place.\n- Answer a question you did not expect — and ask one.\n- **Record your Unit-8 milestone.** 🎙️\n\n> دي حكاية كاملة، مش جمل مرصوصة. حاول تحكيها كأنك بتحكي لصاحبك.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن (سلسلة)\nRapid chain across the unit: a transport word → a polite request → a travel rule with *have to* or *can't* →\na past verb.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** One item each, fast, no repeats. This is the retrieval sweep before the load goes up.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — النموذج\nA full trip narration (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **Two years ago** I **went** to Istanbul with two friends. We **flew** from Cairo — the flight **was** only\n> two hours. From the airport we **took** the metro, and we **got off at** Sultanahmet. We **stayed** in a\n> small hotel near the mosque. **On the first day** we **visited** the old market, and I **bought** a lamp for\n> my mother. My bag **was** very heavy, so at the station I **asked** someone — *\"**Could you** help me with\n> my bag?\"* — and he **did**. One thing surprised me: you **can't** take photos inside some of the mosques.\n> **There were** signs everywhere. **The best part was** the food. I **want** to go back.\n\n**Notice:** nine sentences, and it contains the whole unit — transport, *get off at*, a **request** in direct\nspeech, a **rule** with *can't*, past narration throughout, and a feeling at the end.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Play once for the story, once for the checklist. Ask them to find the request and the rule —\n> both are folded in naturally rather than announced, which is the standard.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 The Checklist — تشيك ليست الحكاية الكاملة\n1. ✅ **When and where** — *Two years ago I went to…*\n2. ✅ **How you travelled** — *We flew · we took the metro · we got off at…*\n3. ✅ **Where you stayed** — *We stayed in…*\n4. ✅ **What you did** — two things, in sequence\n5. ✅ **A request or a rule** — *\"Could you…?\"* or *You can't…*\n6. ✅ **How it felt** — *The best part was… · It was…*\n7. ⭐ **Handle one question** you were not expecting\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Hand out the card. Item 5 is the one they forget, because it is the one that is not\n> chronological — it has to be inserted deliberately.\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — عيادة النطق\nDrill **your** weakest from this unit:\n\n- ***can* against *can't*** — the final /t/, released properly\n- **place names** — *Cairo · Alexandria · Dubai · Doha* — English stress\n- **linking** — *get off at* → *ge-TOF-fat* · *could you* → /kʊdʒə/\n- **rising intonation** on a request\n- **clean *-ed*** at narrative speed\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare** your weakest one only.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Personalised from your L01–L04 flags. One target each. *can/can't* is the priority if it is\n> still unclear — it changes meaning, and nothing else on this list does.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — التسجيل (milestone)\nRehearse with a partner against the checklist, swap, then record.\n\nTell the story of a trip you really took, in **7–9 sentences**. Include how you travelled, where you stayed,\ntwo things you did, **one request or one rule**, and how it felt. Then answer one question about it.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it and keep it.** 👑\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min recording + 2 feedback.** Ask your live follow-up **after** the recording and unscripted — *\"Would\n> you go back? Who paid?\"* — so it is genuine interaction.\n> **Success check (Unit-8):** a connected 7–9 sentence narration with correct past forms, accurate travel\n> language, one request or rule folded in, and a handled follow-up (rubric: Fluency + Accuracy +\n> Pronunciation at A2).\n> **Compare it with their Unit-0 clip if you have it.** This is the point in Stage 1 where the learner can\n> hear their own progress, and hearing it is worth more than being told.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- **Post your trip narration.**\n- **Complete the Unit-8 quiz.**\n- **Keep your maps app in English.** You started that in L01. Two weeks of it and station vocabulary stops\n  being vocabulary and becomes furniture.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر (Unit 8 wrap)\n- ***How do I get to…?*** — and the answer is **take the …** → **get off at …**\n- **by bus** *or* **take the bus** — never crossed. **on foot**, not *by foot*.\n- **Could I…?** = permission · **Could you…?** = a request · and the rise ↗️ makes it polite.\n- **have to** = required · **don't have to** = not required · **can't** = forbidden.\n- Five story slots: **when/where · how · where you stayed · what you did · how it felt.**\n- **can** /kən/ against **can't** /kænt/ — release the /t/.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — Unit 8 mini-quiz\n1. *How do I ___ to the station?* → **get**\n2. *I go to work ___ bus.* → **by**\n3. *I go ___ foot.* → **on**\n4. *___ you help me with my bag?* (a request) → **Could**\n5. *___ I sit here?* (permission) → **Can / Could**\n6. *You ___ smoke here.* (forbidden) → **can't**\n7. *You ___ book — there's plenty of space.* (not necessary) → **don't have to**\n8. *I didn't ___ to the museum.* → **go**\n9. A ticket there and back is a ___. → **return**\n10. *There ___ a lot of tourists.* → **were**\n\n---\n\n## 🎉 Unit 8 complete — بقيت تعرف توصل وترجع وتحكي\nYou can get around a city you have never seen, ask a stranger for help politely, buy a ticket, understand\nwhat is required and what is forbidden — and afterwards, tell the whole story in the past tense.\n\n**Next → Unit 9: People, Feelings & Everyday Situations.** Describing the people around you, saying how you\nreally feel, and handling the small situations that fill an ordinary day. 👑\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — توصل، وتطلب بأدب، وتحكي الرحلة بعد كده*\n","s1-u9-l01":"# Feelings & Personality: *\"What's He Like?\"*\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 9 · **Lesson 1**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: Unit 8 · Stage-0 adjectives · Unit 1 (feelings)\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit9-people-feelings-everyday.md` · **S1-U9-L01***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 6 · Your Turn 3 · Feedback 1.\n> **Objectives:** describe feelings and basic personality · ask and answer *How do you feel?* and *What's he like?* · describe someone you know.\n> **Two confusions carry this lesson, and both are worth real time.**\n> **1) *What's he like?* against *How is he?*** The first asks about **personality** — permanent. The second asks about **state** — right now. Learners answer *\"What's your brother like?\"* with *\"He's fine\"*, which is a non-answer, and they cannot see why.\n> **2) *-ed* against *-ing* adjectives.** *I'm bored* means you feel nothing is happening. ***I'm boring* means you are a dull person.*** This is the single most embarrassing error at A2 and learners make it constantly. Teach the rule as a direction: **-ed** is how **I** feel, **-ing** is what the **thing** does to me.\n> **Support:** feelings and personality flashcards plus the frames. **Stretch:** add *because* and a comparison from Unit 5.\n> **Arabic ~25%** — mostly English now.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Say how you feel, precisely — not just *good* or *bad*.\n- Describe what someone **is like** as a person.\n- Hear the difference between *bored* and *boring*. 🎙️\n\n> بلاش تقف عند *good* و*bad*. الوحدة دي بتوسّع الكلام عن الإحساس والشخصية.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nMost learners have two words for how they feel: *good* and *tired*. But feelings are the most common thing\npeople talk about, and English separates *feeling* something from *being* something. Get that separation and\nyou also stop making the one mistake that gets a laugh in the wrong way.\n\n> والفرق مهم: **bored** زهقان، و**boring** شخص ممل.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom Unit 1: *How was your day?* From Unit 7: two words for how your body feels. Answer both about today.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Unit 7 gave them *feel* + adjective; today extends the adjective list and adds personality.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nTwo short exchanges (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **A:** **How do you feel** about the exam tomorrow?\n> **B:** A bit **nervous**, honestly. But also kind of **excited**.\n> **A:** You'll be fine. Hey — **what's** your new manager **like**?\n> **B:** She's really **kind**, and very **hard-working**. A bit **serious**, but **friendly** when you know her.\n> **A:** And **how is** she today? I heard she was ill.\n> **B:** Much better, thanks.\n\n**Notice the two different questions.** *What's she **like**?* asked about her **personality** — and the\nanswer was *kind, hard-working, serious*. *How **is** she?* asked about **right now** — and the answer was\n*better*. Different questions, different answers.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| How do you feel? | حاسس بإيه؟ |\n| a bit nervous | متوتر شوية |\n| What's she like? | شخصيتها إيه؟ |\n| How is she? | عاملة إيه؟ (دلوقتي) |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. On the second pass learners label each question *personality* or *now*. That sort\n> is the lesson.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**1) Feelings — how you are right now.**\n\n> *I **am** nervous. · I **feel** nervous.* — both correct, both natural\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| good ones | *happy · excited · relaxed · proud* |\n| hard ones | *sad · nervous · worried · angry · bored · tired* |\n\n**Soften or strengthen with one word:** *a bit tired · really happy · so excited*\n\n**2) Personality — what someone is always like.**\n\n> *He **is** kind. · She **is** hard-working.*\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| warm | *kind · funny · friendly · generous* |\n| serious | *serious · hard-working · quiet · shy* |\n\n**3) The two questions people confuse.**\n\n| Question | Asks about | Answer |\n|---|---|---|\n| ***What's* he *like*?** | personality — always | *He's funny and kind.* |\n| ***How is* he?** | state — now | *He's fine. · He's much better.* |\n\n> ⚠️ **خد بالك:** لو حد سألك *\"What's your brother like?\"* وقلت *\"He's fine\"*، تكون ما جاوبتش. هو بيسأل عن\n> شخصيته، مش عن حالته.\n\n**4) *-ed* or *-ing*? This is the important one.**\n\n| | Means | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **-ed** | how **I** feel | *I'm **bored**. · I'm **interested**.* |\n| **-ing** | what the **thing** does | *The film is **boring**. · The book is **interesting**.* |\n\n> ⚠️ **الغلطة المشهورة:** *\"I'm boring\"* معناها إنك شخص ممل، مش إنك زهقان. اللي بيحسّ ياخد **-ed**، واللي\n> بيسبّب الإحساس ياخد **-ing**.\n\n| I feel | The thing is |\n|---|---|\n| bored · tired · excited | boring · tiring · exciting |\n| worried · interested | worrying · interesting |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Two minutes on the adjective sets, two on *What…like?*, two on *-ed/-ing*. For the last one,\n> the fastest fix is a forced choice: point at a learner, say *\"boring\"*, and have them tell you whether that\n> describes the person or the film.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| nervous | متوتر | *I'm a bit nervous.* |\n| excited | متحمس | *I'm so excited!* |\n| worried | قلقان | *She's worried about the exam.* |\n| bored / boring | زهقان / ممل | *I'm bored. The film is boring.* |\n| kind | طيب | *He's very kind.* |\n| funny | بيضحّك | *My sister is really funny.* |\n| shy | خجول | *He's shy with new people.* |\n| hard-working | مجتهد | *She's hard-working.* |\n| What's he like? | شخصيته إيه؟ | *What's your teacher like?* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Let the voice carry the feeling.** At A2 the words are usually right and the delivery is flat, so the\nfeeling never arrives. English marks emotion with **pitch** and **length**, not extra words.\n\n> *I'm **so** ex-**CI**-ted!* — high, and stretch the stressed syllable\n> *I'm a bit **wor**-ried.* — lower, quieter, shorter\n\nSay *\"I'm excited\"* three times: bored, normal, thrilled. Same three words. If a listener cannot tell them\napart, the sentence is not doing its job.\n\n> جرب تقول نفس الجملة بتلات إحساسات مختلفة. الكلمات مش بتتغير، الصوت هو اللي بيتغير.\n\n**And keep the endings on.** These adjectives are long, and the ends are where the meaning sits:\n\n> *nervous* /ˈnɜːvəs/ · *worried* /ˈwʌrid/ · *interested* /ˈɪntrəstɪd/ — three syllables, not four\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"I'm really excited, but I'm also a bit nervous.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** The three-emotions-same-sentence drill is the one to keep. It works instantly and learners\n> remember it.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — *-ed* or *-ing*?**\n1. That lesson was very ______. (interest)\n2. I'm ______ in history. (interest)\n3. The journey was ______. (tire)\n4. I'm ______ — I need to sleep. (tire)\n\n**Exercise 2 — *What's he like?* or *How is he?***\n1. You want to know about your friend's new flatmate's personality. → __________________\n2. Your friend has been ill and you want to know about today. → __________________\n\n**Exercise 3 — fix the mistake.**\n1. *I'm boring in this class.* → __________________________\n2. *— What's your sister like? — She's fine, thanks.* → __________________________\n3. *I'm exciting about the trip.* → __________________________\n4. *He is a hard-working person and very kindly.* → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 4 — true for you.** Describe **one person** you know in two sentences: personality, plus one\nreason with *because*.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** **Ex1:** interesting · interested · tiring · tired. **Ex2:** *What's he like?* · *How is he?*\n> **Ex3:** *bored* · *She's funny and kind* · *excited* · *kind*. Ex3.2 is the one to discuss — ask why\n> *\"She's fine\"* does not answer the question.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nSay **how you feel today** and why, then describe **one person you know** — two personality adjectives and a\nreason.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~3 min.** **Success check:** a precise feeling (not *good*), two personality adjectives, correct\n> *-ed/-ing* if either appears, and audible intonation on the feeling.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post: *\"Today I feel ___ because ___. My friend is ___ and ___.\"*\n- **Name your feeling in English first this week.** When something happens, reach for the English word before\n  the Arabic one. That is the actual beginning of thinking in English — not a technique, just a habit of\n  reaching in a different order.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- ***I am*** *nervous* and ***I feel*** *nervous* are both fine.\n- ***What's* he *like*?** = personality. ***How is* he?** = right now.\n- **-ed** = how I feel · **-ing** = what the thing does to me.\n- ***I'm boring*** means you are dull. Say ***I'm bored***.\n- Pitch and length carry the feeling — flat delivery loses it.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 1) interesting · 2) interested · 3) tiring · 4) tired\n- **Ex2:** 1) *What's he like?* · 2) *How is he?*\n- **Ex3:** 1) *I'm **bored** in this class.* · 2) *She's funny and kind.* · 3) *I'm **excited** about the trip.* · 4) *…and very **kind**.*\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — bored زهقان، boring ممل*\n","s1-u9-l02":"# Adverbs of Manner: \"She Speaks English Well\"\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 9 · **Lesson 2**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L01\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit9-people-feelings-everyday.md` · **S1-U9-L02***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 7 · Your Turn 3 · Feedback —.\n> **Objectives:** form adverbs of manner (*-ly* plus the irregulars) · say **how** someone does something · describe how you do daily activities.\n> ***good* against *well* is the classic.** ❌ *\"I speak English good.\"* It survives to C1 if it is not killed at A2, because it is never misunderstood — so nobody corrects it. Drill *well* as the adverb until it is automatic.\n> **Watch the trap: *hardly* is not the adverb of *hard*.** *He works hard* = a lot. *He hardly works* = almost not at all. Opposite meanings. Teach *hard* as its own irregular and flag *hardly* explicitly, or a learner will build it by rule and say the reverse of what they mean.\n> **Position matters and is easy:** after the verb, or after the whole object. *He speaks English well*, not ❌ *\"He speaks well English\"*.\n> **Support:** the adjective↔adverb card. **Stretch:** combine with a Unit-5 comparative — *\"I speak more slowly than my brother.\"*\n> **Arabic ~25%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Turn an adjective into an adverb.\n- Say **how** someone does something: *carefully · quickly · well*.\n- Stop saying *\"I speak English good.\"* 🎙️\n\n> الدرس ده بيضيف طبقة على كلامك: مش بس إيه اللي بيحصل، لكن بيحصل إزاي.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\n*He drives* tells me a fact. *He drives **carefully*** tells me about him. One word, and the sentence carries\ninformation about character, effort, and skill.\n\n> والغلطة المستهدفة: *\"I speak English good\"*. لأنها مفهومة، ممكن تفضل سنين.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom L01: three feeling adjectives and three personality adjectives, said with matching intonation.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Those adjectives are today's raw material — most of them will take *-ly* in ten minutes.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nDescribing a colleague (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **A:** What's Omar like at work?\n> **B:** He's great. He speaks English **well** — really **well**, actually — and he works very **hard**.\n> **A:** Is he fast?\n> **B:** He doesn't work **quickly**, he works **carefully**. He reads everything **slowly** and he **hardly\n> ever** makes a mistake.\n> **A:** That sounds better than fast.\n> **B:** Much better.\n\n**Notice where the adverbs sit:** *speaks English **well**· works **hard** · reads everything **slowly***.\nThey come **after** the verb, or after the whole object — never between them.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| He speaks English well. | بيتكلم إنجليزي كويس |\n| He works hard. | بيشتغل بجد |\n| carefully | بعناية |\n| slowly | ببطء |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second pass: learners underline every adverb and mark what comes immediately before\n> it. The position rule emerges from the data rather than being announced.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**1) The rule: adjective + *-ly*.**\n\n| Adjective | Adverb |\n|---|---|\n| quick | quick**ly** |\n| careful | careful**ly** |\n| quiet | quiet**ly** |\n| bad | bad**ly** |\n\n**Two spelling changes:**\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| *-y* → *-ily* | happy → happ**ily** · easy → eas**ily** |\n| *-ble* → *-bly* | possible → possi**bly** |\n\n**2) Three irregulars — no *-ly* at all.**\n\n| Adjective | Adverb | |\n|---|---|---|\n| good | **well** | *She sings well.* |\n| fast | **fast** | *He drives fast.* |\n| hard | **hard** | *They work hard.* |\n\n> ⚠️ **دي الغلطة الكلاسيكية:** *good* صفة، و**well** هي الظرف. بنقول *I speak English **well***، مش\n> *English good*.\n\n**3) One word to be careful with:** ***hardly*** exists, but it does **not** mean *hard*.\n\n| | Means |\n|---|---|\n| *He works **hard**.* | a lot 💪 |\n| *He **hardly** works.* | almost never ❌ |\n\n> ⚠️ **دي فخ حقيقي:** **hardly** معناها \"تقريبًا مش\"، فهي عكس **hard** بالظبط. متبنيهاش بالقاعدة.\n\n**4) Where it goes:** after the verb, or after the whole object.\n\n> *He speaks **well**.* ✅ · *He speaks English **well**.* ✅ · *He speaks **well** English.* ❌\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Two minutes forming adverbs, two on *good/well*, one on *hardly*, one on position. Do\n> *good/well* as rapid-fire oral correction — they need to hear themselves say *well* many times.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| well | كويس (ظرف) | *She speaks English well.* |\n| carefully | بعناية | *Drive carefully.* |\n| quickly | بسرعة | *He eats too quickly.* |\n| slowly | ببطء | *Please speak slowly.* |\n| quietly | بهدوء | *She came in quietly.* |\n| badly | بشكل سيئ | *I slept badly.* |\n| hard | بجد | *They work hard.* |\n| easily | بسهولة | *I found it easily.* |\n| hardly | تقريبًا مش | *I hardly know him.* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Part 1 — *-ly* is a real syllable, and it disappears first.** Under speed, learners drop it and the adverb\nturns back into an adjective:\n\n> *care-ful-**ly*** — three syllables · *quick-**ly*** — two · *eas-i-**ly*** — three\n\nSay *carefully* and count on your fingers. If you get two, the *-ly* is being swallowed.\n\n**Part 2 — the stress does not move to the adverb.** The verb keeps the emphasis:\n\n> *She **SPEAKS** English well.* ✅\n> *She speaks English **WELL**.* — only if you are correcting someone\n\n> ولاحظ إن النبرة بتفضل على الفعل. الظرف بييجي بعده هادي، مش مضروب.\n\n**And a word worth its own moment:** *well* is one syllable with a clean /w/ — not *\"vell\"*.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"He works very hard and he speaks English really well.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Finger-counting syllables is the fastest way to expose a swallowed *-ly*. Two minutes there,\n> two on keeping stress on the verb.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — make the adverb.**\n1. slow → __________ 2. happy → __________ 3. good → __________\n4. careful → __________ 5. fast → __________ 6. easy → __________\n\n**Exercise 2 — *good* or *well*?**\n1. She's a ______ driver.\n2. She drives ______.\n3. My English isn't very ______.\n4. I don't speak French ______.\n\n**Exercise 3 — fix the mistake.**\n1. *I speak English good.* → __________________________\n2. *He speaks well English.* → __________________________\n3. *She drives very careful.* → __________________________\n4. *He works hardly every day.* (he works a lot) → __________________________\n5. *I sleeped very bad last night.* → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 4 — true for you.** Say **how** you do three things: drive, work, and speak English.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~7 min.** **Ex1:** slowly · happily · well · carefully · fast · easily. **Ex2:** good · well · good ·\n> well. **Ex3:** *well* · *speaks English well* · *carefully* · *works hard* · *slept badly*.\n> Ex3.4 is the one to slow down on — ask what the original sentence actually said.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nDescribe **how** you do three daily things, and **how one person you know** does something — four sentences,\nfour adverbs. Use *well* at least once.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~3 min.** **Success check:** correctly formed adverbs, correct position after the object, and *well* used\n> as an adverb rather than *good*.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post three sentences about yourself using three different adverbs.\n- **You do not need to speak fast — you need to speak clearly.** Speed is what beginners think fluency is.\n  Clarity is what listeners actually reward. Slow and understood beats fast and repeated.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- adjective + **-ly** → adverb. *happy → happily · easy → easily*.\n- Irregulars: **good → well · fast → fast · hard → hard.**\n- **hardly** ≠ **hard**. *He hardly works* means almost never.\n- Position: after the verb, or after the object. Never *speaks **well** English*.\n- **-ly** is a syllable — keep it alive at speed.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** slowly · happily · well · carefully · fast · easily\n- **Ex2:** 1) good · 2) well · 3) good · 4) well\n- **Ex3:** 1) *…English **well**.* · 2) *He speaks English **well**.* · 3) *…very **carefully**.* · 4) *He works **hard** every day.* · 5) *I **slept** very **badly** last night.*\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — good صفة، well ظرف*\n","s1-u9-l03":"# *Usually* vs *Now*: Present Simple vs Continuous\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 9 · **Lesson 3**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L02 · Stage-0 U4 & U9\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit9-people-feelings-everyday.md` · **S1-U9-L03***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 7 · Your Turn 3 · Feedback —.\n> **Objectives:** contrast habit (present simple) with now (present continuous) · use the time markers · describe what you usually do against what you are doing these days.\n> **What is new here is not the continuous — it is *temporary*.** Stage 0 taught the continuous for this second (*I'm eating*). This lesson extends it to a **temporary period**: *this week I'm working from home*. That extension is the whole lesson, and it is the use learners never acquire by themselves.\n> **Anchor the choice to the time marker, not to a feeling about the tense.** Arabic speakers reliably over-use the simple for now. *Usually / every day / always* → simple. *Now / at the moment / this week / today* → continuous. Give them the marker and the tense follows; that is a rule they can actually apply under pressure.\n> **The *-ing* ending is /ŋ/, with no hard /g/.** *Working*, not *\"workingg\"*. It appears in every sentence of this lesson, so it is worth the four minutes.\n> **Support:** the sort activity plus the time-marker list. **Stretch:** add a reason and a feeling about the change.\n> **Arabic ~25%.**\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Say what you **usually** do, and what you're doing **right now**.\n- Talk about a **temporary** period: *these days · this week*.\n- Choose the tense from the time word. 🎙️\n\n> ودي أول مرة تقدر تفرق بين حاجة بتعملها دايمًا وحاجة بتعملها الفترة دي بس.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nEnglish forces a choice that Arabic does not always force: is this your **life**, or is this **today**?\n*I work in an office* is who you are. *I'm working from home* is this week only. Same job, different sentence.\n\n> والمفتاح إنك تسمع كلمة الوقت وتمشي وراها: **usually** يبقى بسيط، و**now** أو **this week** يبقى مستمر.\n> متختارش بالإحساس.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom Stage 0: two things you do every day (simple), and what your partner is doing right now (continuous).\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** This is the baseline check. If the *this second* continuous is shaky, fix it here — today builds\n> directly on top of it.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nCatching up with someone (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **A:** What do you do?\n> **B:** I'm a teacher. I **usually work** in a school in Giza.\n> **A:** Usually?\n> **B:** Well, **this month I'm working** online — they're repairing the building. **At the moment I'm\n> teaching** from my bedroom.\n> **A:** How is that?\n> **B:** Strange! **I normally see** thirty students in a room. **These days I'm looking** at thirty little\n> squares.\n> **A:** And do you like it?\n> **B:** Not really. But **it's only** for a few weeks.\n\n**Notice:** *usually work · normally see* — his real life. *this month I'm working · at the moment I'm\nteaching · these days I'm looking* — a temporary situation, and every one of them is marked by a time phrase.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| I usually work in a school. | عادةً بشتغل في مدرسة |\n| This month I'm working online. | الشهر ده بشتغل أونلاين |\n| At the moment… | في الوقت الحالي… |\n| These days… | الأيام دي… |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second pass: learners write the time phrase from each sentence and the tense next\n> to it. The pairing is the takeaway.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**1) Present simple — your life. What is generally true.**\n\n> *I **work** in an office. · She **drinks** tea every morning.*\n\n**Its time words:** *usually · normally · always · often · every day · never*\n\n**2) Present continuous — *am/is/are* + *-ing*. Two jobs.**\n\n| Job | Example |\n|---|---|\n| happening **this second** | *I**'m talking** to you now.* |\n| happening for a **temporary period** ⭐ | ***This week** I**'m working** from home.* |\n\n**Its time words:** *now · right now · at the moment · today · this week · these days*\n\n> ⚠️ **الجديد في الدرس هو التاني:** المستمر مش بس للحظة دي، هو كمان لفترة مؤقتة. *This month I'm working\n> online* — الشهر ده بس، مش دايمًا. ولو الحالة مدتها شهر كامل، برضه مؤقتة؛ المهم إنها مش حياتك العادية\n> وهتنتهي. كلمة الوقت هي اللي بتكشف الفرق.\n\n**3) The pair sentence — this is the shape to master.**\n\n> *I **usually** *****drive***** to work, **but this week** I**'m taking** the metro.*\n> *She **normally** *****cooks*****, **but today** she**'s ordering** food.*\n\n**Habit + *but* + temporary.** One sentence, both tenses, and the contrast does the work.\n\n**4) A small warning.** Some verbs describe a state, not an action, and they stay simple even with *now*:\n\n> *I **know** him.* ✅ · *I'm knowing him.* ❌\n> *I **want** coffee.* ✅ · *I'm wanting coffee.* ❌\n\n**The common ones:** *know · want · like · need · understand · have* (own)\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Two minutes on the two tenses, two on the temporary use, two on the pair sentence. Keep the\n> stative-verb note to a list of six and move on — recognition is enough at A2.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| usually / normally | عادةً | *I usually get up at seven.* |\n| every day | كل يوم | *I study every day.* |\n| at the moment | في الوقت الحالي | *At the moment I'm studying.* |\n| these days | الأيام دي | *These days I'm reading more.* |\n| this week / this month | الأسبوع ده / الشهر ده | *This week I'm working late.* |\n| right now | حالًا | *He's sleeping right now.* |\n| I'm working from home. | بشتغل من البيت | *This month I'm working from home.* |\n| I know / I want | أعرف / عايز | *I know him well.* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Part 1 — *-ing* is /ŋ/, and there is no /g/ at the end.**\n\nThe back of your tongue closes against the soft palate and **stays** there. You do not release it into a *g*.\n\n> *work-ing* /ˈwɜːkɪŋ/ · *go-ing* /ˈɡəʊɪŋ/ · *do-ing* /ˈduːɪŋ/\n\n**The test:** say *\"sing\"* and hold the last sound. If you hear *\"sing-guh\"*, you released it. Freeze instead.\n\n> ودي بتغير شكل كلامك كله، لأن كل جملة في الدرس ده فيها **-ing**.\n\n**Part 2 — stress the time marker when you are contrasting.** The contrast is the meaning:\n\n> *I **USUALLY** drive, but **THIS WEEK** I'm taking the metro.*\n\nSay it once flat, then again with those two phrases pushed. The second version makes the contrast audible\nwithout any extra words.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"I usually work in an office, but these days I'm working from home.\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Two minutes on /ŋ/ with the hold-and-freeze test, two on contrastive stress. Both are\n> immediately audible in the recording, so flag them there.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — simple or continuous?**\n1. I ______ (get up) at six every day.\n2. Look — it ______ (rain)!\n3. She ______ (usually / take) the bus, but today she ______ (walk).\n4. These days I ______ (study) for an exam.\n5. He ______ (know) the answer.\n\n**Exercise 2 — make a pair sentence.** Use *usually … but this week …* for:\n1. drive / take the metro 2. cook at home / eat out 3. work in the office / work from home\n\n**Exercise 3 — fix the mistake.**\n1. *I am working in a bank. It's my job for ten years.* → __________________________\n2. *Every day I am going to the gym.* → __________________________\n3. *I'm wanting a new phone.* → __________________________\n4. *At the moment I study English.* → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 4 — true for you.** Two habits, then two things you're doing **these days** that are not permanent.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~7 min.** **Ex1:** get up · is raining · usually takes / is walking · am studying · knows.\n> **Ex3:** *I **work** in a bank* · *Every day I **go*** · *I **want*** · *At the moment I**'m studying***.\n> Ex3.1 is the most useful — a permanent job needs the simple, however \"now\" it feels.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nSay **two things you usually do**, then **two things you're doing these days** that will not last. Use a time\nmarker in every sentence.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~3 min.** **Success check:** correct tense for each time marker, clean /ŋ/ endings, and at least one full\n> *usually … but …* pair sentence.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post: *\"I usually ___, but these days I'm ___.\"*\n- **Say your current habit out loud in English.** *\"These days I'm practising every day.\"* Describing the\n  habit in the language you are building is a small thing that makes it feel like yours.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- **Simple** = your life. **Continuous** = now, **or a temporary period**.\n- *usually · every day · always* → simple. *now · at the moment · this week · these days* → continuous.\n- The pair sentence: *I **usually** X, **but this week** I**'m** Y-ing.*\n- *know · want · like · need · understand* stay simple.\n- *-ing* ends in /ŋ/ — freeze it, do not release a /g/.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** 1) get up · 2) is raining · 3) usually takes / is walking · 4) am studying · 5) knows\n- **Ex3:** 1) *I **work** in a bank.* · 2) *Every day I **go** to the gym.* · 3) *I **want** a new phone.* · 4) *At the moment I**'m studying** English.*\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — كلمة الوقت هي اللي تختار الزمن*\n","s1-u9-l04":"# On the Phone & Small Talk\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 9 · **Lesson 4**\n⏱️ ~30 min · 📹 Recorded + live · Prerequisite: L01–L03\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit9-people-feelings-everyday.md` · **S1-U9-L04***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~30 min):** Warm-up 4 · Input 6 · Decode 6 · Accent Lab 4 · Train 7 · Your Turn 3 · Feedback —.\n> **Objectives:** manage a simple phone call · leave or take a message · open, sustain, and close light small talk naturally.\n> **Teach chunks, not grammar.** A phone call is one of the few situations where people use nearly identical fixed phrases every time. Fluency here comes from retrieving a whole line — *Can I speak to…?* — not constructing it word by word.\n> **The missing visual channel is the difficulty.** Learners cannot point, read a face, or repair with a gesture. Give them explicit repair phrases: *Could you say that again? Could you speak more slowly?*\n> **Small talk has a social shape:** open → answer + return question → one follow-up → close. Learners who answer *How's it going?* with *Fine* and silence are grammatically correct but conversationally unavailable. The return question is the lesson.\n> **Support:** phone-stage and small-talk phrase cards. **Stretch:** handle an unavailable person and leave a detailed message with name, number, and reason.\n> **Arabic ~20%** — fixed English phrases should carry the lesson.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Ask for someone and leave or take a message on the phone.\n- Start small talk, ask back, and react naturally.\n- Close a conversation without sounding abrupt. 🎙️\n\n> المكالمة ليها ترتيب ثابت. لما تحفظ الترتيب، مش محتاج تترجم وإنت بتتكلم.\n\n---\n\n## 👑 Why this matters — ليه ده مهم\nOn the phone, your English loses its safety net: no face, no pointing, no gesture. But the conversation is\nmore predictable than it feels. Learn its stages and the pressure drops.\n\nSmall talk works the same way. It is not empty speech; it is how people signal *I am open to talking to you*.\nOne returned question changes the whole exchange.\n\n> الرد لوحده مش كفاية. جاوب واسأل نفس السؤال للشخص التاني.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن\nFrom L01: say how you feel and ask your partner how they feel. From Unit 1: ask *How was your weekend?*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Require an answer plus a return question. That interaction pattern becomes today's small-talk\n> engine.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — اسمع وشوف\nA short phone call (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **A:** Hello, Empire Travel. How can I help?\n> **B:** Hi. **Can I speak to** Ms Hassan, please?\n> **A:** **Who's calling**, please?\n> **B:** It's Kareem Ali.\n> **A:** Thanks. **Hold on** a moment… I'm sorry, she's not available right now. **Can I take a message?**\n> **B:** Yes, please. Could you ask her to call me back about my booking?\n> **A:** Of course. Does she have your number?\n> **B:** Yes, she does. Thanks for your help.\n> **A:** You're welcome. Goodbye.\n\nLater, two colleagues meet:\n\n> **C:** Hey, **how's it going**?\n> **D:** Not bad, thanks. **How about you?**\n> **C:** Pretty good. **How was your weekend?**\n> **D:** Quiet, but nice. I visited my family. What did you do?\n> **C:** I worked, unfortunately! **Anyway, I have to go. Good talking to you.**\n> **D:** You too. See you later.\n\n**Notice the work done by fixed lines:** the phone call moves through open → ask → message → close. The small\ntalk answer always comes back with a question.\n\n| English | بالعربي |\n|---|---|\n| Can I speak to…? | ممكن أكلم…؟ |\n| Who's calling? | مين بيتكلم؟ |\n| Can I take a message? | أبلغه حاجة؟ |\n| How's it going? | الدنيا ماشية إزاي؟ |\n| Anyway, I have to go. | عمومًا، لازم أمشي |\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Play twice. Second pass: learners identify each phone stage and circle every return question.\n> Do not translate every phrase — these are memorize-and-use chunks.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Decode it — القاعدة\n\n**A phone call has four stages.**\n\n| Stage | Useful line |\n|---|---|\n| **1 Open** | *Hello, this is Noor.* |\n| **2 Ask** | *Can I speak to Ahmed, please?* |\n| **3 Connect or message** | *Hold on. · Can I take a message?* |\n| **4 Close** | *Thanks for your help. Goodbye.* |\n\n**If the person is unavailable:**\n\n> *I'm sorry, she isn't available right now.*\n> *Could you ask her to call me back?*\n> *Can I leave a message?*\n\n**If you did not hear:**\n\n> *Could you say that again? · Could you speak more slowly? · Did you say fifteen or fifty?*\n\n> استخدام جملة إصلاح مش فشل. دي مهارة المكالمة نفسها. ولو مسمعتش، اطلب الإعادة بدل ما تخمّن وتضيّع\n> الرسالة أو الرقم المهم.\n\n**Small talk has four moves too.**\n\n| Move | Example |\n|---|---|\n| open | *How's it going? · How was your weekend?* |\n| answer | *Not bad. · Pretty good. · A bit busy.* |\n| **ask back** ⭐ | *How about you? · And you?* |\n| close | *Anyway, I have to go. · Good talking to you.* |\n\n**The rule that makes it a conversation:** answer + **ask back**.\n\n> *How's it going?* — *Pretty good, thanks. **How about you?***\n\nA one-word answer is not rude, but it closes the door. The returned question keeps it open.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~6 min.** Keep the phone and small-talk stages visible. Drill complete chunks, then remove one line at a\n> time from the board. Spend the final two minutes forcing answer-plus-return-question pairs.\n\n---\n\n## 🗡️ Your Arsenal — ذخيرتك\n\n| English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Can I speak to…? | ممكن أكلم…؟ | *Can I speak to Lina?* |\n| Who's calling? | مين بيتكلم؟ | *Who's calling, please?* |\n| Hold on. | استنى لحظة | *Hold on a moment.* |\n| available | متاح | *He isn't available right now.* |\n| take / leave a message | ياخد / يسيب رسالة | *Can I leave a message?* |\n| call me back | يكلمني تاني | *Could you ask her to call me back?* |\n| How's it going? | أخبارك إيه؟ | *Hey, how's it going?* |\n| How about you? | وإنت؟ | *Not bad. How about you?* |\n| Good talking to you. | اتبسطت بالكلام معاك | *Good talking to you. Bye.* |\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — معمل النطق\n\n**Fixed phrases need one rhythm, not one beat per word.**\n\n> *How's it **GO**ing?* → /haʊzɪtˈɡəʊɪŋ/\n> *Can I **SPEAK** to Ahmed?* → the small words compress\n> *Could you ask her to **CALL me BACK**?* → stress the action\n\nSay *How · is · it · going?* with four separate beats. It sounds like a language exercise. Now let the first\nthree words slide together and land on **go**. That sounds like a greeting.\n\n**Friendly intonation rises into the question and relaxes through the answer.** A flat *How's it going?*\nsounds as if you do not want to know.\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare:** *\"Hi, can I speak to Mariam, please?\"* Then: *\"How's it going? Pretty good — how about you?\"*\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Contrast mechanical word-by-word delivery with chunk rhythm. Prioritise fluent delivery and\n> warmth over analysis; every line here is functional language.\n\n---\n\n## 🏋️ Train — تدرّب\n\n**Exercise 1 — put the phone call in order.**\n- a) *Can I take a message?*\n- b) *Can I speak to Mr Adel, please?*\n- c) *Thanks for your help. Goodbye.*\n- d) *Hello, this is Rania from Empire English.*\n\n**Exercise 2 — choose the line.**\n1. You did not hear the number. → __________________________\n2. The person is not there. Offer to record information. → __________________________\n3. You want the person to contact you later. → __________________________\n4. You need two seconds to connect the call. → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 3 — keep the small talk alive.** Add an answer **and** a return question.\n1. *How's it going?* → __________________________\n2. *How was your weekend?* → __________________________\n\n**Exercise 4 — close naturally.** Replace the abrupt ending.\n> *OK. Bye.* → _________________________________________________\n\n**Exercise 5 — role-play.** Caller: ask for Salma and leave your name, number, and reason. Receiver: she is\nnot available; take the message. Swap roles.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~7 min.** **Ex1:** d → b → a → c. **Ex2 models:** *Could you say the number again? · Can I take a\n> message? · Could you ask them to call me back? · Hold on a moment.* **Ex4:** *Anyway, I have to go. Good\n> talking to you.* In Ex5, remove the phrase card for the second round.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — دورك\nMake a short phone call: introduce yourself, ask for someone, learn they are unavailable, and leave a message\nwith your name and reason. Close naturally.\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it.**\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~3 min.** **Success check:** coherent open → ask → message → close, at least three correct fixed phrases,\n> and natural chunk rhythm rather than word-by-word reading.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- Post a short phone-call voice note **or** a small-talk opener, response, return question, and closer.\n- Memorise three complete lines, not twelve separate words. Functional English gets fast when the unit of\n  memory is the phrase.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر\n- Phone shape: **open → ask → connect/message → close**.\n- *Can I speak to…? · Who's calling? · Hold on. · Can I take a message?*\n- Repair is a skill: *Could you say that again?*\n- Small talk: **open → answer + ask back → follow-up → close**.\n- *Anyway, I have to go. Good talking to you.* closes without cutting someone off.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — صحّح لنفسك\n- **Ex1:** d → b → a → c\n- **Ex2:** 1) *Could you say the number again?* · 2) *Can I take a message?* · 3) *Could you ask them to call me back?* · 4) *Hold on a moment.*\n- **Ex4:** *Anyway, I have to go. Good talking to you.*\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — جاوب واسأل، متقفلش الكلام عندك*\n","s1-u9-l05":"# Unit Speaking Task: A Two-Minute Chat About Your Week (+ Review)\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · Unit 9 · **Lesson 5** — *UNIT MILESTONE*\n⏱️ ~35 min · 📹 Recorded + live (assessment-style) · Prerequisite: L01–L04\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit9-people-feelings-everyday.md` · **S1-U9-L05***\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **At a glance (~35 min):** Warm-up 5 · Model 4 · Checklist 4 · Accent clinic 4 · Rehearse 7 · Record 5 · Feedback 2 · Assign —.\n> **Objectives:** sustain a roughly two-minute chat · combine feelings, adverbs, and usually-versus-now · ask back, follow up, react, and close naturally · record the Unit-9 milestone.\n> **This is the most conversational task in Stage 1.** Score interaction as seriously as accuracy. A learner who produces perfect sentences without reacting is delivering parallel monologues, not having a conversation.\n> **The partner must introduce one unpredictable detail.** Without it, reactions can be memorised. A genuine *Really? Why?* after new information is stronger evidence of A2 than another correct present-continuous sentence.\n> **Do not rescue silence immediately.** Give learners two seconds to reach for a follow-up. Interaction needs retrieval time before it becomes automatic.\n> **Support:** checklist and phrase card. **Stretch:** sustain beyond two minutes with two genuine follow-ups and no teacher prompt.\n> **Arabic ~20%** — this should sound like English being used, not displayed.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Your Conquest — هدفك (milestone)\nBy the end, **you'll be able to:**\n- Hold a **two-minute conversation** about your week and how you feel.\n- Contrast what you usually do with what you are doing now.\n- Ask back, follow up, react, and close naturally.\n- **Record your Unit-9 milestone.** 🎙️\n\n> الهدف مش إنك تقول كلام كتير. الهدف إنك تسمع، ترد، وترجع تسأل.\n\n---\n\n## 🔁 Warm-up — سخّن (سلسلة)\nRapid chain: a feeling → a personality adjective → an adverb → a *usually … but these days …* sentence → a\nsmall-talk opener.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min.** No repeats. The chain retrieves every lesson before learners combine them under interaction\n> pressure.\n\n---\n\n## 👂 Watch & Listen — النموذج\nA natural chat between two classmates (📹 in the portal).\n\n> **A:** Hey, **how's it going**?\n> **B:** Pretty good, thanks. **How about you?**\n> **A:** A bit tired, honestly. I'm working late this week.\n> **B:** **Really? Why?**\n> **A:** We usually finish at five, but **these days we're preparing** a big event, so I'm leaving at eight.\n> **B:** That sounds tiring. Are you enjoying it?\n> **A:** Actually, yes. My team works **really well** together, and my manager explains everything **clearly**.\n> What about you — how's your week going?\n> **B:** Quiet. I normally study at home, but **this week I'm studying** with a friend. It's much better.\n> **A:** Nice! Does your friend study quietly?\n> **B:** Not at all — she talks constantly! Anyway, I have to go. **Good talking to you.**\n> **A:** You too. See you tomorrow.\n\n**Notice what makes this a conversation:** each person answers, asks back, reacts to new information, and\nfollows it. The grammar is accurate, but the interaction is what keeps the two minutes alive.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Play once without interruption. Second pass: learners count return questions, reactions, and\n> follow-ups. There are no speeches longer than three sentences.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 The Checklist — تشيك ليست المحادثة\n1. ✅ **Open naturally** — *How's it going? · How's your week going?*\n2. ✅ **Name a real feeling** and say why\n3. ✅ Use one **adverb** — *well · carefully · slowly · clearly*\n4. ✅ Contrast **usually** with **this week / these days**\n5. ✅ **Ask back** — *How about you?*\n6. ✅ **React + follow up** — *Really? Why? · That sounds… · What happened?*\n7. ✅ **Close naturally** — *Anyway, I have to go. Good talking to you.*\n8. ⭐ Handle one detail you did not know before the conversation\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** Items 5, 6, and 8 are the assessment heart. Grammar can be rehearsed; interaction only appears\n> when the learner listens and responds.\n\n---\n\n## 🎙️ Accent Lab — عيادة النطق\nDrill **your** weakest from this unit:\n\n- feelings carried by pitch — *excited* should not sound *bored*\n- adjective endings — *interested · worried · nervous*\n- the **-ly** syllable — *carefully · clearly · slowly*\n- **-ing** /ŋ/ with no released /g/\n- small-talk chunk rhythm — *How's it going? · How about you?*\n\nThen rehearse one reaction so it sounds real rather than read:\n\n> *Really?* ↗️ · *That sounds **exhausting**.* ↘️ · *What happened?* ↗️\n\n> 🎧 **Record & compare** one weak sound and one reaction.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~4 min.** One personal target per learner. Do not run all five as a class list. The reaction line matters\n> because flat reactions are the main reason a technically correct chat still sounds staged.\n\n---\n\n## 🎤 Your Turn — التسجيل (milestone)\nWith a partner, hold a chat for **about two minutes**.\n\nTalk about how you feel, how your week is going, what you usually do, and what is different these days. Use\none adverb. **Ask back, react to something new, ask a follow-up, and close naturally.**\n\n> 🎙️ **Record it and keep it.** 👑\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **~5 min recording + 2 feedback.** Secretly give each partner one detail to introduce — *a new job, a bad\n> journey, an exam tomorrow* — so the other's reaction cannot be scripted. Allow two seconds before\n> prompting a silence.\n> **Success check (Unit-9):** a coherent roughly two-minute chat containing a precise feeling, one correct\n> adverb, a simple/continuous contrast, and visible interaction through asking back, reacting, and following\n> up (rubric: Fluency + **Interaction** + Pronunciation at A2).\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Your Orders — مهمتك\n- **Post the chat** or your side of it with the partner questions left in.\n- **Complete the Unit-9 quiz.**\n- This week, do not measure success by how long you spoke. Count the moments when you understood something\n  new and responded to it. That is when English becomes conversation.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Remember — افتكر (Unit 9 wrap)\n- ***What's* someone *like*?** asks about personality; ***How are* they?** asks about now.\n- **-ed** is the feeling; **-ing** causes it: *bored / boring*.\n- adjective + **-ly**; but **good → well · fast → fast · hard → hard**.\n- **Simple** for the usual; **continuous** for now or a temporary period.\n- Small talk lives on **answer + ask back + react + follow up**.\n- A natural close is a phrase, not an escape: *Anyway, I have to go. Good talking to you.*\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Self-check — Unit 9 mini-quiz\n1. *What's your manager ___?* (personality) → **like**\n2. *I'm ___ because this film is ___.* (bore) → **bored / boring**\n3. *She speaks English ___.* (good) → **well**\n4. *He works very ___.* (hard) → **hard**\n5. *I usually ___ in an office, but this week I___ from home.* (work) → **work / 'm working**\n6. *At the moment she ___ for an exam.* (study) → **is studying**\n7. After *\"Pretty good, thanks\"*, keep the talk open with: → **How about you?**\n8. Ask to record information for an unavailable person: → **Can I take a message?**\n\n---\n\n## 🎉 Unit 9 complete — بقيت بتتحاور، مش بس بتجاوب\nYou can describe people and feelings precisely, say how things happen, separate ordinary life from what is\ntemporary, manage a phone call, and sustain a conversation by listening and responding.\n\n**Next → Unit 10: Putting It Together.** The full A2 campaign: consolidation, real-world missions, and your\nStage-1 milestone recording. 👑\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 — المستخدم الحقيقي بيسمع ويرد، مش بس بيكوّن جملة*\n"};

/** Stage front matter, stage glossary, and each unit's campaign front matter. */
export const WRAPPER_MD: Record<string, string> = {"stage1-front-matter":"# 👑 Stage 1 — Elementary\n\n**Empire English** · CEFR **A2** · Rank: **Citizen**\n*Built from: `curriculum/01-cefr-roadmap.md` §4 + `curriculum/stage1/00-stage1-unit-map.md`*\n\n> **من وصف يومك إلى حكاية ماضيك وخططك وعالمك.**\n\n---\n\n## Welcome back, Citizen — أهلاً برجوعك\n\nYou are not starting again. You arrive with A1: you can introduce yourself, describe your routine, handle\nsimple needs, and keep a short exchange alive. Stage 1 opens the timeline and gives that foundation range.\n\n> إنت مش راجع للصفر. الأساس موجود؛ المرحلة دي بتخليك تتحرك للماضي والمستقبل، وتقارن، وتنصح، وتتعامل مع\n> مواقف أطول باستقلال أكبر.\n\n**By the end of Stage 1, you will be able to tell a simple story, make and discuss plans, compare choices,\ngive advice, and handle ordinary travel, food, health, phone, and small-talk situations in English.**\n\nYour final evidence will not be a certificate alone. You will complete an extended A2 exchange and record a\nstandard milestone sample that can sit beside your Day-1 and Stage-0 recordings.\n\n> الفرق المرة دي مش بس عدد كلمات أكبر. الفرق إنك هتربط الكلام، وتتصرف لما الموقف يتغير، وتصلّح نفسك وتكمل.\n\n---\n\n## Your rank — رُتبتك\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Rank** | Citizen — المواطن |\n| **CEFR** | A2 — Elementary |\n| **Entry** | A1 achieved through Stage 0 or placement evidence |\n| **What it means** | you can complete familiar everyday tasks and connect short accounts across time |\n\nThe rank path is deliberate: Recruit (A1) → **Citizen (A2)** → Legionary (B1) → **Confident** (B2, the\nCoronation) → Sovereign (C1).\n\nA Citizen does more than survive a conversation. You begin to participate in it — with a past, a plan, an\nopinion, a reason, and a response to the other person.\n\n> الرُتبة معناها إنك بقيت تستخدم اللغة في حياتك، مش بس تنفّذ تمرين عنها.\n\n---\n\n## The roadmap — خريطة المرحلة\n\n**10 units · 50 lessons · about 10–14 weeks at roughly 7 guided hours a week.**\n\n| Unit | Theme | What you take from it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1** | The Past (1) — *Was & Were* | locate people, places, states, and experiences in the past |\n| **2** | The Past (2) — Regular Verbs | say what happened and produce the three *-ed* endings |\n| **3** | The Past (3) — Irregulars & Questions | ask, negate, and tell a connected simple story |\n| **4** | Future Plans | distinguish plans from predictions, offers, and instant decisions |\n| **5** | Making Comparisons | compare options, choose the best, and explain degree |\n| **6** | Food, Quantity & Restaurants | manage amounts, containers, questions, and a polite meal order |\n| **7** | Health & Advice | describe symptoms, advise warmly, and separate advice from necessity |\n| **8** | Travel & Transport | get around, request help, buy a ticket, and narrate a trip |\n| **9** | People, Feelings & Everyday Situations | describe people, say how, contrast usual with temporary, and sustain small talk |\n| **10** | Putting It Together | retrieve the stage, complete the A2 exchange, and record your milestone |\n\n**The grammar spine:** past *was/were* → regular and irregular past → *did/didn't* → *going to/will* →\ncomparatives and superlatives → count/uncount quantity → *should/have to/must/can't* → adverbs and the\nsimple/continuous contrast.\n\nThis stage is built around **time**. The first three units unlock the past because telling a story is the\nlargest A2 jump. The future follows, then the tools to compare, quantify, advise, and act in everyday domains.\nEvery unit retrieves language from earlier ones before adding its own layer.\n\n**Vocabulary target:** roughly **1,000–1,500 words cumulative**, taught in context and collected in the\ngenerated glossary at the back of this book.\n\n**Accent Lab priorities:**\n- the three past *-ed* endings: /t/, /d/, /ɪd/\n- /θ/ and /ð/ consolidation\n- consonant clusters without an inserted vowel\n- stress in longer words\n- sentence rhythm, linking, and a light introduction to weak forms\n- personal weak sounds identified from recordings, not guessed from a generic list\n\n> النطق هنا هدفه الوضوح. بنقارن تسجيلك بتسجيلك، ونشتغل على الصوت اللي فعلاً بيأثر عليك.\n\n---\n\n## The Arabic fade — الدعم العربي بيخف\n\nStage 1 begins with moderate Arabic support, around 40%, and fades across the course. This is not removal of\nhelp for its own sake. Familiar lesson structure, examples, tables, and repeated English instructions gradually\ntake over work that translation once did.\n\n> لو الشرح بقى إنجليزي أكتر، ده مش لأن الدعم اختفى. ده لأنك بقيت قادر تستقبل جزء أكبر من اللغة باللغة نفسها.\n\nWhen a precise Arabic contrast prevents a durable error, the lesson uses it. When English is already clear,\nthe lesson lets English carry the meaning. The fade is planned, not random.\n\n---\n\n## How to use this book — إزاي تستخدم الكتاب\n\nEvery teaching lesson keeps the same route, while review, milestone, and assessment lessons adapt the middle\nto the task.\n\n| Section | What you do there |\n|---|---|\n| 🎯 **Your Conquest** | name the ability you are building |\n| 👑 **Why this matters** | connect the language to a real reason to use it |\n| 🔁 **Warm-up** | retrieve earlier language before adding more |\n| 👂 **Watch & Listen** | meet the language inside a complete situation |\n| 🧩 **Decode it** | notice the form, meaning, and high-value contrast |\n| 🗡️ **Your Arsenal** | collect the words and functional phrases |\n| 🎙️ **Accent Lab** | perceive, cue, produce, record, and compare |\n| 🏋️ **Train** | practise accurately before the speaking load rises |\n| 🎤 **Your Turn** | connect the lesson into meaningful speech |\n| 📜 **Your Orders** | carry one small action into real life |\n| 🧠 **Remember** | compress the lesson into what must survive |\n| ✅ **Self-check** | verify and correct without waiting for a teacher |\n\n**Four rules make Stage 1 work:**\n\n1. **Retrieve before rereading.** The effort to remember is part of the learning.\n2. **Connect sentences.** A2 grows through *because, but, so, then,* and follow-up questions, not longer lists.\n3. **Record before you feel ready.** Listening back turns a vague impression into specific evidence.\n4. **Never miss twice.** One missed day is human; returning the next day protects the habit.\n\n> ⭐ **القاعدة الذهبية:** الاستمرار أهم من الشدّة. ارجع بسرعة، حتى لو رجعت بعشر دقايق بس.\n\n---\n\n## What gets recorded — إيه اللي بيتسجل\n\nEach unit ends in a speaking task. Those recordings have three jobs:\n\n- give the coach evidence for focused feedback\n- let you compare current speech with your own earlier speech\n- build the proof timeline for milestone points\n\nRecording for learning is not permission to publish. Storage, comparison, and any public use follow the\nconsent tier in the before/after protocol. You can participate in the course without granting marketing use.\n\n> التسجيل دليل ليك وأداة تعليم. استخدامه خارج ده محتاج موافقة واضحة ومنفصلة.\n\n---\n\n## How you are assessed — التقييم\n\n- **Entry:** A1 evidence through the Stage-0 exit or placement process.\n- **End of every unit:** a short quiz plus a speaking task. These are formative checks; they direct feedback\n  and the review queue.\n- **Unit 10 mock:** the exact shape of the final exchange, completed without a pass/fail label.\n- **Stage-1 summative:** a mixed quiz and an extended exchange — narration, role-play, and Q&A — scored on\n  Fluency, Accuracy, Pronunciation, Range, and Interaction at the committed A2 threshold.\n- **A2 milestone sample:** a separate standard-prompt recording for fair Day-1 → Stage-0 → A2 comparison.\n\nA green quiz is not proof of speaking ability. A strong speech is not proof that every grammar target is\nstable. The determination uses the required evidence together.\n\n> التقييم مش كمين. شكله معروف، ومعاييره ثابتة، والنتيجة بتبدأ بالدليل قبل الاسم.\n\n---\n\n## An honest promise — وعد صادق\n\nWe promise structured practice toward **clear, confident, intelligible communication**. We do not promise a\nnative accent, effortless fluency in a number of days, or progress without sustained use.\n\n> مانوعدكش بلكنة أصلية ولا بطلاقة سريعة. نوعدك بمسار واضح، تدريب حقيقي، ودليل تقدر تسمعه بنفسك.\n\n**What Stage 1 can honestly deliver:** you can narrate familiar past events, discuss straightforward plans,\ncompare and choose, talk about amounts, describe symptoms and give advice, manage predictable travel and\nphone tasks, and sustain a short everyday conversation. You will still pause, make errors, ask for repetition,\nand need support in unfamiliar situations. That is not a failed B1; it is a real A2.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **Stage overview for the coach.** 10 units, 50 lessons, ~100 guided hours, normally 10–14 weeks. Entry is\n> evidenced A1. Arabic begins around 40% and fades to roughly 10–20% in the final units; do not restore\n> translation merely because productive struggle appears.\n> **Stage structure:** U1–U3 build the past, U4 future, U5 comparison, U6 quantity/food, U7 health/advice,\n> U8 travel, U9 everyday interaction, and U10 retrieval + clinic + mock + summative.\n> **Non-negotiables:** every requested recording, the Accent Lab, retrieval before support, and interaction\n> evidence. A correct monologue is not a conversation; reward asking back, clarification, repair, and response\n> to new information.\n> **Pronunciation honesty:** clear and intelligible, never “native.” Use tracker evidence to personalise the\n> Unit-10 clinic and compare each learner only with their own earlier sample.\n> **Proof protocol:** the extended A2 exchange and standard milestone prompt are separate files. Use the\n> assessment suite for determination and the before/after protocol for comparable capture, storage, and\n> granular consent. Course participation never implies publication permission.\n> **Persistent errors to recycle:** *did/didn't + base*; missing *be* in *going to*; double comparison;\n> *much/many*; *should + base*; *don't have to* against *can't*; *good* against *well*; simple against\n> temporary continuous; meaning-changing *can/can't* clarity.\n> **The predictable dip:** learners often say they are getting worse when the Arabic fades and output grows.\n> Show them two recordings, name the increased task difficulty, and compare like with like before changing\n> the plan.\n\n---\n\n## Next → **Unit 1: The Past — Was & Were**\n\nYour first campaign opens the timeline: where you were, how things were, what existed, and how a past\nexperience felt.\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 · Stage 1 — Elementary · من دراسة الإنجليزي لاستخدامه*\n","stage1-glossary":"# 🗡️ Stage 1 — Glossary\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · **269 entries**\n*Generated from the **Your Arsenal** tables of all 50 Stage-1 lessons by*\n*`tools/audit/generate-stage-glossary.mjs --stage 1` — do not edit by hand.*\n\n> ذخيرتك في المرحلة كلها، مرتبة بنفس ترتيب الوحدات والدروس عشان ترجع للسياق مش تحفظ قايمة.\n\nEvery word here is taught somewhere in the book, and the lesson it comes from is named, so you can go back to the explanation rather than memorising a list out of context.\n\n---\n\n## Contents\n\n- **Unit 1 — The Past (1) — Was & Were** · 25 entries\n- **Unit 2 — The Past (2) — Regular Verbs** · 23 entries\n- **Unit 3 — The Past (3) — Irregular Verbs & Questions** · 28 entries\n- **Unit 4 — Future Plans** · 26 entries\n- **Unit 5 — Making Comparisons** · 28 entries\n- **Unit 6 — Food, Quantity & Restaurants** · 28 entries\n- **Unit 7 — Health & Advice** · 27 entries\n- **Unit 8 — Travel & Transport** · 31 entries\n- **Unit 9 — People, Feelings & Everyday Situations** · 35 entries\n- **Unit 10 — Putting It Together (A2)** · 18 entries\n\n---\n\n## Unit 1 — The Past (1) — Was & Were\n\n| Lesson | English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| L01 | was / were | كان / كانوا | *I was busy. They were late.* |\n| L01 | yesterday | امبارح | *Yesterday I was at home.* |\n| L01 | last night / week | امبارح بالليل / الأسبوع اللي فات | *She was here last night.* |\n| L01 | … ago | فات / من | *two days ago* |\n| L01 | tired / busy / happy | تعبان / مشغول / مبسوط | *I was tired.* |\n| L01 | at work / at home | في الشغل / في البيت | *He was at work.* |\n| L02 | wasn't / weren't | ماكانش / ماكانوش | *I wasn't ready.* |\n| L02 | Were you…? | كنت…؟ | *Were you at home?* |\n| L02 | Was he / she / it…? | كان…؟ | *Was it good?* |\n| L02 | Yes, I was. / No, I wasn't. | أيوه كنت / لأ ماكنتش | short answers |\n| L02 | really | فعلاً / قوي | *I was really tired.* |\n| L02 | ready / late / cold | جاهز / متأخر / برد | *We weren't late.* |\n| L03 | There was / There were | كان فيه | *There was a garden.* |\n| L03 | There wasn't / weren't | ماكانش فيه | *There weren't any shops.* |\n| L03 | a lot of / some / any | كتير / شوية / أي | *There were a lot of people.* |\n| L03 | near / next to | جنب / قريب من | *There was a café near us.* |\n| L03 | old / small / simple | قديم / صغير / بسيط | *It was a simple room.* |\n| L03 | garden / building / shop | جنينة / عمارة / دكان | *There was a garden.* |\n| L04 | How was…? | كان عامل إيه…؟ | *How was your day?* |\n| L04 | great / fun | جميل / ممتع | *It was great!* |\n| L04 | boring / tiring | ممل / متعب | *It was tiring.* |\n| L04 | delicious | لذيذ | *The food was delicious.* |\n| L04 | a bit / really | شوية / قوي | *a bit boring* |\n| L04 | because | لأن | *because I was busy* |\n| L04 | That's a pity. | يا خسارة | reaction |\n\n## Unit 2 — The Past (2) — Regular Verbs\n\n| Lesson | English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| L01 | worked / finished | اشتغلت / خلصت | *I worked late.* |\n| L01 | cooked / cleaned | طبخت / نضفت | *She cooked dinner.* |\n| L01 | watched / listened | اتفرجت / سمعت | *We watched a film.* |\n| L01 | called / talked | كلمت / اتكلمت | *He called me.* |\n| L01 | studied / visited | درست / زرت | *I visited my aunt.* |\n| L01 | … ago | فات | *two days ago* |\n| L01 | until | لحد | *I worked until six.* |\n| L02 | worked · watched · stopped · helped · talked · finished |  | after voiceless: **/t/** |\n| L02 | played · cleaned · called · listened · stayed · studied |  | after voiced / vowel: **/d/** |\n| L02 | visited · wanted · started · needed · waited · decided |  | after t / d: **/ɪd/** |\n| L03 | stop → stopped | وقف | *I stopped early.* |\n| L03 | plan → planned | خطّط | *We planned the trip.* |\n| L03 | study → studied | درس | *She studied all night.* |\n| L03 | try → tried | حاول | *I tried twice.* |\n| L03 | play → played | لعب | *They played football.* |\n| L03 | live → lived | عاش | *He lived in Cairo.* |\n| L03 | arrive → arrived | وصل | *We arrived late.* |\n| L04 | first | الأول | *First, I woke up.* |\n| L04 | then | بعدين | *Then I cooked.* |\n| L04 | after that | بعد كده | *After that, I studied.* |\n| L04 | next | وبعدها | *Next, I called him.* |\n| L04 | later | بعد شوية | *Later, I watched TV.* |\n| L04 | finally | وفي الآخر | *Finally, I stopped.* |\n\n## Unit 3 — The Past (3) — Irregular Verbs & Questions\n\n| Lesson | English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| L01 | go → went | رحت | *I went home.* |\n| L01 | have → had | كان عندي / اتغديت | *I had lunch.* |\n| L01 | do → did | عملت | *I did my work.* |\n| L01 | see → saw | شفت | *I saw my friend.* |\n| L01 | eat → ate | اكلت | *We ate together.* |\n| L01 | early / late | بدري / متأخر | *I went early.* |\n| L01 | enough | كفاية | *It was enough.* |\n| L02 | buy → bought | اشتريت | *I bought bread.* |\n| L02 | get → got | خدت / وصلت | *I got home late.* |\n| L02 | come → came | جيت | *She came early.* |\n| L02 | make → made | عملت | *He made coffee.* |\n| L02 | take → took | خدت | *We took a taxi.* |\n| L02 | price | سعر | *a good price* |\n| L02 | everything | كل حاجة | *They ate everything.* |\n| L03 | Did you…? | إنت…؟ | *Did you eat?* |\n| L03 | Yes, I did. / No, I didn't. | أيوه / لأ | short answers |\n| L03 | What did you do? | عملت إيه؟ | *What did you do?* |\n| L03 | Where did you go? | رحت فين؟ | *Where did you go?* |\n| L03 | Who did you see? | شفت مين؟ | *Who did you see?* |\n| L03 | I didn't… | أنا ما…ش | *I didn't go.* |\n| L03 | nowhere | مكانش في حاجة | *I went nowhere.* |\n| L04 | One day… | مرة… | *One day I lost my keys.* |\n| L04 | Suddenly | فجأة | *Suddenly it started to rain.* |\n| L04 | In the end | في الآخر | *In the end I found them.* |\n| L04 | so | فـ | *It was late, so I ran.* |\n| L04 | wait → waited | استنيت | *I waited an hour.* |\n| L04 | lose → lost | ضيّعت | *I lost my phone.* |\n| L04 | find → found | لقيت | *I found it.* |\n\n## Unit 4 — Future Plans\n\n| Lesson | English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| L01 | I'm going to… | أنا هـ… | *I'm going to study.* |\n| L01 | tomorrow / tonight | بكرة / الليلة | *tomorrow morning* |\n| L01 | next week / month | الأسبوع / الشهر الجاي | *next week* |\n| L01 | this weekend | الويكند ده | *this weekend* |\n| L01 | visit / travel | أزور / أسافر | *I'm going to travel.* |\n| L01 | start / finish | أبدأ / أخلص | *She's going to start.* |\n| L01 | look for | أدوّر على | *look for a job* |\n| L02 | Are you going to…? | هتـ…؟ | *Are you going to eat?* |\n| L02 | What are you going to do? | هتعمل إيه؟ | *What are you going to do?* |\n| L02 | Where / When | فين / امتى | *When are you going to go?* |\n| L02 | Yes, I am. / No, I'm not. | أيوه / لأ | short answers |\n| L02 | What about you? | وإنت؟ | hands it back |\n| L02 | leave / stay | أمشي / أفضل | *Are you going to stay?* |\n| L03 | I'll… | هـ… | *I'll call you.* |\n| L03 | won't | مش هـ… | *I won't be late.* |\n| L03 | I think… will | أظن هـ… | *I think he'll come.* |\n| L03 | I'll have… | هاخد… | *I'll have tea.* |\n| L03 | I'll help you | هساعدك | offering |\n| L03 | Don't worry | ماتقلقش | *Don't worry, I'll do it.* |\n| L03 | maybe | يمكن | *Maybe it'll rain.* |\n| L04 | I'll get it! | أنا هفتح | decided now |\n| L04 | I'm going to… | كنت ناوي… | already planned |\n| L04 | Look! It's going to… | بص! هـ… | evidence |\n| L04 | I promise | أوعدك | *I promise I'll come.* |\n| L04 | I've decided | قررت | *I've decided — I'm going to move.* |\n| L04 | Then I'll… | يبقى هـ… | reacting |\n\n## Unit 5 — Making Comparisons\n\n| Lesson | English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| L01 | bigger than | أكبر من | *Cairo is bigger than Tanta.* |\n| L01 | cheaper / more expensive | أرخص / أغلى | *It's cheaper than that.* |\n| L01 | older / newer | أقدم / أحدث | *My phone is older.* |\n| L01 | faster / slower | أسرع / أبطأ | *The metro is faster.* |\n| L01 | quieter / busier | أهدى / أزحم | *This street is quieter.* |\n| L01 | much | بكتير | *much bigger* |\n| L01 | than | من (في المقارنة) | *bigger than* |\n| L02 | more expensive | أغلى | *It's more expensive.* |\n| L02 | more beautiful | أجمل | *more beautiful than* |\n| L02 | more difficult | أصعب | *This is more difficult.* |\n| L02 | more important | أهم | *Family is more important.* |\n| L02 | more comfortable | أريح | *a more comfortable chair* |\n| L02 | better | أحسن | *This one is better.* |\n| L02 | worse | أسوأ | *The traffic is worse.* |\n| L03 | the biggest | الأكبر | *the biggest city* |\n| L03 | the cheapest | الأرخص | *the cheapest option* |\n| L03 | the most beautiful | الأجمل | *the most beautiful place* |\n| L03 | the best | الأحسن | *the best restaurant* |\n| L03 | the worst | الأسوأ | *the worst day* |\n| L03 | in the world | في العالم | *the best in the world* |\n| L03 | I've ever… | في حياتي | *the best I've ever had* |\n| L04 | as … as | زي | *as big as* |\n| L04 | not as … as | مش زي | *not as good as* |\n| L04 | too | زيادة عن اللزوم | *too expensive* |\n| L04 | enough | كفاية | *big enough* |\n| L04 | enough money / time | فلوس / وقت كفاية | *enough time* |\n| L04 | I prefer | أنا أفضّل | *I prefer this one.* |\n| L04 | because | لأن | *because it's cheaper* |\n\n## Unit 6 — Food, Quantity & Restaurants\n\n| Lesson | English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| L01 | rice / bread | رز / عيش | *some bread* |\n| L01 | water / milk / sugar | ماية / لبن / سكر | *any milk* |\n| L01 | eggs / apples / tomatoes | بيض / تفاح / طماطم | *some eggs* |\n| L01 | vegetables / fruit | خضار / فاكهة | *some vegetables* |\n| L01 | a bottle of | إزازة | *a bottle of water* |\n| L01 | a piece of | حتة | *a piece of bread* |\n| L01 | a kilo of | كيلو | *a kilo of rice* |\n| L02 | a lot of | كتير | *a lot of people* |\n| L02 | many | كتير (معدود) | *many eggs* |\n| L02 | much | كتير (غير معدود) | *not much time* |\n| L02 | a few | شوية (معدود) | *a few days* |\n| L02 | a little | شوية (غير معدود) | *a little sugar* |\n| L02 | not much / not many | مش كتير | *There isn't much.* |\n| L02 | more | أكتر | *a little more* |\n| L03 | How much is it? | بكام؟ | *How much is it?* |\n| L03 | How many…? | كام…؟ | *How many eggs?* |\n| L03 | a bottle of | إزازة | *a bottle of water* |\n| L03 | a cup / glass of | كوباية | *a cup of tea* |\n| L03 | a piece of | حتة | *a piece of bread* |\n| L03 | a kilo of | كيلو | *two kilos of rice* |\n| L03 | altogether | الإجمالي | *That's 90 altogether.* |\n| L04 | Could I have…? | ممكن آخد…؟ | *Could I have the fish?* |\n| L04 | I'll have… | هاخد… | *I'll have tea.* |\n| L04 | I'd like… | عايز… (بأدب) | *I'd like the soup.* |\n| L04 | the menu | المنيو | *Could we see the menu?* |\n| L04 | the bill | الحساب | *the bill, please* |\n| L04 | Anything else? | حاجة تانية؟ | waiter's question |\n| L04 | for two | لاتنين | *A table for two.* |\n\n## Unit 7 — Health & Advice\n\n| Lesson | English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| L01 | a headache | صداع | *I have a headache.* |\n| L01 | a cold | برد | *She has a cold.* |\n| L01 | a sore throat | التهاب زور | *I have a sore throat.* |\n| L01 | a temperature | حرارة | *Do you have a temperature?* |\n| L01 | tired / sick / dizzy | تعبان / تعبان جدًا / دايخ | *I feel dizzy.* |\n| L01 | … hurts | بيوجعني | *My back hurts.* |\n| L01 | teeth / feet | سنان / رجلين | *My teeth hurt.* |\n| L02 | You should… | لازم / الأفضل إنك | *You should rest.* |\n| L02 | You shouldn't… | ماينفعش | *You shouldn't smoke.* |\n| L02 | What should I do? | أعمل إيه؟ | asking |\n| L02 | I think you should… | أنا شايف إنك | softener |\n| L02 | Maybe you should… | يمكن الأفضل | softener |\n| L02 | see a doctor | تروح لدكتور | *You should see a doctor.* |\n| L02 | take medicine | تاخد دوا | *take some medicine* |\n| L03 | have to / has to | لازم / مضطر | *I have to go.* |\n| L03 | don't have to | مش ضروري | *You don't have to wait.* |\n| L03 | must | لازم (قوي) | *You must stop smoking.* |\n| L03 | a rule | قاعدة | *It's a rule.* |\n| L03 | take a day off | تاخد يوم أجازة | *I have to take a day off.* |\n| L03 | be on time | تكون في الميعاد | *We have to be on time.* |\n| L04 | How can I help you? | أقدر أساعدك؟ | pharmacist |\n| L04 | twice a day | مرتين في اليوم | *Take it twice a day.* |\n| L04 | after food | بعد الأكل | *after food* |\n| L04 | medicine / tablets | دوا / حبوب | *take these tablets* |\n| L04 | exercise | أتمرن | *I exercise twice a week.* |\n| L04 | Do I have to…? | لازم…؟ | *Do I have to rest?* |\n| L04 | get better | أتحسن | *I hope you get better.* |\n\n## Unit 8 — Travel & Transport\n\n| Lesson | English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| L01 | How do I get to…? | أوصل لـ… إزاي؟ | *How do I get to the airport?* |\n| L01 | take the bus / metro | اركب الأتوبيس / المترو | *Take the metro to Sadat.* |\n| L01 | get off at… | انزل في… | *Get off at the next stop.* |\n| L01 | a station / a stop | محطة / موقف | *The station is near here.* |\n| L01 | a platform | رصيف القطر | *Platform four.* |\n| L01 | by bus / by train | بالأتوبيس / بالقطر | *I go by train.* |\n| L01 | on foot | ماشي | *I go on foot.* |\n| L01 | How long does it take? | بتاخد قد إيه؟ | *How long does it take?* |\n| L02 | Could you…? | تقدر…؟ (طلب) | *Could you help me?* |\n| L02 | Can I…? / Could I…? | أقدر…؟ (إذن) | *Could I sit here?* |\n| L02 | Excuse me… | لو سمحت | *Excuse me, could you…?* |\n| L02 | Of course. / Sure. | طبعًا | *Sure, no problem.* |\n| L02 | I'm afraid not. | للأسف لأ | *I'm afraid not.* |\n| L02 | Could you say that again? | تقدر تعيد؟ | *Could you say that again, please?* |\n| L02 | Could you speak more slowly? | تقدر تتكلم أبطأ؟ | *Could you speak more slowly?* |\n| L03 | a single / a return | ذهاب / ذهاب وعودة | *A return to Cairo, please.* |\n| L03 | What time does it leave? | بتقوم الساعة كام؟ | *What time does it leave?* |\n| L03 | a platform / a gate | رصيف / بوابة | *Platform three.* |\n| L03 | to book | يحجز | *Do I have to book?* |\n| L03 | to check in | يعمل تسجيل وصول | *You have to check in online.* |\n| L03 | You have to… | لازم… | *You have to wear a seatbelt.* |\n| L03 | You can't… | ممنوع… | *You can't smoke here.* |\n| L03 | It's sold out. | التذاكر خلصت | *Sorry, it's sold out.* |\n| L04 | I went to… | رحت… | *I went to Luxor.* |\n| L04 | I took the train / a taxi | ركبت القطر / تاكسي | *We took the night train.* |\n| L04 | we stayed in / at… | قعدنا في… | *We stayed in a small hotel.* |\n| L04 | we visited… | زرنا… | *We visited the temple.* |\n| L04 | There was / There were… | كان فيه… | *There were a lot of tourists.* |\n| L04 | a trip / a journey | رحلة | *It was a long journey.* |\n| L04 | The best part was… | أحلى حاجة كانت… | *The best part was the train.* |\n| L04 | in the end | في الآخر | *In the end we went home.* |\n\n## Unit 9 — People, Feelings & Everyday Situations\n\n| Lesson | English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| L01 | nervous | متوتر | *I'm a bit nervous.* |\n| L01 | excited | متحمس | *I'm so excited!* |\n| L01 | worried | قلقان | *She's worried about the exam.* |\n| L01 | bored / boring | زهقان / ممل | *I'm bored. The film is boring.* |\n| L01 | kind | طيب | *He's very kind.* |\n| L01 | funny | بيضحّك | *My sister is really funny.* |\n| L01 | shy | خجول | *He's shy with new people.* |\n| L01 | hard-working | مجتهد | *She's hard-working.* |\n| L01 | What's he like? | شخصيته إيه؟ | *What's your teacher like?* |\n| L02 | well | كويس (ظرف) | *She speaks English well.* |\n| L02 | carefully | بعناية | *Drive carefully.* |\n| L02 | quickly | بسرعة | *He eats too quickly.* |\n| L02 | slowly | ببطء | *Please speak slowly.* |\n| L02 | quietly | بهدوء | *She came in quietly.* |\n| L02 | badly | بشكل سيئ | *I slept badly.* |\n| L02 | hard | بجد | *They work hard.* |\n| L02 | easily | بسهولة | *I found it easily.* |\n| L02 | hardly | تقريبًا مش | *I hardly know him.* |\n| L03 | usually / normally | عادةً | *I usually get up at seven.* |\n| L03 | every day | كل يوم | *I study every day.* |\n| L03 | at the moment | في الوقت الحالي | *At the moment I'm studying.* |\n| L03 | these days | الأيام دي | *These days I'm reading more.* |\n| L03 | this week / this month | الأسبوع ده / الشهر ده | *This week I'm working late.* |\n| L03 | right now | حالًا | *He's sleeping right now.* |\n| L03 | I'm working from home. | بشتغل من البيت | *This month I'm working from home.* |\n| L03 | I know / I want | أعرف / عايز | *I know him well.* |\n| L04 | Can I speak to…? | ممكن أكلم…؟ | *Can I speak to Lina?* |\n| L04 | Who's calling? | مين بيتكلم؟ | *Who's calling, please?* |\n| L04 | Hold on. | استنى لحظة | *Hold on a moment.* |\n| L04 | available | متاح | *He isn't available right now.* |\n| L04 | take / leave a message | ياخد / يسيب رسالة | *Can I leave a message?* |\n| L04 | call me back | يكلمني تاني | *Could you ask her to call me back?* |\n| L04 | How's it going? | أخبارك إيه؟ | *Hey, how's it going?* |\n| L04 | How about you? | وإنت؟ | *Not bad. How about you?* |\n| L04 | Good talking to you. | اتبسطت بالكلام معاك | *Good talking to you. Bye.* |\n\n## Unit 10 — Putting It Together (A2)\n\n| Lesson | English | بالعربي | Example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| L01 | *Last… · Two years ago… · When I was…* |  | set the scene |\n| L01 | *There was/were… · It was…* |  | describe it |\n| L01 | *first · then · after that · while* |  | move forward |\n| L01 | *because · so* |  | explain |\n| L01 | *didn't + base · Did… + base?* |  | negative / question |\n| L01 | *in the end · finally · the best part was…* |  | close |\n| L02 | *I'm going to…* |  | state the plan |\n| L02 | *I think it will…* |  | predict |\n| L02 | *X is cheaper / more useful than Y.* |  | compare |\n| L02 | *much better · a little cheaper* |  | add degree |\n| L02 | *many people · much time · a few options · a little money* |  | quantify |\n| L02 | *I think you should… because…* |  | recommend |\n| L02 | *You have to… · You don't have to…* |  | state reality |\n| L03 | pause, repeat the **whole phrase**, continue |  | the word was unclear |\n| L03 | slow the final word, not the whole sentence |  | a missing ending |\n| L03 | reset the mouth cue, then say the phrase once |  | a substituted sound |\n| L03 | record two versions and choose by listening |  | uncertainty |\n| L03 | step back: sentence → phrase → word → sound |  | repeated failure |\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 · Stage 1 — Elementary · من دراسة الإنجليزي لاستخدامه.*\n","unit1-front-matter":"# 👑 Unit 1 — The Past (1): Was & Were\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · **Unit 1 of 10**\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit1-was-and-were.md` (unit map + lessons)*\n\n---\n\n## The Campaign — حملة الوحدة\n> **Your mission:** by the end of Unit 1 you'll **talk about the past** — say where you and others were,\n> describe a place from your memory, say how an experience felt, and ask someone about theirs.\n> مهمتك في الوحدة دي: تتكلم عن اللي فات، وتوصف مكان من ذكرياتك، وتقول إحساسك عن تجربة.\n\n**Why this is your first A2 campaign:** in Stage 0 you could describe **now**. Everything else — stories,\nmemories, explanations, excuses — needs the past. This unit opens all of it with a verb that has only\n**two** forms.\n\n> ده أكبر فرق بين المستوى اللي فات والمستوى ده.\n\n---\n\n## The Lessons — دروس الوحدة\n\n| # | Lesson | You'll be able to… |\n|---|---|---|\n| **L01** | I was, you were | use *was/were* · past time expressions (*yesterday, last week, … ago*) |\n| **L02** | wasn't · weren't · Were you…? | negatives · questions by inversion · short answers |\n| **L03** | There was / There were | describe what existed in a past place · singular vs plural |\n| **L04** | How was it? | opinion adjectives · *because* + a reason · natural reactions |\n| **L05** | Weekend account (milestone) 🎙️ | a connected 5–7 sentence past account + **record your milestone** |\n\n**Grammar spine:** *was / were* — affirmative, negative, questions, short answers — plus\n*there was / there were*.\n**Accent Lab:** /w/ in *was·were* · the weak *was* /wəz/ · the final /t/ in *wasn't·weren't* · linking\n*there-wuz-a* · stress on opinion words.\n**Mindset:** momentum into a new stage — a new level is exactly where people quietly stop, so lock your\ndaily slot again.\n**Arabic support:** moderate (~40%), fading to ~30% by L05.\n\n---\n\n## The Prize — جايزة الوحدة\n- Your **Unit-1 weekend account** — the first recording where you talk about your past.\n- A pair worth keeping: play your Stage-0 graduation clip, then this one.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **Unit overview:** 5 lessons (~30 min each, L05 ~35) plus this wrapper. Grammar spine is *was/were* in\n> every form. The Arabic dial drops sharply from Stage 0 — explain in English first and reserve Arabic for\n> the two errors that actually cost marks.\n> **The two errors to hunt all unit:** subject agreement (***we was***) and existential plural agreement\n> (***there was two windows***). Both are habits rather than misunderstandings, so they need repetitions in\n> warm-ups, not more explanation.\n> **The thing that most raises their score** is *because* + a reason, introduced in L04. Insist on it even\n> when the surrounding grammar is imperfect — connected speech is the A2 threshold.\n> **L05 is a proof asset.** Capture every learner's recording with consent and hold it beside their Stage-0\n> graduation clip; that pair is the clearest evidence of progress the programme produces.\n\n---\n\n## End-of-Unit Check — تقييم آخر الوحدة (formative)\n- **Quiz:** *was/were* choice · negative and question forms · *there was* vs *there were* ·\n  match *How was…?* to a reaction.\n- **Speaking task:** the L05 weekend account, scored on the rubric (A2 threshold).\n- **Feeds:** the Cohort Tracker (weekly activity + each learner's flagged weak sound) and the proof timeline.\n\n---\n\n## Next Campaign → **Unit 2 — The Past (2): Regular Verbs**\nthe *-ed* ending and its three sounds — building directly on the past you just unlocked.\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 · Stage 1 · Citizen rank · consistency beats intensity*\n","unit10-front-matter":"# 👑 Unit 10 — Putting It Together\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · **Unit 10 of 10**\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit10-putting-it-together.md` (unit map + lessons)*\n\n---\n\n## The Final Campaign — الحملة الأخيرة\n> **Your mission:** retrieve the whole stage, repair the patterns that still slip under pressure, complete a\n> full A2 exchange, and record the milestone sample that proves how far you have travelled.\n> مهمتك الأخيرة: تجمع المرحلة كلها في استخدام حقيقي، وتدخل التقييم عارف شكله ومن غير مفاجآت.\n\nThis unit adds almost no new language. That is deliberate. Knowledge becomes ability when it can be retrieved,\ncombined, heard, repaired, and used with another person. The final campaign tests exactly that.\n\n> أنت مش داخل تجمع قواعد أكتر. أنت داخل تشغّل اللي بنيته كله مع بعض.\n\n---\n\n## The Lessons — دروس الوحدة\n\n| # | Lesson | The proof |\n|---|---|---|\n| **L01** | Big Review 1 — past narration | retrieve the full past and connect a 6–8 sentence event |\n| **L02** | Big Review 2 — range | combine future, comparison, quantity, and advice naturally |\n| **L03** | Personal Accent Clinic | improve your evidence-based top three and self-repair |\n| **L04** | The A2 Exchange mock | rehearse narration → role-play → Q&A in the exact format |\n| **L05** | Stage-1 summative 🎓 | demonstrate A2 + record the standard milestone sample |\n\n**Language spine:** the complete Stage-1 range — past · future · comparison · quantity · symptoms and advice ·\ntravel functions · feelings and adverbs · routine against temporary change.\n**Accent Lab:** personalised top-three clinic, with *-ed* for everyone and meaning-changing contrasts first.\n**Mindset:** from **“I studied English”** to **“I use English.”**\n**Arabic support:** ~20% in consolidation, fading to ~10% in the English-first finale.\n\n---\n\n## What A2 Evidence Looks Like — شكل الدليل\nA2 is not an error-free performance. Across the whole exchange, the learner can:\n\n- narrate a connected past event\n- discuss a future plan\n- compare options and talk about quantity\n- give advice and distinguish it from necessity or prohibition\n- complete an ordinary role-play and handle one complication\n- ask, answer, clarify, repair, and keep interaction alive\n- remain generally intelligible across the stage's target sounds\n\nThe governing threshold and full bands remain in `curriculum/05-assessment-suite.md`; this wrapper does not\nreplace them.\n\n---\n\n## The Three-Point Proof Timeline — خط إثبات التطور\n\n1. **Day 1** — the original standard prompt\n2. **Stage 0** — the same prompt after A1 foundations\n3. **Stage 1** — the same prompt plus a past/future extension at A2\n\nComparison is only fair when prompt and conditions are controlled. Use `curriculum/06-before-after-protocol.md`.\nConsent to learn, consent to record, and consent to publish are different. The learner controls use of the clip.\n\n---\n\n## The Prize — جايزة المرحلة\n- An evidence-based **A2 determination**.\n- Your extended A2 exchange and standard milestone recording.\n- A precise handoff to Stage 2: one language/interaction target and one residual pronunciation target.\n- Proof that the learner who once named a routine can now move across time, choices, needs, and live interaction.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **Unit overview:** 5 lessons; L04 ~35 min, L05 ~40 min, plus this wrapper.\n> **L01 and L02 are retrieval reviews.** Struggle before support is useful. Correct repeated patterns rather\n> than interrupting every slip, and demand connected meaning rather than requirement sentences.\n> **L03 must be personalised from evidence.** A generic “top three” defeats the design. If tracker evidence\n> is missing, diagnose from the seeded passage before choosing. The target is clarity against the learner's\n> own earlier recording, never native imitation.\n> **L04 previews L05 exactly.** Keep prompt types, order, and rubric stable. Its output is a maximum of two\n> polish targets, not a premature determination.\n> **L05 separates assessment from proof capture.** The extended exchange is scored; the standard prompt is a\n> second recording for comparable progress evidence. Follow the assessment suite and before/after protocol,\n> record consent tier, and never treat a green technical check as evidence that a learner met A2.\n> **Be exact and warm at once.** Evidence-first scoring and humane delivery are not opposites.\n\n---\n\n## Stage-1 Completion Check — تقييم المرحلة\n- **Quiz:** mixed recognition plus short production across all Stage-1 targets.\n- **Speaking:** extended narration + role-play + Q&A, scored on Fluency, Accuracy, Pronunciation, Range, and\n  Interaction using the committed rubric bands.\n- **Proof sample:** standard prompt recorded separately under comparable conditions.\n- **Tracker:** determination, score evidence, clip links, consent tier, and Stage-2 carry-over flags.\n\n---\n\n## Next Campaign → **Stage 2 — Intermediate (B1)**\npresent perfect against past · conditionals · relative clauses · detailed storytelling · opinions with reasons ·\nconnected speech · learning to cope independently when the conversation does not follow the script.\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 · Stage 1 · Citizen rank · COMPLETE when the evidence meets the standard*\n","unit2-front-matter":"# 👑 Unit 2 — The Past (2): Regular Verbs\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · **Unit 2 of 10**\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit2-past-regular-verbs.md` (unit map + lessons)*\n\n---\n\n## The Campaign — حملة الوحدة\n> **Your mission:** by the end of Unit 2 you'll **say what you did** — put *-ed* on any regular verb,\n> pronounce all three endings correctly, spell them right, and narrate your day in order.\n> مهمتك في الوحدة دي: تحكي عملت إيه، بترتيب، وبنطق وإملاء صح.\n\n**Why this follows Unit 1:** *was* and *were* describe **states** — where you were, how you felt. This unit\ngives you **actions**, and actions are what stories are made of. One ending unlocks thousands of verbs.\n\n> قاعدة واحدة، وكل الأفعال العادية بتبقى تحت إيدك.\n\n---\n\n## The Lessons — دروس الوحدة\n\n| # | Lesson | You'll be able to… |\n|---|---|---|\n| **L01** | Yesterday I worked | verb + *-ed* for all persons · *ago* |\n| **L02** | The *-ed* sound ⭐ | hear and produce **/t/ · /d/ · /ɪd/** · sort any verb into its group |\n| **L03** | Spelling rules | *stopped · studied · lived* — doubling, *y→ied*, drop-*e* |\n| **L04** | Sequencing a day | *first · then · after that · finally* · narrate 4–6 sentences in order |\n| **L05** | \"What did you do yesterday?\" (milestone) 🎙️ | a 6–8 sentence narration + **record your milestone** |\n\n**Grammar spine:** past simple **regular** (affirmative) · *-ed* spelling · *ago* · sequence words.\n**Accent Lab:** ⭐ **the three *-ed* endings** — this is the flagship pronunciation lesson of the stage.\n**Mindset:** habit-stacking — attach English to something you already do every day.\n**Arabic support:** ~40% at L01, fading to ~30% by L05.\n\n---\n\n## The Prize — جايزة الوحدة\n- Your **Unit-2 \"my day\" narration** — the first recording where you *tell* rather than *describe*.\n- The three *-ed* endings, which every past sentence you ever say will use.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **Unit overview:** 5 lessons (L02 and L05 run ~35 min, the rest ~30) plus this wrapper.\n> **L02 is the highest-value lesson in the stage.** Arabic speakers insert a full vowel into every *-ed*,\n> so *worked* becomes three syllables. Because regular verbs appear in every past sentence they will ever\n> speak, that one habit repeats a hundred times a day. Teach perception before production — an ending they\n> cannot hear yet is an ending they can only guess at.\n> **The errors to hunt, in order:** dropping *-ed* entirely (L01), the inserted vowel (L02), *y→ied* and\n> the doubling rule (L03), and endings collapsing once sentences get longer (L04–L05).\n> **Do not hold L05 hostage to perfect endings.** They need many more reps across later units; the\n> milestone is the narration.\n> **L05 is a proof asset.** Capture it with consent and hold it beside the Unit-1 recording — states\n> against actions is an easy difference for a learner to hear in themselves.\n\n---\n\n## End-of-Unit Check — تقييم آخر الوحدة (formative)\n- **Quiz:** form the regular past · choose the *-ed* sound group · spelling rules · order a scrambled sequence.\n- **Speaking task:** the L05 narration, scored on the rubric (A2 threshold), with a specific eye on endings.\n- **Feeds:** the Cohort Tracker (weekly activity + each learner's *-ed* group error) and the proof timeline.\n\n---\n\n## Next Campaign → **Unit 3 — The Past (3): Irregular Verbs & Questions**\nthe verbs that refuse *-ed*, plus *did / didn't* and the questions that turn narration into conversation.\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 · Stage 1 · Citizen rank · consistency beats intensity*\n","unit3-front-matter":"# 👑 Unit 3 — The Past (3): Irregular Verbs & Questions\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · **Unit 3 of 10**\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit3-past-irregular-and-questions.md` (unit map + lessons)*\n\n---\n\n## The Campaign — حملة الوحدة\n> **Your mission:** by the end of Unit 3 you'll **tell stories** — use the ten most common irregular verbs,\n> ask and answer past questions, say what didn't happen, and narrate a real event.\n> مهمتك في الوحدة دي: تحكي حكاية حقيقية، وتسأل غيرك عن ماضيه.\n\n**Why this closes the block:** Unit 1 gave you past **states**, Unit 2 gave you past **actions** with *-ed*.\nThis unit adds the verbs that refuse *-ed*, and — more importantly — the ability to **ask**. That is what\nturns narration into conversation.\n\n> بعد الوحدة دي تبقى خلصت الماضي بكل صيغه.\n\n---\n\n## The Lessons — دروس الوحدة\n\n| # | Lesson | You'll be able to… |\n|---|---|---|\n| **L01** | Irregular verbs (1) | *went · had · did · saw · ate* — and why they take no *-ed* |\n| **L02** | Irregular verbs (2) | *bought · got · came · made · took* · the silent **gh** |\n| **L03** | *did · didn't* | ask and negate the past — **the base verb after *did*** |\n| **L04** | Telling a story | *One day · Suddenly · In the end* · a 5–7 sentence true story |\n| **L05** | Last holiday (milestone) 🎙️ | a 7–9 sentence narration + a live follow-up + **record your milestone** |\n\n**Grammar spine:** irregular past · *did / didn't* for questions and negatives · story connectors.\n**Accent Lab:** main-verb stress · the vowels in *went · saw · ate · bought* · weak *did* against crisp\n*didn't* · narrative rhythm.\n**Mindset:** environment design, then your input diet — one show, not five.\n**Arabic support:** ~35% at L01, fading to ~25% by L05.\n\n---\n\n## The Prize — جايزة الوحدة\n- Your **Unit-3 story** — the first recording where you *tell* something that happened to you.\n- The **did/didn't** rule, which makes every past question you will ever ask correct.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **Unit overview:** 5 lessons (L03 and L05 run ~35 min, the rest ~30) plus this wrapper. This unit closes\n> the past-tense block begun in Unit 1 — the single biggest milestone of A2.\n> **L03 is the priority lesson.** Keeping the past form after *did* — *\"Did you went?\"* — is the most\n> common A2 error there is, and it appears in every past question a learner will ever ask. Drill it to\n> reflex. The mental model that fixes it: **the past is said once in the sentence**, and *did* already said\n> it.\n> **Expect over-regularisation** in L01–L02 (*goed*, *eated*). It is evidence the *-ed* rule landed, not\n> evidence of failure — correct warmly and re-expose. Ten irregulars is the right number for A2; resist\n> handing out a fifty-verb table.\n> **In L04, correct almost nothing mid-story.** Stories are the fluency engine; interrupting them teaches\n> hesitation. Note the two patterned errors and give them as delayed group feedback.\n> **L05 is a proof asset,** and the best one so far — play it beside the learner's Stage-0 clip.\n\n---\n\n## End-of-Unit Check — تقييم آخر الوحدة (formative)\n- **Quiz:** irregular past forms · spot the *\"Did you went?\"* error · *wh-* past questions · order a\n  scrambled story.\n- **Speaking task:** the L05 narration, scored on the rubric (A2 threshold), with an eye on *did* + base\n  verb and irregular accuracy.\n- **Feeds:** the Cohort Tracker (weekly activity + weak irregulars and endings) and the proof timeline.\n\n---\n\n## Next Campaign → **Unit 4 — Future Plans**\n*be going to* and *will* — the stage pivots from what happened to what is coming.\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 · Stage 1 · Citizen rank · consistency beats intensity*\n","unit4-front-matter":"# 👑 Unit 4 — Future Plans\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · **Unit 4 of 10**\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit4-future-plans.md` (unit map + lessons)*\n\n---\n\n## The Campaign — حملة الوحدة\n> **Your mission:** by the end of Unit 4 you'll **talk about what's coming** — state plans with\n> *be going to*, ask about other people's, and use *will* to decide, offer and predict.\n> مهمتك في الوحدة دي: تتكلم عن خططك، وتسأل غيرك، وتقرر وتتوقع.\n\n**The stage pivots here.** Units 1 to 3 were all past. This unit turns you around to face forward — and it\ndoes it with a structure you already own: *am · is · are* from Stage 0, with *going to* bolted on.\n\n> يعني ده مش زمن جديد، ده تركيب على حاجة إنت عارفها.\n\n---\n\n## The Lessons — دروس الوحدة\n\n| # | Lesson | You'll be able to… |\n|---|---|---|\n| **L01** | I'm going to… | plans and intentions · future time expressions · hearing *gonna* |\n| **L02** | Asking about plans | inversion, *wh-* questions, short answers · *What about you?* |\n| **L03** | I'll… | *will* for **deciding now · offering · predicting** · *'ll* and *won't* |\n| **L04** | *going to* vs *will* | one question decides it: **had you decided before you spoke?** |\n| **L05** | My plans for next month (milestone) 🎙️ | 6–8 sentences with both forms + **record your milestone** |\n\n**Grammar spine:** *be going to* (statements, questions, negatives) · *will / 'll / won't* · choosing between\nthem.\n**Accent Lab:** linking *going to* → *gonna* (recognise it) · the contraction *'ll* · *won't* against *want*\n· the two question tunes.\n**Mindset:** goal-setting — one concrete A2 target, said out loud to a real person.\n**Arabic support:** ~35% at L01, fading to ~25% by L05.\n\n---\n\n## The Prize — جايزة الوحدة\n- Your **Unit-4 plans recording** — the first time you talk about a future you intend to make happen.\n- The **past and the future**, both available to you, four units into the stage.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **Unit overview:** 5 lessons (L05 ~35 min, the rest ~30) plus this wrapper.\n> **Teach *will* by function, not as \"the future tense\" (L03).** Learners who meet it as a tense confuse it\n> with *going to* permanently. Learners who meet it as *deciding now · offering · predicting* keep them\n> apart naturally, which is what L04 then formalises.\n> **The L04 rule that actually holds:** the difference is **when the decision happened**, not when the\n> action happens. Decided before you spoke → *going to*. Decided as you spoke → *will*. Every other framing\n> of this pair leaks somewhere.\n> **Do not chase perfection on the contrast.** Native speakers overlap the two constantly, and a wrong\n> choice is almost never misunderstood. Reward correct meaning over correct form, or you will make learners\n> hesitant about the most useful tense they have.\n> **Two pronunciation items with real consequences:** a swallowed *'ll* makes a future sound like a present,\n> and *won't* mistaken for *want* reverses the meaning.\n\n---\n\n## End-of-Unit Check — تقييم آخر الوحدة (formative)\n- **Quiz:** *be going to* forms · question inversion and short answers · the three jobs of *will* ·\n  choose *going to* or *will* and say why.\n- **Speaking task:** the L05 plans narration, scored on the rubric (A2 threshold), judging form choice by\n  meaning.\n- **Feeds:** the Cohort Tracker (weekly activity + each learner's flagged sound) and the proof timeline.\n\n---\n\n## Next Campaign → **Unit 5 — Making Comparisons**\n*bigger · better · the best* — and how to say that one thing is not like another.\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 · Stage 1 · Citizen rank · consistency beats intensity*\n","unit5-front-matter":"# 👑 Unit 5 — Making Comparisons\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · **Unit 5 of 10**\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit5-making-comparisons.md` (unit map + lessons)*\n\n---\n\n## The Campaign — حملة الوحدة\n> **Your mission:** by the end of Unit 5 you'll **compare and choose** — say what's bigger, better and\n> the best, say what falls short, and give a reasoned preference.\n> مهمتك في الوحدة دي: تقارن، وتقول رأيك، وتقول ليه.\n\n**Why this matters more than it looks:** the past and future units gave you *facts*. This unit gives you\n**opinion**. Once you can say *\"I prefer this because it's cheaper and not as far\"*, you can take part in\nreal decisions instead of only reporting them.\n\n> ودي أول وحدة بتخليك تتكلم عن رأيك، مش بس عن اللي حصل.\n\n---\n\n## The Lessons — دروس الوحدة\n\n| # | Lesson | You'll be able to… |\n|---|---|---|\n| **L01** | Comparatives (-er) | *bigger than* · the spelling rules · **than, not from** |\n| **L02** | Longer & irregular | *more expensive* · *better · worse* · the **syllable test** |\n| **L03** | Superlatives | *the biggest · the most beautiful* · **the** is obligatory |\n| **L04** | *as…as · too · enough* | equality and degree · **too** before, **enough** after |\n| **L05** | Compare two cities (milestone) 🎙️ | 6–8 sentences + a justified preference + **record it** |\n\n**Grammar spine:** comparatives · superlatives · irregulars · *(not) as…as · too · enough*.\n**Accent Lab:** /ð/ in *than* · word stress in long adjectives · the *-est* syllable · weak *as … as* ·\n*too* against *to*.\n**Mindset:** the only fair comparison is you-today against you-on-Day-1.\n**Arabic support:** ~35% at L01, fading to ~25% by L05.\n\n---\n\n## The Prize — جايزة الوحدة\n- Your **Unit-5 comparison** — the first recording where you argue a position.\n- The habit of giving a **reason**, which raises every score you will ever get.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **Unit overview:** 5 lessons (L05 ~35 min, the rest ~30) plus this wrapper.\n> **Three predictable errors, each with a cause worth naming:**\n> **1. *than* → *from*** (L01). Direct transfer from *أكبر من*. Block it actively from the first sentence;\n> a warning alone will not do it.\n> **2. The double comparative** — *more cheaper*, *more better* (L02). Over-application of the new form.\n> Teach the **syllable test** rather than a list of \"long adjectives\", so they have a rule they can apply.\n> **3. The missing *the*** before a superlative (L03). Arabic needs no article in *أحسن مطعم*, so the\n> omission is structural, not careless. Accept no superlative without *the*, every single time.\n> **The word-order split in L04 is the one thing to drill physically:** *too* **before** the adjective,\n> *enough* **after** it. Hands showing left and right beats explaining it twice.\n> **In L05, weight the reasoning above the forms.** A learner who justifies a preference well is performing\n> at A2 even with a wrong comparative inside it.\n\n---\n\n## End-of-Unit Check — تقييم آخر الوحدة (formative)\n- **Quiz:** *-er* against *more* · superlatives with *the* · *as…as* · *too* against *enough* word order.\n- **Speaking task:** the L05 comparison, scored on the rubric (A2 threshold), judging the justified\n  preference first.\n- **Feeds:** the Cohort Tracker (weekly activity + each learner's flagged sound) and the proof timeline.\n\n---\n\n## Next Campaign → **Unit 6 — Food, Quantity & Restaurants**\ncountable and uncountable, *how much · how many*, and holding a real restaurant conversation.\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 · Stage 1 · Citizen rank · consistency beats intensity*\n","unit6-front-matter":"# 👑 Unit 6 — Food, Quantity & Restaurants\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · **Unit 6 of 10**\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit6-food-quantity-restaurants.md` (unit map + lessons)*\n\n---\n\n## The Campaign — حملة الوحدة\n> **Your mission:** by the end of Unit 6 you'll **handle a restaurant on your own** — talk about food and\n> amounts, ask how much and how many, and order politely from start to finish.\n> مهمتك في الوحدة دي: تدير موقف مطعم كامل بالإنجليزي، من أول ما تقعد لحد ما تدفع.\n\n**Why this unit feels different:** the earlier units gave you language for *talking about* things. This one\ngives you language for **doing** something. At the end you will be able to walk into a café and handle it.\n\n> دي أول وحدة نتيجتها مهارة تستخدمها بره الفصل فورًا.\n\n---\n\n## The Lessons — دروس الوحدة\n\n| # | Lesson | You'll be able to… |\n|---|---|---|\n| **L01** | Countable & uncountable · *some/any* | which foods take a plural · *some* against *any* |\n| **L02** | *much · many · a lot of · a few · a little* | say how much without a number |\n| **L03** | *How much? / How many?* | ask amounts and **prices** · containers |\n| **L04** | At the restaurant | *Could I have…, please?* · polite intonation |\n| **L05** | Role-play a meal (milestone) 🎙️ | the whole transaction + **record your milestone** |\n\n**Grammar spine:** countable and uncountable nouns · *some/any* · quantifiers · *How much / How many* ·\npolite requests with *Could*.\n**Accent Lab:** food-word clusters with no inserted vowel · the reduced *of* · **thirTEEN against THIRty**,\nbecause it is money · rising intonation on requests.\n**Mindset:** real-world reps — ask a price, order a coffee, use it outside the classroom.\n**Arabic support:** ~35% at L01, fading to ~25% by L05.\n\n---\n\n## The Prize — جايزة الوحدة\n- Your **Unit-6 restaurant recording** — a complete transaction, not a description.\n- A skill you can use **this week**, outside any lesson.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **Unit overview:** 5 lessons (L05 ~35 min, the rest ~30) plus this wrapper.\n> **L01's error is structural, not careless.** Arabic pluralises many nouns English treats as uncountable, so\n> *\"some breads\"* and *\"many informations\"* are transfer. Teach the countable test as *\"can you count them\n> one by one?\"* and the container workaround, rather than as a grammatical category to memorise.\n> **In L02, give *a lot of* first.** It works with everything, so it is the safe option — and a learner who\n> always has a safe option keeps talking, which is the actual goal. Refine into *many/much* and\n> *a few/a little* afterwards.\n> **L03's teen-versus-ty stress is not a detail — it is money.** *Thirteen* and *thirty* differ only in\n> stress placement, and learners are usually unaware they are producing it ambiguously.\n> **L04 carries the most important social point in the stage:** English politeness lives in **grammar and\n> intonation**, not in extra words. *Give me a coffee* is grammatically perfect and socially rude, and\n> learners almost never realise they sound blunt. Demonstrate the same sentence rising and falling and let\n> them hear it.\n> **L05 is scored with Interaction included** — it is a two-way task, so introduce one unscripted turn\n> (\"we don't have any fish today\") and see them adapt.\n\n---\n\n## End-of-Unit Check — تقييم آخر الوحدة (formative)\n- **Quiz:** countable against uncountable · *some/any* · quantifier choice · *How much* against *How many* ·\n  make a blunt request polite.\n- **Speaking task:** the L05 role-play, scored on the rubric (A2 threshold) with **Interaction** included.\n- **Feeds:** the Cohort Tracker (weekly activity + each learner's flagged sound) and the proof timeline.\n\n---\n\n## Next Campaign → **Unit 7 — Health & Advice**\nsaying what hurts, and using *should* to tell someone what to do about it.\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 · Stage 1 · Citizen rank · consistency beats intensity*\n","unit7-front-matter":"# 👑 Unit 7 — Health & Advice\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · **Unit 7 of 10**\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit7-health-and-advice.md` (unit map + lessons)*\n\n---\n\n## The Campaign — حملة الوحدة\n> **Your mission:** by the end of Unit 7 you'll **say what's wrong and help someone else** — describe\n> symptoms, give advice, separate what's necessary from what isn't, and handle a pharmacy.\n> مهمتك في الوحدة دي: تقول إيه اللي وجعك، وتنصح غيرك، وتفرّق بين اللازم واللي مش لازم.\n\n**Why this unit is different in kind:** everything before it was about **you** — your past, your plans, your\nopinions. This unit is the first time you use English to **help another person**. That is a social skill, and\nlearners feel the difference.\n\n> ودي أول وحدة نتيجتها إنك تنفع حد تاني، مش إنك تعبّر عن نفسك.\n\n---\n\n## The Lessons — دروس الوحدة\n\n| # | Lesson | You'll be able to… |\n|---|---|---|\n| **L01** | Body & symptoms | *have* + a symptom · *feel* + an adjective · *My back hurts* |\n| **L02** | *should / shouldn't* | give advice, and **soften** it so it helps |\n| **L03** | *have to · must* | necessity — and **don't have to ≠ shouldn't** |\n| **L04** | Health habits & the doctor | assemble all three in a real pharmacy exchange |\n| **L05** | Advise a friend (milestone) 🎙️ | react, ask, advise with a reason + **record your milestone** |\n\n**Grammar spine:** *have/feel* for symptoms · *should / shouldn't* · *have to / don't have to / must*.\n**Accent Lab:** the **/θ/–/ð/** consolidation — health words are unusually thick with them — plus stress on\nthe advice verb, *have to* → /hæftə/, and the silent *l* in *should · could · would*.\n**Mindset:** never miss twice · health is the base of consistency.\n**Arabic support:** ~30% at L01, fading to ~25% by L05.\n\n---\n\n## The Prize — جايزة الوحدة\n- Your **Unit-7 advice recording** — the first time you use English to look after someone.\n- A pharmacy exchange you could genuinely handle abroad.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **Unit overview:** 5 lessons (L05 ~35 min, the rest ~30) plus this wrapper.\n> **L01's split is the grammar:** English uses **have** for a symptom you possess and **feel** for a state.\n> Blur them and you get *\"I have tired\"* and *\"I feel headache\"*. It is also the **/θ/–/ð/** consolidation\n> point, and that is not arbitrary — *throat, teeth, mouth, health, breathe* are all in this lexical field.\n> **L02's form takes two minutes; spend the rest on softeners.** Bare *should* sounds like an instruction and\n> learners cannot hear that they are being blunt. *I think* and *maybe* are the whole social lesson.\n> **L03 contains the single most valuable correction in the unit:** ***don't have to* does not mean\n> *shouldn't***. It means there is no need. A learner who confuses them turns *\"You don't have to come\"* into\n> a rejection. Give it four examples of its own.\n> **L04 is integration, not new grammar.** Correct the assembly under conversational pressure, not the parts —\n> learners routinely get each piece right in isolation and lose two of them inside a real exchange.\n> **L05 is scored with Interaction, and warmth counts.** Give them a specific problem to respond to so the\n> advice cannot be generic.\n\n---\n\n## End-of-Unit Check — تقييم آخر الوحدة (formative)\n- **Quiz:** *have* against *feel* · *should/shouldn't* form · *have to* against *don't have to* · soften a\n  blunt piece of advice.\n- **Speaking task:** the L05 advice exchange, scored on the rubric (A2 threshold) with **Interaction**\n  included.\n- **Feeds:** the Cohort Tracker (weekly activity + each learner's flagged sound) and the proof timeline.\n\n---\n\n## Next Campaign → **Unit 8 — Travel & Transport**\ntickets, timetables, and asking for directions somewhere you have never been.\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 · Stage 1 · Citizen rank · consistency beats intensity*\n","unit8-front-matter":"# 👑 Unit 8 — Travel & Transport\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · **Unit 8 of 10**\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit8-travel-and-transport.md` (unit map + lessons)*\n\n---\n\n## The Campaign — حملة الوحدة\n> **Your mission:** by the end of Unit 8 you'll **get yourself there and back** — find your route, ask a\n> stranger for help politely, buy a ticket, know what is required and what is forbidden, and then tell the\n> story of the trip.\n> مهمتك في الوحدة دي: توصل لأي مكان، وتطلب مساعدة بأدب، وتشتري تذكرة، وتحكي الرحلة بعد كده.\n\n**Why this unit feels different:** Unit 7 taught you to help someone else. This one is about **not needing\nhelp** — and, when you do need it, asking for it in a way that gets a yes. It is the most portable unit in\nStage 1: the language works in every airport on earth.\n\n> ودي أكتر وحدة هتستخدمها بره. الكلام اللي جواها بيشتغل في أي مطار وأي محطة.\n\n---\n\n## The Lessons — دروس الوحدة\n\n| # | Lesson | You'll be able to… |\n|---|---|---|\n| **L01** | Getting around | *How do I get to…?* · **take the** / **by** · *get off at* |\n| **L02** | *Can / Could I…?* | ask permission, make requests — and sound polite doing it |\n| **L03** | Booking & rules | buy a ticket · **have to / don't have to / can't** |\n| **L04** | A past trip | narrate a journey — the past tense finally carrying a real story |\n| **L05** | Tell me about a trip (milestone) 🎙️ | a 7–9 sentence narration + **record your milestone** |\n\n**Grammar spine:** *can/could* for requests, permission and past ability · *have to* and *can't* for rules ·\npast simple recycled from Units 1–3 inside a real narration.\n**Accent Lab:** linking in fixed phrases (*get off at* → *ge-TOF-fat*, *could you* → /kʊdʒə/) · rising\nintonation on requests · **/kən/ against /kænt/** · English stress in place names · narrative rhythm.\n**Mindset:** the input diet — put your maps, tickets and travel apps into English.\n**Arabic support:** ~30% at L01, fading to ~25% by L05.\n\n---\n\n## The Prize — جايزة الوحدة\n- Your **Unit-8 trip narration** — a strong candidate for your mid-A2 proof clip.\n- The genuine ability to travel without someone translating for you.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **Unit overview:** 5 lessons (L05 ~35 min, the rest ~30) plus this wrapper.\n> **L01's real error set is the two transport patterns.** *Go **by** bus* takes no article; *take **the*** bus\n> does. Crossed, they give ❌ *\"I go by the bus\"* — very common, very audible. And *on foot* breaks the *by*\n> pattern, so teach it as its own item rather than hoping it lands.\n> **L02 is Unit 7's softening lesson in a new setting,** and worth naming as such. *Should* needed *I think*;\n> requests need *could* plus a friendly rise. Intonation is the whole lesson — said flat, *Could you help me*\n> is an order, and learners cannot hear that about themselves until they hear both versions back to back.\n> **L03 introduces no new grammar and is still the hardest lesson here.** *Have to* is from Unit 7, *can't*\n> from Stage 0; the difficulty is assembly under conversational pressure. Protect the three-way meaning\n> check — **required / not required / forbidden** — because *don't have to* and *can't* look alike and mean\n> opposite things.\n> **/kən/ against /kænt/ is the priority sound of this unit, and arguably of the stage.** A weakly released\n> final /t/ inverts the meaning of the sentence. Test it with eyes-closed pair work; do not accept \"close\n> enough\", because no other pronunciation error on the list changes what you said.\n> **L04 is the spaced-review payoff and should be framed that way.** Units 1–3 built the past tense as\n> exercises; here it carries a story the learner wants to tell. Say out loud that this was impossible six\n> weeks ago — they consistently underestimate it. Watch for *didn't* + past (❌ *\"I didn't went\"*), which\n> returns under narrative load even in learners who had it clean in Unit 3.\n> **L05's difficulty is integration, not any single part.** Under a 7–9 sentence load, two of the three\n> strands slip. Correct the assembly, and ask your follow-up question unscripted.\n\n---\n\n## End-of-Unit Check — تقييم آخر الوحدة (formative)\n- **Quiz:** transport vocabulary · *Can/Could I…?* against *Could you…?* · booking language · *have to* and\n  *can't* rules · past-trip verb forms.\n- **Speaking task:** the L05 trip narration, scored on the rubric (A2 threshold).\n- **Feeds:** the Cohort Tracker (weekly activity + pronunciation notes) and flags *can/can't* plus place-name\n  vowels for the review queue.\n\n---\n\n## Next Campaign → **Unit 9 — People, Feelings & Everyday Situations**\ndescribing the people around you, saying how you actually feel, and handling the small situations that fill\nan ordinary day.\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 · Stage 1 · Citizen rank · consistency beats intensity*\n","unit9-front-matter":"# 👑 Unit 9 — People, Feelings & Everyday Situations\n\n**Empire English** · Stage 1 — **Citizen** (A2) · **Unit 9 of 10**\n*Built from: `curriculum/stage1/unit9-people-feelings-everyday.md` (unit map + lessons)*\n\n---\n\n## The Campaign — حملة الوحدة\n> **Your mission:** by the end of Unit 9 you'll **hold an everyday conversation** — describe people and\n> feelings precisely, say how things happen, contrast ordinary life with what is temporary, manage a phone\n> call, and keep small talk moving by listening and responding.\n> مهمتك في الوحدة دي: متقولش جمل وخلاص؛ اسمع، رد، اسأل، وخلّي الكلام يكمل.\n\n**Why this unit changes the game:** every earlier unit gave you language for a topic. This one teaches the\nbehaviour of conversation itself — asking back, reacting, following up, and closing naturally. That is the\nline between someone who knows English and someone who **uses** it.\n\n> هنا الإنجليزي بيبطل يبقى إجابات منفصلة، ويبدأ يبقى كلام بين شخصين.\n\n---\n\n## The Lessons — دروس الوحدة\n\n| # | Lesson | You'll be able to… |\n|---|---|---|\n| **L01** | Feelings & personality | *What's he like?* · *How is he?* · **bored / boring** |\n| **L02** | Adverbs of manner | say **how** — *carefully · well · hard* — in the right position |\n| **L03** | *Usually* vs *now* | separate a habit from now and from a temporary period |\n| **L04** | Phone & small talk | open → ask → message → close · answer **and ask back** |\n| **L05** | Two-minute chat (milestone) 🎙️ | sustain real interaction + **record your milestone** |\n\n**Grammar spine:** adverbs of manner, including *good → well* · present simple against present continuous,\nextended from right now to temporary periods · phone and small-talk functions.\n**Accent Lab:** feeling intonation · clear adjective and *-ly* endings · *-ing* /ŋ/ · natural rhythm in fixed\nphrases and reactions.\n**Mindset:** thinking in English starts by reaching for a feeling or phrase directly, before translating.\n**Arabic support:** ~25% at L01, fading to ~20% by L05.\n\n---\n\n## The Prize — جايزة الوحدة\n- Your **Unit-9 two-minute chat** — the most conversational proof clip in Stage 1.\n- The ability to keep a real exchange alive instead of waiting for the next question.\n\n> [!TEACHER]\n> **Unit overview:** 5 lessons (L05 ~35 min, the rest ~30) plus this wrapper.\n> **L01 has two high-value contrasts.** *What's he like?* asks personality; *How is he?* asks current state.\n> And **-ed** describes the feeling while **-ing** describes its cause. The sentence *\"I'm boring\"* is the\n> classic embarrassing A2 error — handle it directly, not delicately.\n> **L02's durable correction is *good → well*.** ❌ *\"I speak English good\"* is always understood, so it can\n> survive for years without correction. Also make *hardly* explicit: it means almost not at all and is not\n> the adverb of *hard*.\n> **L03's new content is the temporary continuous.** Stage 0 already taught *I'm eating now*. The extension\n> is *This week I'm working from home*. Anchor tense choice to visible time markers; it is more usable under\n> pressure than an abstract tense explanation.\n> **L04 is chunk-first.** Phone calls and small talk use stable phrases. Teach their social shape and fluent\n> delivery, not their grammar. The key small-talk behaviour is **answer + ask back**.\n> **L05 is an interaction assessment.** Perfect sentences without reaction are parallel monologues. Give\n> each partner one unpredictable detail, allow retrieval time, and score asking back, reacting, and following\n> up as seriously as tense accuracy.\n\n---\n\n## End-of-Unit Check — تقييم آخر الوحدة (formative)\n- **Quiz:** feeling against personality · *-ed/-ing* · adjective to adverb, including *good/well* · simple\n  against continuous · choosing an appropriate phone or small-talk phrase.\n- **Speaking task:** the L05 two-minute chat, scored on the A2 rubric with **Interaction** foregrounded.\n- **Feeds:** the Cohort Tracker and flags *-ing*, *-ly*, and feeling/small-talk intonation for Unit 10.\n\n---\n\n## Next Campaign → **Unit 10 — Putting It Together**\nconsolidation across the whole stage, real-world missions, and the final A2 milestone recording.\n\n---\n\n*Empire English 👑 · Stage 1 · Citizen rank · consistency beats intensity*\n"};
